CC.provideCourse.bind(null,"db")({"lessons":{"db-01":{"html":"<p>Every app you use — a university portal, a bank, an online shop — needs to store facts and find them again later. Before databases existed, programs saved data in plain text or binary files. That caused big problems as data grew.</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-data-vs-information\"><h2 id=\"data-vs-information\">Data vs information</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Data (<em>veri</em>):</strong> Raw, unprocessed facts with no context on their own. Example: <code>85</code>, <code>Deniz</code>, <code>CMP153</code>.</li><li><strong>Information (<em>bilgi</em>):</strong> Data that has been organized, given context, or processed so it means something to a person. Example: <em>\"Deniz Aksoy scored 85 in CMP153 (Programming Basics I), which is above the class average of 78.\"</em></li></ul>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Raw data</th><th>Organized information</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>2026-09-14</code>, <code>3.80</code>, <code>Ece</code></td><td>Ece joined in 2026 and has a 3.80 GPA (Honour list)</td></tr><tr><td><code>37</code>, <code>Istanbul</code>, <code>24</code></td><td>14 of the 24 students live in Istanbul</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-problems-with-file-based-storage\"><h2 id=\"problems-with-file-based-storage\">Problems with file-based storage</h2>\n<p>In a <strong>file system</strong>, each department or program keeps its own separate files (for example, <code>registrations.csv</code> for the registrar and <code>grades.csv</code> for the department). Five problems appear quickly:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Data redundancy (<em>veri tekrarı</em>):</strong> The same student's name and address are stored in 3 or 4 different files, wasting disk space.</li><li><strong>Data inconsistency (<em>veri tutarsızlığı</em>):</strong> When a student changes their email in one file, the other files still hold the old email.</li><li><strong>Isolation:</strong> Data is scattered in different formats, so combining student records with course grades takes custom code every time.</li><li><strong>No concurrent control:</strong> If two staff members edit the same file at the same moment, one person's changes overwrite the other's.</li><li><strong>Weak security:</strong> It is hard to let a clerk see a student's name without also letting them see salaries or grades in the same file.</li></ol>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-what-a-database-is\"><h2 id=\"what-a-database-is\">What a database is</h2>\n<p>A <strong>database</strong> (<em>veritabanı</em>) is an organized, shared collection of related data stored electronically. Instead of every program inventing its own file format, one central system manages the data, enforces rules (like \"every enrollment must belong to a real student\"), and lets many users query it safely at once.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Look at our live sample university database below. Press <strong>Run</strong> to see how one query pulls clean information from the <code>Students</code> table.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"sqlrun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">SELECT first_name, last_name, city, gpa\nFROM Students\nWHERE city = 'Istanbul'\nORDER BY gpa DESC\nLIMIT 5;</pre></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Using <em>data</em> and <em>information</em> as exact synonyms in a written exam — always contrast raw facts vs processed, meaningful output.</li><li>Thinking a spreadsheet file on your desktop is a full database system — a single file lacks multi-user concurrency, strict relational constraints and fine-grained security.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>data</strong></td><td>raw, unprocessed facts such as numbers, dates or names</td><td lang=\"tr\">veri</td></tr><tr><td><strong>information</strong></td><td>processed, structured data with meaning and context</td><td lang=\"tr\">bilgi</td></tr><tr><td><strong>data redundancy</strong></td><td>storing the same fact unnecessarily in multiple places</td><td lang=\"tr\">veri tekrarı</td></tr><tr><td><strong>data inconsistency</strong></td><td>conflicting copies of the same fact in different files</td><td lang=\"tr\">veri tutarsızlığı</td></tr><tr><td><strong>database</strong></td><td>an organized, shared collection of related data</td><td lang=\"tr\">veritabanı</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"Which of the following is an example of <strong>information</strong> rather than raw <strong>data</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>3.45</code>","why":"A bare number with no label or context is raw data."},{"h":"<code>'Istanbul'</code>","why":"A single city name on its own is raw data."},{"h":"\"The average GPA of Computer Programming students is 3.21.\"","ok":1,"why":"It summarizes and gives meaning to raw records."},{"h":"<code>2026</code>","why":"A standalone year has no context."}],"why":"Data becomes information when it is processed, labelled or summarized to answer a question."},{"q":"A student updates their phone number in the library file, but the registrar's file still has the old number. Which file-system problem is this?","opts":[{"h":"Data encryption","why":"Encryption protects data by scrambling it."},{"h":"Data inconsistency","ok":1,"why":"Duplicated data in separate files got out of sync."},{"h":"Normalization","why":"Normalization is a database design technique that prevents this problem."},{"h":"Atomicity","why":"Atomicity is a transaction property."}],"why":"Redundancy (storing the same fact in multiple files) leads directly to inconsistency when one copy is updated."},{"q":"Which is a major advantage of a database over separate flat files?","opts":[{"h":"It never needs backups","why":"Databases still require regular backups."},{"h":"Centralized control reduces redundancy and enforces rules across all users","ok":1,"why":"One system manages sharing, security and consistency."},{"h":"It stores everything as a single unformatted text line","why":"Databases store structured tables, rows and columns."},{"h":"Only one user can read it per day","why":"Databases support many concurrent users."}],"why":"A database centralizes storage so multiple applications share consistent, rule-checked data."}]],"id":"db-01","title":"Data, information, file systems and databases","goal":"explain the difference between data and information and why databases replaced flat file systems","week":"1","minutes":15,"terms":[{"t":"data","m":"raw, unprocessed facts such as numbers, dates or names","tr":"veri"},{"t":"information","m":"processed, structured data with meaning and context","tr":"bilgi"},{"t":"data redundancy","m":"storing the same fact unnecessarily in multiple places","tr":"veri tekrarı"},{"t":"data inconsistency","m":"conflicting copies of the same fact in different files","tr":"veri tutarsızlığı"},{"t":"database","m":"an organized, shared collection of related data","tr":"veritabanı"}]},"db-02":{"html":"<p>A database is the collection of data itself. The software that creates, protects and queries that database is called a <strong>DBMS (Database Management System)</strong>. Examples include <strong>Microsoft SQL Server</strong>, <strong>PostgreSQL</strong>, <strong>MySQL</strong>, <strong>Oracle</strong> and <strong>SQLite</strong> (which powers the playground on this site).</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-what-a-dbms-does\"><h2 id=\"what-a-dbms-does\">What a DBMS does</h2>\n<p>A DBMS sits between users/applications and the stored files on disk. It handles:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Defining structure:</strong> creating tables, columns and data types.</li><li><strong>Querying and updating:</strong> running SQL commands to read or change rows.</li><li><strong>Integrity and constraints:</strong> rejecting invalid data (such as a negative credit count or a grade of 150).</li><li><strong>Security:</strong> controlling who can read or edit which tables.</li><li><strong>Concurrency and recovery:</strong> letting many users work at once without corrupting data, and recovering after a crash.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-data-models-at-a-glance\"><h2 id=\"data-models-at-a-glance\">Data models at a glance</h2>\n<p>A <strong>data model</strong> describes how data is structured and connected:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Shape</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Hierarchical</strong></td><td>Tree (parent → children)</td><td>Fast for 1:N, rigid (each child has only 1 parent). Used in early mainframes.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Network</strong></td><td>Graph (children can have multiple parents)</td><td>Flexible, but complex to navigate with pointers.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Relational</strong></td><td><strong>Tables</strong> (rows and columns) linked by keys</td><td>The standard model taught in this course (SQL Server, PostgreSQL, SQLite).</td></tr><tr><td><strong>NoSQL</strong></td><td>Documents (JSON), key–value, wide-column or graph</td><td>Built for massive scale or flexible schemas (e.g. MongoDB, Redis).</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-three-level-ansi-sparc-architecture\"><h2 id=\"three-level-ansi-sparc-architecture\">Three-level (ANSI-SPARC) architecture</h2>\n<p>To separate how users see data from how bytes are stored on disk, a DBMS uses three levels:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>External level (View level):</strong> What each user or app is allowed to see (for example, a student sees only their own grades, while an advisor sees advisees' GPAs).</li><li><strong>Conceptual level (Logical level):</strong> The whole database design — all tables, columns, data types and relationships.</li><li><strong>Internal level (Physical level):</strong> How the data is actually stored on disk (files, pages, indexes).</li></ol>\n<p>This separation gives <strong>data independence</strong>: you can move files to a faster disk (internal) without breaking SQL queries (conceptual) or user screens (external).</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-client-server-architecture\"><h2 id=\"client-server-architecture\">Client–server architecture</h2>\n<p>In most real systems (including <strong>SQL Server</strong>), the <strong>DBMS runs on a server</strong> machine. Your application, SSMS (SQL Server Management Studio) or a web backend acts as a <strong>client</strong> that sends SQL requests over the network and receives result tables back. (SQLite is an exception: it is an <em>embedded</em> library that runs inside the app itself.)</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Run this query to inspect the tables stored in our relational database (<code>sqlite_master</code> is SQLite's system catalog).</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"sqlrun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">SELECT name, type\nFROM sqlite_master\nWHERE type = 'table' AND name NOT LIKE 'sqlite_%'\nORDER BY name;</pre></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Confusing <strong>database</strong> (the data) with <strong>DBMS</strong> (the software like SQL Server or MySQL).</li><li>Mixing up the <strong>external</strong> level (individual user views) with the <strong>conceptual</strong> level (the full logical schema of all tables).</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>DBMS</strong></td><td>Database Management System — software that manages databases</td><td lang=\"tr\">Veritabanı Yönetim Sistemi (VTYS)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>relational model</strong></td><td>data model storing records in tables linked by keys</td><td lang=\"tr\">ilişkisel model</td></tr><tr><td><strong>conceptual level</strong></td><td>the complete logical structure of tables and relationships</td><td lang=\"tr\">kavramsal düzey</td></tr><tr><td><strong>data independence</strong></td><td>ability to change storage or schema without breaking apps</td><td lang=\"tr\">veri bağımsızlığı</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"Which of the following is a <strong>DBMS</strong> rather than a data model?","opts":[{"h":"Relational","why":"Relational is a data model."},{"h":"Hierarchical","why":"Hierarchical is a data model."},{"h":"Microsoft SQL Server","ok":1,"why":"SQL Server is DBMS software that implements the relational model."},{"h":"NoSQL","why":"NoSQL is a family of non-relational data models."}],"why":"A DBMS is the software product (SQL Server, PostgreSQL, SQLite); a data model is the structural concept."},{"q":"Which level of the three-level architecture defines how bytes and indexes are physically stored on disk?","opts":[{"h":"External level","why":"External level defines tailored user views."},{"h":"Conceptual level","why":"Conceptual level defines all tables and relationships logically."},{"h":"Internal level","ok":1,"why":"Internal (physical) level handles files, storage blocks and indexes."},{"h":"Application level","why":"Not one of the three ANSI-SPARC levels."}],"why":"The three ANSI-SPARC levels are external (views), conceptual (logical schema) and internal (physical storage)."},{"q":"In the relational model, how is data organized?","opts":[{"h":"As a strict single-parent tree","why":"That is the hierarchical model."},{"h":"As tables made of rows (records) and columns (attributes)","ok":1,"why":"Tables are linked by primary and foreign keys."},{"h":"As unlinked plain text files","why":"Flat files lack relational structure."},{"h":"Only as JSON documents","why":"JSON documents belong to document-based NoSQL databases."}],"why":"Relational databases store data in two-dimensional tables (relations) linked by keys."}]],"id":"db-02","title":"DBMS, data models and architectures","goal":"explain what a DBMS does, compare the main data models and describe the three-level and client–server architectures","week":"2","minutes":15,"terms":[{"t":"DBMS","m":"Database Management System — software that manages databases","tr":"Veritabanı Yönetim Sistemi (VTYS)"},{"t":"relational model","m":"data model storing records in tables linked by keys","tr":"ilişkisel model"},{"t":"conceptual level","m":"the complete logical structure of tables and relationships","tr":"kavramsal düzey"},{"t":"data independence","m":"ability to change storage or schema without breaking apps","tr":"veri bağımsızlığı"}]},"db-03":{"html":"<p>Before you write a single <code>CREATE TABLE</code>, you design the database on paper. Database design moves through three stages:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Conceptual design:</strong> Identify the real-world things (<strong>entities</strong>) and how they relate, independent of any software. This produces an <strong>ER diagram</strong>.</li><li><strong>Logical design:</strong> Turn the ER diagram into tables, columns, primary keys and foreign keys (and normalize them).</li><li><strong>Physical design:</strong> Choose exact data types (e.g. <code>INT</code>, <code>VARCHAR(50)</code>), indexes and storage in a specific DBMS like SQL Server.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"sec sec-entities-attributes-and-keys\"><h2 id=\"entities-attributes-and-keys\">Entities, attributes and keys</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Entity (<em>varlık</em>):</strong> A thing we store data about, such as <code>Students</code>, <code>Courses</code>, or <code>Departments</code>. An entity becomes a <strong>table</strong>.</li><li><strong>Attribute (<em>nitelik</em>):</strong> A property of an entity, such as <code>first_name</code>, <code>city</code>, or <code>credits</code>. Attributes become <strong>columns</strong>.</li><li><strong>Primary Key (PK, <em>birincil anahtar</em>):</strong> A column (or group of columns) that <strong>uniquely identifies</strong> every row in a table, e.g. <code>student_id</code>. Never NULL, never duplicated.</li><li><strong>Foreign Key (FK, <em>yabancı anahtar</em>):</strong> A column in one table that points to the Primary Key of another table to create a link, e.g. <code>Students.dept_id</code> → <code>Departments.dept_id</code>.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-cardinality-1-1-1-n-and-m-n\"><h2 id=\"cardinality-1-1-1-n-and-m-n\">Cardinality: 1:1, 1:N and M:N</h2>\n<p><strong>Cardinality</strong> (<em>kardinalite</em>) states how many rows of one entity can link to how many rows of another:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>1:1 (One-to-One):</strong> One person has one passport; one passport belongs to one person.</li><li><strong>1:N (One-to-Many):</strong> One department has many students, while each student belongs to one department. Put the FK (<code>dept_id</code>) on the <strong>N (many)</strong> side (<code>Students</code>).</li><li><strong>M:N (Many-to-Many):</strong> One student takes many courses, AND one course has many students. <strong>Relational databases cannot store M:N directly between two tables!</strong> You resolve an M:N relationship by adding a <strong>junction table</strong> (bridge table) in the middle — here, <code>Enrollments</code> — with FKs to both tables.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-worked-example-our-university-er-diagram\"><h2 id=\"worked-example-our-university-er-diagram\">Worked example: our university ER diagram</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"erd\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">Departments: *dept_id, dept_name, building\nInstructors: *instructor_id, first_name, last_name, dept_id (FK)\nStudents: *student_id, first_name, last_name, city, dept_id (FK)\nCourses: *course_id, course_code, title, credits, dept_id (FK), instructor_id (FK)\nEnrollments: *enrollment_id, student_id (FK), course_id (FK), semester, grade\n\nDepartments 1--N Students : belongs to\nDepartments 1--N Courses : offers\nStudents 1--N Enrollments : registers\nCourses 1--N Enrollments : has\nInstructors 1--N Courses : teaches</pre></div>\n<p>Notice how <code>Students</code> and <code>Courses</code> have an <strong>M:N</strong> relationship, resolved by <code>Enrollments</code> (1:N from <code>Students</code> and 1:N from <code>Courses</code>).</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Read each short scenario, write down the entities, attributes, PKs, FKs and relationships on paper, then open the model answer to check.</p>\n<h3>Scenario 1: Library</h3>\n<p>A library has books (with ISBN, title, year) and members (member ID, name, phone). A member can borrow many books over time, and a book copy can be borrowed by many members over time; each loan records the borrow date and return date.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show model ERD (Library)</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><ul><li><strong>Books</strong> (<code>*book_id</code>, <code>isbn</code>, <code>title</code>, <code>pub_year</code>)</li><li><strong>Members</strong> (<code>*member_id</code>, <code>full_name</code>, <code>phone</code>)</li><li><strong>Loans</strong> (<code>*loan_id</code>, <code>member_id</code> FK → Members, <code>book_id</code> FK → Books, <code>borrow_date</code>, <code>return_date</code>)</li><li>Relationships: <code>Members</code> <strong>1:N</strong> <code>Loans</code>, <code>Books</code> <strong>1:N</strong> <code>Loans</code> (resolving the <strong>M:N</strong> between Members and Books).</li></ul></div></details>\n<h3>Scenario 2: Hospital</h3>\n<p>A hospital has departments (e.g. Cardiology) and doctors. Each doctor works in one department. Patients book appointments with doctors; each appointment has a date, time and notes.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show model ERD (Hospital)</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><ul><li><strong>Departments</strong> (<code>*dept_id</code>, <code>dept_name</code>)</li><li><strong>Doctors</strong> (<code>*doctor_id</code>, <code>full_name</code>, <code>specialty</code>, <code>dept_id</code> FK → Departments)</li><li><strong>Patients</strong> (<code>*patient_id</code>, <code>full_name</code>, <code>birth_date</code>, <code>phone</code>)</li><li><strong>Appointments</strong> (<code>*appt_id</code>, <code>patient_id</code> FK → Patients, <code>doctor_id</code> FK → Doctors, <code>appt_datetime</code>, <code>notes</code>)</li><li>Relationships: <code>Departments</code> <strong>1:N</strong> <code>Doctors</code>; <code>Patients</code> <strong>M:N</strong> <code>Doctors</code> resolved via <code>Appointments</code>.</li></ul></div></details>\n<h3>Scenario 3: Online shop</h3>\n<p>Customers place orders. Each order belongs to one customer and has an order date. An order can contain many products, and a product can appear in many orders, with a quantity and unit price for each line.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show model ERD (Online shop)</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><ul><li><strong>Customers</strong> (<code>*customer_id</code>, <code>full_name</code>, <code>email</code>, <code>city</code>)</li><li><strong>Orders</strong> (<code>*order_id</code>, <code>customer_id</code> FK → Customers, <code>order_date</code>)</li><li><strong>Products</strong> (<code>*product_id</code>, <code>name</code>, <code>stock_qty</code>, <code>current_price</code>)</li><li><strong>OrderItems</strong> (<code>*order_item_id</code>, <code>order_id</code> FK → Orders, <code>product_id</code> FK → Products, <code>quantity</code>, <code>unit_price</code>)</li><li>Relationships: <code>Customers</code> <strong>1:N</strong> <code>Orders</code>; <code>Orders</code> <strong>M:N</strong> <code>Products</code> resolved via <code>OrderItems</code>.</li></ul></div></details>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Trying to put <code>course_id_1, course_id_2, course_id_3</code> columns inside <code>Students</code> instead of creating a junction table (<code>Enrollments</code>) for an M:N relationship.</li><li>Putting the Foreign Key on the <strong>1</strong> side instead of the <strong>N (many)</strong> side of a 1:N relationship.</li><li>Using a person's name or email as a Primary Key when names can repeat or emails can change — a surrogate integer ID (<code>student_id</code>) is safer.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>entity</strong></td><td>a real-world object or concept represented as a table</td><td lang=\"tr\">varlık</td></tr><tr><td><strong>attribute</strong></td><td>a property or column of an entity</td><td lang=\"tr\">nitelik</td></tr><tr><td><strong>primary key</strong></td><td>a column (or set) that uniquely identifies each row</td><td lang=\"tr\">birincil anahtar</td></tr><tr><td><strong>foreign key</strong></td><td>a column referencing the primary key of another table</td><td lang=\"tr\">yabancı anahtar</td></tr><tr><td><strong>junction table</strong></td><td>a bridge table that resolves an M:N relationship into two 1:N links</td><td lang=\"tr\">ara tablo (bağlantı tablosu)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"One <code>Instructor</code> teaches many <code>Courses</code>, and each <code>Course</code> is taught by one <code>Instructor</code>. Where should the Foreign Key go?","opts":[{"h":"<code>instructor_id</code> in <code>Courses</code> and <code>course_id</code> in <code>Instructors</code>","why":"Only one side needs the FK in a 1:N relationship."},{"h":"<code>instructor_id</code> inside the <code>Courses</code> table","ok":1,"why":"The FK always goes on the \"many\" (N) side."},{"h":"<code>course_id</code> inside the <code>Instructors</code> table","why":"That would limit an instructor to a single course."},{"h":"A new junction table is required","why":"Junction tables are for M:N, not 1:N."}],"why":"In a 1:N relationship, place the Foreign Key on the \"many\" (N) side (<code>Courses</code>)."},{"q":"How do you represent a Many-to-Many (M:N) relationship between <code>Students</code> and <code>Courses</code> in relational tables?","opts":[{"h":"Add <code>course_id</code> to <code>Students</code>","why":"One column can hold only one course ID per student."},{"h":"Add <code>student_id</code> to <code>Courses</code>","why":"One column can hold only one student ID per course."},{"h":"Create a junction table (e.g. <code>Enrollments</code>) with foreign keys to both <code>Students</code> and <code>Courses</code>","ok":1,"why":"This splits M:N into two 1:N relationships."},{"h":"Merge <code>Students</code> and <code>Courses</code> into one big table","why":"That causes massive data redundancy."}],"why":"M:N relationships are always resolved with a junction table containing FKs to both entities."},{"q":"Which stage of database design creates the ER diagram, independent of any specific DBMS software?","opts":[{"h":"Conceptual design","ok":1,"why":"Conceptual design models entities and relationships before picking a DBMS."},{"h":"Physical design","why":"Physical design chooses DBMS-specific types, indexes and storage."},{"h":"Backup design","why":"Backup is an operational task, not a modeling stage."},{"h":"Query tuning","why":"Query tuning happens after tables exist."}],"why":"The three modeling stages are Conceptual (ER) → Logical (tables/normalization) → Physical (DBMS types/indexes)."}]],"id":"db-03","title":"Data modeling and ER diagrams","goal":"read and design Entity-Relationship (ER) diagrams with entities, attributes, keys and cardinalities (1:1, 1:N, M:N)","week":"3","minutes":25,"terms":[{"t":"entity","m":"a real-world object or concept represented as a table","tr":"varlık"},{"t":"attribute","m":"a property or column of an entity","tr":"nitelik"},{"t":"primary key","m":"a column (or set) that uniquely identifies each row","tr":"birincil anahtar"},{"t":"foreign key","m":"a column referencing the primary key of another table","tr":"yabancı anahtar"},{"t":"junction table","m":"a bridge table that resolves an M:N relationship into two 1:N links","tr":"ara tablo (bağlantı tablosu)"}]},"db-04":{"html":"<p>Suppose someone puts all student, course and department facts into one giant spreadsheet-like table. Why is that a bad idea? Because of <strong>anomalies</strong> (<em>anormallikler</em>) — bugs that happen when you add, change or delete data.</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-the-three-anomalies\"><h2 id=\"the-three-anomalies\">The three anomalies</h2>\n<p>Look at this unnormalized <code>CourseSheet</code> table:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>student_id</th><th>student_name</th><th>dept_name</th><th>building</th><th>courses</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Deniz Aksoy</td><td>Computer Prog.</td><td>Block A</td><td>CMP153, CMP151</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Ece Yildiz</td><td>Computer Prog.</td><td>Block A</td><td>CMP153, MTH153</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol><li><strong>Insert anomaly (<em>ekleme anormalliği</em>):</strong> You cannot add a new department (<code>Graphic Design</code> in <code>Block C</code>) until at least one student enrolls in it.</li><li><strong>Update anomaly (<em>güncelleme anormalliği</em>):</strong> If <code>Computer Prog.</code> moves from <code>Block A</code> to <code>Block E</code>, you must update <em>every</em> row for every student in that department. Missing one row creates inconsistent data.</li><li><strong>Delete anomaly (<em>silme anormalliği</em>):</strong> If <code>Ece Yildiz</code> drops out and you delete her row, you might accidentally lose the last record that <code>MTH153</code> existed!</li></ol>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-functional-dependency\"><h2 id=\"functional-dependency\">Functional dependency</h2>\n<p>A column <code>B</code> is <strong>functionally dependent</strong> on <code>A</code> (written <code>A → B</code>) if knowing <code>A</code> tells you <em>exactly one</em> value of <code>B</code>.</p>\n<ul><li><code>student_id → student_name</code> (knowing the student's ID gives one name).</li><li><code>dept_id → dept_name, building</code> (knowing the department ID gives its name and building).</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-first-normal-form-1nf\"><h2 id=\"first-normal-form-1nf\">First Normal Form (1NF)</h2>\n<p>A table is in <strong>1NF</strong> when:</p>\n<ol><li>Every cell holds <strong>one atomic (single) value</strong> — no comma-separated lists like <code>CMP153, CMP151</code>, and no repeating columns like <code>course1, course2</code>.</li><li>Each row is unique (there is a <strong>Primary Key</strong>).</li></ol>\n<p>To fix <code>CourseSheet</code> into <strong>1NF</strong>, we split the multi-valued <code>courses</code> column into separate rows and make <code>(student_id, course_code)</code> the composite primary key:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>&lt;u&gt;student_id&lt;/u&gt;</th><th>student_name</th><th>dept_name</th><th>building</th><th>&lt;u&gt;course_code&lt;/u&gt;</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Deniz Aksoy</td><td>Computer Prog.</td><td>Block A</td><td>CMP153</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Deniz Aksoy</td><td>Computer Prog.</td><td>Block A</td><td>CMP151</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Ece Yildiz</td><td>Computer Prog.</td><td>Block A</td><td>CMP153</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Ece Yildiz</td><td>Computer Prog.</td><td>Block A</td><td>MTH153</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>1NF fixes the comma-separated list, but <code>student_name</code>, <code>dept_name</code> and <code>building</code> still repeat! We fix that in the next lesson with <strong>2NF</strong> and <strong>3NF</strong>.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Look at this unnormalized table of student phone numbers:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>student_id</th><th>name</th><th>phones</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>10</td><td>Defne</td><td>0532-111, 0555-222</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Arda</td><td>0544-333</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>How would you put it into <strong>1NF</strong>? Think for a minute, then check the answer.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show 1NF solution</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><p>Give each phone number its own row (or move phones to a <code>StudentPhones(student_id, phone)</code> table with composite PK <code>(student_id, phone)</code>):</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>&lt;u&gt;student_id&lt;/u&gt;</th><th>name</th><th>&lt;u&gt;phone&lt;/u&gt;</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>10</td><td>Defne</td><td>0532-111</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Defne</td><td>0555-222</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Arda</td><td>0544-333</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></details>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Leaving two values in one cell separated by a comma (<code>\"CMP153, CMP151\"</code>) and calling the table 1NF.</li><li>Adding columns <code>phone1, phone2, phone3</code> instead of rows — repeating groups also violate 1NF.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>normalization</strong></td><td>organizing tables to reduce redundancy and prevent anomalies</td><td lang=\"tr\">normalizasyon</td></tr><tr><td><strong>anomaly</strong></td><td>an unwanted side effect when inserting, updating or deleting data</td><td lang=\"tr\">anormallik</td></tr><tr><td><strong>functional dependency</strong></td><td><code>A → B</code>: each value of <code>A</code> determines a single value of <code>B</code></td><td lang=\"tr\">işlevsel bağımlılık</td></tr><tr><td><strong>First Normal Form (1NF)</strong></td><td>every cell has one atomic value and every row has a primary key</td><td lang=\"tr\">Birinci Normal Form</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"Which rule MUST a table satisfy to be in <strong>First Normal Form (1NF)</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"No foreign keys are allowed","why":"Relational tables in 1NF, 2NF and 3NF use foreign keys freely."},{"h":"Every cell contains a single atomic value (no lists or repeating groups) and rows are uniquely identified","ok":1,"why":"Atomicity and a primary key define 1NF."},{"h":"Every table must have at most 3 columns","why":"There is no column-count limit."},{"h":"All numbers must be integers","why":"Any data type is allowed."}],"why":"1NF requires atomic (single) values per cell, no repeating groups, and a primary key."},{"q":"You cannot record a new <code>Course</code> in the database until at least one student enrolls in it. Which anomaly is this?","opts":[{"h":"Insert anomaly","ok":1,"why":"You are blocked from inserting valid data because unrelated data is missing."},{"h":"Update anomaly","why":"Update anomalies happen when editing repeated values."},{"h":"Delete anomaly","why":"Delete anomalies happen when removing a row destroys unrelated facts."},{"h":"Redundancy","why":"Redundancy is duplicate storage, not the inability to insert."}],"why":"An insert anomaly occurs when you cannot add one fact without also having another unrelated fact."},{"q":"What does the notation <code>student_id → city</code> mean?","opts":[{"h":"<code>city</code> uniquely identifies <code>student_id</code>","why":"Many students live in the same city, so <code>city</code> does not determine <code>student_id</code>."},{"h":"Knowing <code>student_id</code> determines a single <code>city</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>city</code> is functionally dependent on <code>student_id</code>."},{"h":"<code>student_id</code> is greater than <code>city</code>","why":"<code>→</code> means functional dependency, not comparison."},{"h":"<code>student_id</code> is a foreign key to <code>city</code>","why":"<code>→</code> denotes functional dependency."}],"why":"<code>A → B</code> means each value of <code>A</code> maps to at most one value of <code>B</code>."}]],"id":"db-04","title":"Normalization (Part 1): Anomalies and 1NF","goal":"spot insert, update and delete anomalies, identify functional dependencies and convert an unnormalized table to 1NF","week":"4","minutes":15,"terms":[{"t":"normalization","m":"organizing tables to reduce redundancy and prevent anomalies","tr":"normalizasyon"},{"t":"anomaly","m":"an unwanted side effect when inserting, updating or deleting data","tr":"anormallik"},{"t":"functional dependency","m":"`A → B`: each value of `A` determines a single value of `B`","tr":"işlevsel bağımlılık"},{"t":"First Normal Form (1NF)","m":"every cell has one atomic value and every row has a primary key","tr":"Birinci Normal Form"}]},"db-05":{"html":"<p>In Part 1 we reached <strong>1NF</strong> by putting one value per cell. Now we take one messy <code>EnrollmentsReport</code> table all the way from <strong>UNF → 1NF → 2NF → 3NF</strong> using the interactive stepper below.</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-second-normal-form-2nf\"><h2 id=\"second-normal-form-2nf\">Second Normal Form (2NF)</h2>\n<p>A table is in <strong>2NF</strong> when:</p>\n<ol><li>It is already in <strong>1NF</strong>, AND</li><li>There are <strong>no partial dependencies</strong>: every non-key column must depend on the <strong>whole</strong> primary key, not just <em>part</em> of a composite key. - <em>(Shortcut: if a table's primary key is a single column, it is automatically in 2NF!)</em></li></ol>\n<p>In our 1NF table with composite PK <code>(student_id, course_code)</code>:</p>\n<ul><li><code>grade</code> depends on BOTH <code>student_id</code> and <code>course_code</code> (good!).</li><li><code>student_name</code>, <code>dept_id</code>, <code>dept_name</code>, <code>building</code> depend <strong>only on <code>student_id</code></strong> (partial dependency!).</li><li><code>course_title</code> depends <strong>only on <code>course_code</code></strong> (partial dependency!).</li></ul>\n<p>So we split them out into <code>Students</code>, <code>Courses</code>, and <code>Enrollments</code>.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-third-normal-form-3nf\"><h2 id=\"third-normal-form-3nf\">Third Normal Form (3NF)</h2>\n<p>A table is in <strong>3NF</strong> when:</p>\n<ol><li>It is already in <strong>2NF</strong>, AND</li><li>There are <strong>no transitive dependencies</strong>: no non-key column may depend on <em>another non-key column</em>. - In plain words: <em>\"Every non-key column must depend on the key, the whole key, and nothing but the key.\"</em></li></ol>\n<p>In <code>Students(student_id, student_name, dept_id, dept_name, building)</code>:</p>\n<ul><li><code>student_id → dept_id → dept_name, building</code>.</li><li><code>dept_name</code> and <code>building</code> depend on <code>dept_id</code> (a non-key column!), not directly on <code>student_id</code>. That is a <strong>transitive dependency</strong>.</li><li>Move <code>dept_id, dept_name, building</code> into a <code>Departments</code> table, leaving only <code>dept_id</code> as a Foreign Key in <code>Students</code>.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-worked-example-interactive-stepper-unf-3nf\"><h2 id=\"worked-example-interactive-stepper-unf-3nf\">Worked example: interactive stepper (UNF → 3NF)</h2>\n<p>Step through all four stages below to watch one messy table become four clean 3NF tables.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"stepper\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">{\n  \"title\": \"Normalizing a university grade sheet (UNF → 3NF)\",\n  \"stages\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"UNF (Unnormalized)\",\n      \"note\": \"Everything is crammed into one table, with comma-separated courses and grades inside single cells.\",\n      \"tables\": [\n        {\n          \"name\": \"GradeSheet\",\n          \"pk\": [\"student_id\"],\n          \"cols\": [\"student_id\", \"student_name\", \"dept_id\", \"dept_name\", \"building\", \"courses_and_grades\"],\n          \"rows\": [\n            [\"1\", \"Deniz Aksoy\", \"D1\", \"Computer Prog.\", \"Block A\", \"CMP153 (85), CMP151 (90)\"],\n            [\"2\", \"Ece Yildiz\", \"D1\", \"Computer Prog.\", \"Block A\", \"CMP153 (95)\"]\n          ]\n        }\n      ],\n      \"problem\": \"Multi-valued cells ('CMP153 (85), CMP151 (90)') violate 1NF and make querying or updating individual grades painful.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"1NF\",\n      \"note\": \"Split multi-valued cells into one row per enrollment. Primary key is now composite: (student_id, course_code).\",\n      \"tables\": [\n        {\n          \"name\": \"GradeSheet_1NF\",\n          \"pk\": [\"student_id\", \"course_code\"],\n          \"cols\": [\"student_id\", \"student_name\", \"dept_id\", \"dept_name\", \"building\", \"course_code\", \"course_title\", \"grade\"],\n          \"rows\": [\n            [\"1\", \"Deniz Aksoy\", \"D1\", \"Computer Prog.\", \"Block A\", \"CMP153\", \"Prog. Basics I\", \"85\"],\n            [\"1\", \"Deniz Aksoy\", \"D1\", \"Computer Prog.\", \"Block A\", \"CMP151\", \"DBMS I\", \"90\"],\n            [\"2\", \"Ece Yildiz\", \"D1\", \"Computer Prog.\", \"Block A\", \"CMP153\", \"Prog. Basics I\", \"95\"]\n          ]\n        }\n      ],\n      \"problem\": \"Partial dependencies! student_name and dept info depend only on student_id; course_title depends only on course_code — not on the whole key (student_id, course_code).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"2NF\",\n      \"note\": \"Move columns that depend on only part of the composite key into their own tables: Students, Courses and Enrollments.\",\n      \"tables\": [\n        {\n          \"name\": \"Students\",\n          \"pk\": [\"student_id\"],\n          \"cols\": [\"student_id\", \"student_name\", \"dept_id\", \"dept_name\", \"building\"],\n          \"rows\": [\n            [\"1\", \"Deniz Aksoy\", \"D1\", \"Computer Prog.\", \"Block A\"],\n            [\"2\", \"Ece Yildiz\", \"D1\", \"Computer Prog.\", \"Block A\"]\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"name\": \"Courses\",\n          \"pk\": [\"course_code\"],\n          \"cols\": [\"course_code\", \"course_title\"],\n          \"rows\": [\n            [\"CMP153\", \"Prog. Basics I\"],\n            [\"CMP151\", \"DBMS I\"]\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"name\": \"Enrollments\",\n          \"pk\": [\"student_id\", \"course_code\"],\n          \"cols\": [\"student_id\", \"course_code\", \"grade\"],\n          \"rows\": [\n            [\"1\", \"CMP153\", \"85\"],\n            [\"1\", \"CMP151\", \"90\"],\n            [\"2\", \"CMP153\", \"95\"]\n          ]\n        }\n      ],\n      \"problem\": \"Transitive dependency in Students! dept_name and building depend on dept_id (a non-key column), not directly on student_id.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"3NF (Clean!)\",\n      \"note\": \"Move dept_name and building into Departments, keeping dept_id as a Foreign Key in Students.\",\n      \"tables\": [\n        {\n          \"name\": \"Departments\",\n          \"pk\": [\"dept_id\"],\n          \"cols\": [\"dept_id\", \"dept_name\", \"building\"],\n          \"rows\": [\n            [\"D1\", \"Computer Prog.\", \"Block A\"]\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"name\": \"Students\",\n          \"pk\": [\"student_id\"],\n          \"cols\": [\"student_id\", \"student_name\", \"dept_id\"],\n          \"rows\": [\n            [\"1\", \"Deniz Aksoy\", \"D1\"],\n            [\"2\", \"Ece Yildiz\", \"D1\"]\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"name\": \"Courses\",\n          \"pk\": [\"course_code\"],\n          \"cols\": [\"course_code\", \"course_title\"],\n          \"rows\": [\n            [\"CMP153\", \"Prog. Basics I\"],\n            [\"CMP151\", \"DBMS I\"]\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"name\": \"Enrollments\",\n          \"pk\": [\"student_id\", \"course_code\"],\n          \"cols\": [\"student_id\", \"course_code\", \"grade\"],\n          \"rows\": [\n            [\"1\", \"CMP153\", \"85\"],\n            [\"1\", \"CMP151\", \"90\"],\n            [\"2\", \"CMP153\", \"95\"]\n          ]\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}</pre></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Normalize this <code>Orders</code> table (currently in 1NF) to <strong>3NF</strong>: <code>OrderItems(&lt;u&gt;order_id&lt;/u&gt;, &lt;u&gt;product_id&lt;/u&gt;, order_date, customer_id, customer_city, product_name, qty)</code></p>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show 2NF and 3NF solution</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><ul><li><strong>Partial dependencies (fix for 2NF):</strong> <code>order_date, customer_id, customer_city</code> depend only on <code>order_id</code>; <code>product_name</code> depends only on <code>product_id</code>. - <code>Orders(&lt;u&gt;order_id&lt;/u&gt;, order_date, customer_id, customer_city)</code> - <code>Products(&lt;u&gt;product_id&lt;/u&gt;, product_name)</code> - <code>OrderItems(&lt;u&gt;order_id&lt;/u&gt;, &lt;u&gt;product_id&lt;/u&gt;, qty)</code></li><li><strong>Transitive dependency (fix for 3NF):</strong> In <code>Orders</code>, <code>customer_city</code> depends on <code>customer_id</code>, not directly on <code>order_id</code>. - <code>Customers(&lt;u&gt;customer_id&lt;/u&gt;, customer_city)</code> - <code>Orders(&lt;u&gt;order_id&lt;/u&gt;, order_date, customer_id)</code></li></ul></div></details>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Thinking a table with a single-column Primary Key can fail 2NF — partial dependency only happens when the Primary Key has 2 or more columns.</li><li>Stopping at 2NF when <code>zip_code → city</code> or <code>dept_id → dept_name</code> is still sitting inside a table (that is a transitive dependency and violates 3NF).</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>partial dependency</strong></td><td>a non-key column depending on only part of a composite PK</td><td lang=\"tr\">kısmi bağımlılık</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Second Normal Form (2NF)</strong></td><td>in 1NF with no partial dependencies</td><td lang=\"tr\">İkinci Normal Form</td></tr><tr><td><strong>transitive dependency</strong></td><td>a non-key column depending on another non-key column</td><td lang=\"tr\">geçişli bağımlılık</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Third Normal Form (3NF)</strong></td><td>in 2NF with no transitive dependencies</td><td lang=\"tr\">Üçüncü Normal Form</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"A table <code>Invoices(&lt;u&gt;invoice_id&lt;/u&gt;, customer_id, customer_name, total)</code> has a single-column primary key <code>invoice_id</code>. Which normal form does it violate?","opts":[{"h":"1NF","why":"Every cell is atomic and <code>invoice_id</code> is the PK."},{"h":"2NF","why":"A single-column PK cannot have partial dependencies."},{"h":"3NF","ok":1,"why":"<code>customer_name</code> depends on <code>customer_id</code> (a non-key column), which is a transitive dependency."},{"h":"None — it is already in 3NF","why":"<code>invoice_id → customer_id → customer_name</code> violates 3NF."}],"why":"Move <code>customer_id, customer_name</code> into a <code>Customers</code> table to reach 3NF."},{"q":"What does <strong>2NF</strong> forbid?","opts":[{"h":"Foreign keys","why":"Foreign keys are required to link normalized tables."},{"h":"Partial dependencies on part of a composite primary key","ok":1,"why":"Every non-key column must depend on the whole primary key."},{"h":"Having more than one table","why":"Normalization splits tables intentionally."},{"h":"NULL values","why":"2NF is about key dependencies, not NULLs."}],"why":"2NF eliminates partial dependencies (columns depending on only part of a composite PK)."},{"q":"In the 3NF slogan <em>\"the key, the whole key, and nothing but the key\"</em>, what does <em>\"nothing but the key\"</em> refer to?","opts":[{"h":"1NF","why":"1NF is about atomic values."},{"h":"2NF","why":"2NF is <em>\"the whole key\"</em> (no partial dependencies)."},{"h":"3NF","ok":1,"why":"3NF removes transitive dependencies so non-key columns depend <em>only</em> on the key."},{"h":"SQL <code>ORDER BY</code>","why":"<code>ORDER BY</code> is for sorting query output."}],"why":"1NF = atomic + key; 2NF = whole key; 3NF = nothing but the key."}]],"id":"db-05","title":"Normalization (Part 2): 2NF and 3NF","goal":"eliminate partial dependencies (2NF) and transitive dependencies (3NF) to produce clean tables","week":"4","minutes":20,"terms":[{"t":"partial dependency","m":"a non-key column depending on only part of a composite PK","tr":"kısmi bağımlılık"},{"t":"Second Normal Form (2NF)","m":"in 1NF with no partial dependencies","tr":"İkinci Normal Form"},{"t":"transitive dependency","m":"a non-key column depending on another non-key column","tr":"geçişli bağımlılık"},{"t":"Third Normal Form (3NF)","m":"in 2NF with no transitive dependencies","tr":"Üçüncü Normal Form"}]},"db-06":{"html":"<p>From week 5 onward, the course moves from paper diagrams to real SQL. On campus, the university typically uses <strong>Microsoft SQL Server Express</strong> (the free database engine) together with <strong>SSMS (SQL Server Management Studio)</strong> (the graphical tool where you write queries). On this site, you can run SQL directly in the browser without installing anything.</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-setting-up-sql-server-express-ssms-for-home-practice\"><h2 id=\"setting-up-sql-server-express-ssms-for-home-practice\">Setting up SQL Server Express + SSMS (for home practice)</h2>\n<ol><li>Download <strong>SQL Server Express</strong> (free edition) from Microsoft and run the <em>Basic</em> installer.</li><li>Download and install <strong>SSMS</strong> (SQL Server Management Studio).</li><li>Open SSMS, connect to <code>localhost\\SQLEXPRESS</code> using <em>Windows Authentication</em>, then right-click <strong>Databases › New Database…</strong> (or click <strong>New Query</strong> and run <code>CREATE DATABASE UniversityDB;</code>).</li></ol>\n<div class=\"callout callout-tsql\"><div class=\"callout-h\">Creating and selecting a database</div><p>In SQL Server, you create a database and switch to it with:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\"><code>CREATE DATABASE UniversityDB;\nGO\nUSE UniversityDB;</code></pre>\n<p>SQLite (the browser playground here) stores one database per file, so <code>CREATE DATABASE</code> and <code>USE</code> are not needed in SQLite.</p></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-sql-data-types\"><h2 id=\"common-sql-data-types\">Common SQL data types</h2>\n<p>Every column in a table must have a <strong>data type</strong> so the database knows what values are allowed and how to sort or calculate them.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Value kind</th><th>SQL Server (T-SQL)</th><th>SQLite (here)</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Whole numbers</td><td><code>INT</code>, <code>SMALLINT</code></td><td><code>INTEGER</code></td><td><code>credits = 5</code>, <code>enrollment_year = 2026</code></td></tr><tr><td>Exact decimals (money, GPA)</td><td><code>DECIMAL(4,2)</code></td><td><code>REAL</code></td><td><code>gpa = 3.45</code>, <code>salary = 62000.00</code></td></tr><tr><td>Short text (ASCII)</td><td><code>VARCHAR(50)</code></td><td><code>TEXT</code></td><td><code>'CMP151'</code></td></tr><tr><td>Unicode text (Turkish chars <code>ğ, ş, ı, ö, ü, ç</code>)</td><td><code>NVARCHAR(50)</code></td><td><code>TEXT</code></td><td><code>'Ayşe Yılmaz'</code></td></tr><tr><td>Date</td><td><code>DATE</code></td><td><code>TEXT</code> (<code>'YYYY-MM-DD'</code>)</td><td><code>'2026-09-14'</code></td></tr><tr><td>True / False</td><td><code>BIT</code> (<code>0</code> or <code>1</code>)</td><td><code>INTEGER</code> (<code>0</code> or <code>1</code>)</td><td><code>is_active = 1</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-tip\"><div class=\"callout-h\">Why NVARCHAR matters in Turkey</div><p>In SQL Server, <code>VARCHAR</code> can corrupt Turkish letters like <code>ş, ğ, ı, İ</code> depending on collation. Always use <code>NVARCHAR</code> for names, cities and titles in Turkish or multilingual apps.</p></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Run this query to inspect the column names and types of the <code>Students</code> table in our SQLite database.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"sqlrun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">PRAGMA table_info(Students);</pre></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Using <code>INT</code> for a GPA like <code>3.85</code> — <code>INT</code> chops off the decimal part and stores <code>3</code>.</li><li>Using <code>FLOAT</code> for money instead of <code>DECIMAL(10,2)</code> — floating-point numbers can produce tiny rounding errors like <code>19.9900000001</code>.</li><li>Writing <code>VARCHAR(5)</code> for a city name like <code>'Istanbul'</code> (8 letters) — it gets rejected or truncated.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>SSMS</strong></td><td>SQL Server Management Studio — the desktop IDE for SQL Server</td><td lang=\"tr\">SSMS</td></tr><tr><td><strong>data type</strong></td><td>the kind of values a column can store (integer, decimal, text, date)</td><td lang=\"tr\">veri tipi</td></tr><tr><td><strong>NVARCHAR</strong></td><td>SQL Server Unicode variable-length text type supporting Turkish characters</td><td lang=\"tr\">NVARCHAR</td></tr><tr><td><strong>DECIMAL(p,s)</strong></td><td>exact fixed-point number with <code>p</code> total digits and <code>s</code> decimal places</td><td lang=\"tr\">DECIMAL</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"Which SQL Server data type is best for storing Turkish student names like <code>Çağla Şahin</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>INT</code>","why":"<code>INT</code> only stores whole numbers."},{"h":"<code>VARCHAR(50)</code>","why":"Non-Unicode <code>VARCHAR</code> can lose Turkish characters depending on collation."},{"h":"<code>NVARCHAR(50)</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>NVARCHAR</code> stores Unicode text safely."},{"h":"<code>BIT</code>","why":"<code>BIT</code> only stores <code>0</code> or <code>1</code>."}],"why":"Use <code>NVARCHAR</code> in SQL Server whenever text may contain Turkish or other Unicode characters."},{"q":"What does <code>DECIMAL(5, 2)</code> allow you to store?","opts":[{"h":"Up to 5 digits <em>after</em> the decimal point","why":"The second number (2) is the digits after the decimal point."},{"h":"Up to 5 digits total, with 2 digits after the decimal point (e.g. <code>123.45</code>)","ok":1,"why":"Precision = 5, scale = 2."},{"h":"Only whole numbers up to 52","why":"<code>DECIMAL</code> stores fractional numbers."},{"h":"5 text characters","why":"<code>DECIMAL</code> is numeric, not text."}],"why":"In <code>DECIMAL(p, s)</code>, <code>p</code> is total digits and <code>s</code> is digits after the decimal point."},{"q":"Which SQLite/SQL statement creates a new database in SQL Server?","opts":[{"h":"<code>NEW DATABASE UniversityDB;</code>","why":"Not valid SQL."},{"h":"<code>CREATE DATABASE UniversityDB;</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>CREATE DATABASE</code> is the standard DDL statement in SQL Server."},{"h":"<code>INSERT DATABASE UniversityDB;</code>","why":"<code>INSERT</code> adds rows to a table."},{"h":"<code>SELECT DATABASE UniversityDB;</code>","why":"<code>SELECT</code> reads data."}],"why":"Use <code>CREATE DATABASE &lt;name&gt;;</code> followed by <code>USE &lt;name&gt;;</code> in SQL Server."}]],"id":"db-06","title":"Installing tools, creating a database and data types","goal":"know how to set up SQL Server Express + SSMS, create a database and choose the right SQL data types","week":"5","minutes":15,"terms":[{"t":"SSMS","m":"SQL Server Management Studio — the desktop IDE for SQL Server","tr":"SSMS"},{"t":"data type","m":"the kind of values a column can store (integer, decimal, text, date)","tr":"veri tipi"},{"t":"NVARCHAR","m":"SQL Server Unicode variable-length text type supporting Turkish characters","tr":"NVARCHAR"},{"t":"DECIMAL(p,s)","m":"exact fixed-point number with `p` total digits and `s` decimal places","tr":"DECIMAL"}]},"db-07":{"html":"<p>A good database does not trust applications to send clean data — it enforces rules itself using <strong>constraints</strong> (<em>kısıtlamalar</em>).</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-the-6-core-constraints\"><h2 id=\"the-6-core-constraints\">The 6 core constraints</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Constraint</th><th>What it guarantees</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>PRIMARY KEY</code></td><td>Uniquely identifies each row (unique + not null)</td><td><code>student_id INT PRIMARY KEY</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>FOREIGN KEY</code></td><td>Value must exist in the referenced parent table</td><td><code>FOREIGN KEY (dept_id) REFERENCES Departments(dept_id)</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>NOT NULL</code></td><td>Column cannot be left empty (<code>NULL</code>)</td><td><code>first_name NVARCHAR(40) NOT NULL</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>UNIQUE</code></td><td>No two rows can have the same non-null value</td><td><code>email NVARCHAR(80) UNIQUE</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>DEFAULT</code></td><td>Automatic value if none is provided on <code>INSERT</code></td><td><code>semester VARCHAR(20) DEFAULT '2026-Fall'</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>CHECK</code></td><td>Every row must satisfy a boolean condition</td><td><code>CHECK (grade &gt;= 0 AND grade &lt;= 100)</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-tsql\"><div class=\"callout-h\">Auto-incrementing Primary Keys</div><ul><li>In <strong>SQLite:</strong> <code>student_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT</code></li><li>In <strong>SQL Server (T-SQL):</strong> <code>student_id INT PRIMARY KEY IDENTITY(1,1)</code> (starts at 1, increases by 1 for each new row).</li></ul></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-entering-data-gui-vs-insert-into\"><h2 id=\"entering-data-gui-vs-insert-into\">Entering data: GUI vs <code>INSERT INTO</code></h2>\n<p>In SSMS you can right-click a table › <strong>Edit Top 200 Rows</strong> to type rows into a grid. In real work and exams, you write <code>INSERT INTO</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"sqlrun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">INSERT INTO Departments (dept_name, building)\nVALUES ('Artificial Intelligence', 'Block E');\n\nSELECT * FROM Departments;</pre></div>\n<p>You can also insert several rows in one statement:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\"><code>INSERT INTO Departments (dept_name, building) VALUES\n('Mechatronics', 'Block F'),\n('Data Science', 'Block E');</code></pre>\n<p>And if a constraint is violated, the database blocks the bad row:</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"sqlrun\" data-fails=\"1\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">-- This fails because grade 150 violates CHECK (grade &gt;= 0 AND grade &lt;= 100):\nINSERT INTO Enrollments (student_id, course_id, grade)\nVALUES (1, 10, 150);</pre></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Practice inserting a valid row and see how <code>DEFAULT</code> works, then try the challenge below.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"challenges\" data-lesson=\"db-07\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Putting a value in the <code>VALUES (...)</code> list for an <code>IDENTITY</code> / <code>AUTOINCREMENT</code> primary key column — let the database generate it automatically.</li><li>Inserting a child row (like a <code>Student</code> with <code>dept_id = 99</code>) before <code>dept_id = 99</code> exists in <code>Departments</code> — the <code>FOREIGN KEY</code> constraint will reject it.</li><li>Writing <code>NULL</code> inside quotes (<code>'NULL'</code>) — <code>'NULL'</code> is the 4-letter word \"NULL\", not an empty SQL <code>NULL</code>.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>constraint</strong></td><td>a rule enforced by the database on column values</td><td lang=\"tr\">kısıtlama</td></tr><tr><td><strong>NOT NULL</strong></td><td>forbids missing (<code>NULL</code>) values in a column</td><td lang=\"tr\">boş geçilemez</td></tr><tr><td><strong>CHECK</strong></td><td>enforces a custom condition on column values</td><td lang=\"tr\">kontrol kısıtlaması</td></tr><tr><td><strong>IDENTITY / AUTOINCREMENT</strong></td><td>automatically generates sequential primary key numbers</td><td lang=\"tr\">otomatik artan</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"Which constraint stops two students from registering the exact same <code>email</code> address?","opts":[{"h":"<code>NOT NULL</code>","why":"<code>NOT NULL</code> only prevents empty values, not duplicates."},{"h":"<code>CHECK</code>","why":"<code>CHECK</code> tests a condition on a single row's values, not across rows."},{"h":"<code>UNIQUE</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>UNIQUE</code> ensures no two rows share the same value."},{"h":"<code>DEFAULT</code>","why":"<code>DEFAULT</code> supplies a fallback value when none is given."}],"why":"Use <code>UNIQUE</code> to forbid duplicate values in a non-primary-key column like <code>email</code>."},{"q":"What happens if you run <code>INSERT INTO Enrollments (student_id, course_id, grade) VALUES (1, 10, -5);</code> when <code>CHECK (grade &gt;= 0 AND grade &lt;= 100)</code> is on <code>grade</code>?","opts":[{"h":"The database silently changes <code>-5</code> to <code>0</code>","why":"Databases reject invalid data rather than guessing a fix."},{"h":"The <code>INSERT</code> fails with a CHECK constraint error and no row is added","ok":1,"why":"Constraints protect data integrity."},{"h":"The row is inserted and flagged as a warning","why":"The statement is aborted."},{"h":"<code>-5</code> is stored as <code>NULL</code>","why":"It is rejected, not converted."}],"why":"A <code>CHECK</code> constraint rejects any <code>INSERT</code> or <code>UPDATE</code> that makes the condition false."},{"q":"In SQL Server (T-SQL), which keyword automatically numbers a primary key <code>1, 2, 3, ...</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>AUTOINCREMENT</code>","why":"<code>AUTOINCREMENT</code> is SQLite syntax."},{"h":"<code>IDENTITY(1,1)</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>IDENTITY(seed, increment)</code> is SQL Server's auto-numbering keyword."},{"h":"<code>AUTO_INCREMENT</code>","why":"<code>AUTO_INCREMENT</code> is MySQL syntax."},{"h":"<code>SERIAL</code>","why":"<code>SERIAL</code> is PostgreSQL syntax."}],"why":"SQL Server uses <code>IDENTITY(1,1)</code>; SQLite uses <code>AUTOINCREMENT</code>."}]],"id":"db-07","title":"Constraints and entering data","goal":"protect table data using PRIMARY KEY, FOREIGN KEY, NOT NULL, UNIQUE, DEFAULT and CHECK, and insert rows with INSERT","week":"6","minutes":20,"terms":[{"t":"constraint","m":"a rule enforced by the database on column values","tr":"kısıtlama"},{"t":"NOT NULL","m":"forbids missing (`NULL`) values in a column","tr":"boş geçilemez"},{"t":"CHECK","m":"enforces a custom condition on column values","tr":"kontrol kısıtlaması"},{"t":"IDENTITY / AUTOINCREMENT","m":"automatically generates sequential primary key numbers","tr":"otomatik artan"}]},"db-08":{"html":"<p>SQL commands are grouped by job. <strong>DDL (Data Definition Language)</strong> builds and changes the <em>structure</em> of the database (tables, columns, constraints) — not the rows inside.</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-the-four-ddl-commands\"><h2 id=\"the-four-ddl-commands\">The four DDL commands</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Effect on table</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>CREATE TABLE</code></td><td>Builds a new table</td><td>Creates structure + constraints</td></tr><tr><td><code>ALTER TABLE</code></td><td>Adds, changes or drops columns/constraints</td><td>Keeps existing rows</td></tr><tr><td><code>DROP TABLE</code></td><td>Deletes the entire table and its data</td><td>Structure + rows gone</td></tr><tr><td><code>TRUNCATE TABLE</code></td><td>Deletes <strong>all rows</strong> fast, keeps the empty table</td><td>Structure stays, rows gone</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-create-table-and-alter-table\"><h2 id=\"create-table-and-alter-table\"><code>CREATE TABLE</code> and <code>ALTER TABLE</code></h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"sqlrun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">CREATE TABLE Clubs (\n    club_id   INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,\n    club_name TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE,\n    room      TEXT DEFAULT 'Student Center'\n);\n\nALTER TABLE Clubs ADD COLUMN budget REAL DEFAULT 0;\n\nINSERT INTO Clubs (club_name) VALUES ('Coding Club');\nSELECT * FROM Clubs;</pre></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-tsql\"><div class=\"callout-h\">ALTER TABLE and TRUNCATE in SQL Server</div><ul><li>Adding a column in SQL Server omits the word <code>COLUMN</code>: <code>ALTER TABLE Clubs ADD budget DECIMAL(10,2) DEFAULT 0;</code></li><li>Dropping a column: <code>ALTER TABLE Clubs DROP COLUMN budget;</code></li><li><code>TRUNCATE TABLE Clubs;</code> works in SQL Server (resets <code>IDENTITY</code> counter too!), whereas SQLite does not have <code>TRUNCATE</code> (use <code>DELETE FROM Clubs;</code> in SQLite).</li></ul></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-delete-vs-truncate-vs-drop-classic-exam-question\"><h2 id=\"delete-vs-truncate-vs-drop-classic-exam-question\"><code>DELETE</code> vs <code>TRUNCATE</code> vs <code>DROP</code> (classic exam question!)</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th><code>DELETE FROM T</code></th><th><code>TRUNCATE TABLE T</code></th><th><code>DROP TABLE T</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Language group</td><td>DML</td><td>DDL</td><td>DDL</td></tr><tr><td>Can use <code>WHERE</code>?</td><td><strong>Yes</strong></td><td>No (all rows)</td><td>No (whole table)</td></tr><tr><td>Keeps table structure?</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td><strong>No</strong> (table is gone)</td></tr><tr><td>Resets <code>IDENTITY</code>?</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>N/A</td></tr><tr><td>Rollback in transaction?</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes in SQL Server (logged minimally)</td><td>Yes in SQL Server DDL</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Solve the DDL challenges below.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"challenges\" data-lesson=\"db-08\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Running <code>DROP TABLE</code> when you only wanted to empty the rows (<code>TRUNCATE TABLE</code> or <code>DELETE FROM</code>).</li><li>Trying to <code>DROP TABLE Departments</code> while <code>Students</code> still has a <code>FOREIGN KEY</code> pointing to it — you must drop or alter the child table first.</li><li>Writing <code>ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN</code> in SQL Server (SQL Server uses <code>ADD col type</code> without <code>COLUMN</code>).</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>DDL</strong></td><td>Data Definition Language (<code>CREATE</code>, <code>ALTER</code>, <code>DROP</code>, <code>TRUNCATE</code>)</td><td lang=\"tr\">Veri Tanımlama Dili</td></tr><tr><td><strong>CREATE TABLE</strong></td><td>defines a new table, its columns and constraints</td><td lang=\"tr\">tablo oluşturma</td></tr><tr><td><strong>ALTER TABLE</strong></td><td>modifies an existing table's columns or constraints</td><td lang=\"tr\">tablo değiştirme</td></tr><tr><td><strong>DROP TABLE</strong></td><td>permanently removes a table and all its rows</td><td lang=\"tr\">tablo silme</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"Which command removes <strong>all rows</strong> from <code>Logs</code> while keeping the table structure and resetting <code>IDENTITY</code> in SQL Server?","opts":[{"h":"<code>DELETE FROM Logs;</code>","why":"<code>DELETE</code> does not reset the <code>IDENTITY</code> counter."},{"h":"<code>DROP TABLE Logs;</code>","why":"<code>DROP TABLE</code> removes the table structure itself."},{"h":"<code>TRUNCATE TABLE Logs;</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>TRUNCATE</code> empties the table, keeps its columns, and resets <code>IDENTITY</code>."},{"h":"<code>ALTER TABLE Logs EMPTY;</code>","why":"<code>EMPTY</code> is not valid SQL."}],"why":"<code>TRUNCATE TABLE</code> is a DDL command that removes all rows and resets <code>IDENTITY</code>, keeping the table definition."},{"q":"Which command adds a <code>phone</code> column to an existing <code>Students</code> table?","opts":[{"h":"<code>UPDATE Students ADD phone TEXT;</code>","why":"<code>UPDATE</code> modifies row values, not table columns."},{"h":"<code>ALTER TABLE Students ADD COLUMN phone TEXT;</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>ALTER TABLE ... ADD</code> modifies the table structure."},{"h":"<code>INSERT INTO Students COLUMN phone;</code>","why":"<code>INSERT</code> adds rows, not columns."},{"h":"<code>CREATE COLUMN phone ON Students;</code>","why":"Not valid SQL."}],"why":"Use <code>ALTER TABLE</code> to add, modify or drop columns."},{"q":"Which statement is <strong>DML</strong>, not <strong>DDL</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>CREATE TABLE</code>","why":"DDL."},{"h":"<code>ALTER TABLE</code>","why":"DDL."},{"h":"<code>DROP TABLE</code>","why":"DDL."},{"h":"<code>DELETE FROM</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>DELETE</code> manipulates rows (DML), whereas <code>CREATE</code>/<code>ALTER</code>/<code>DROP</code>/<code>TRUNCATE</code> change structure (DDL)."}],"why":"Remember: DDL = structure (<code>CREATE</code>, <code>ALTER</code>, <code>DROP</code>, <code>TRUNCATE</code>); DML = data (<code>SELECT</code>, <code>INSERT</code>, <code>UPDATE</code>, <code>DELETE</code>)."}]],"id":"db-08","title":"DDL queries: CREATE, ALTER, DROP and TRUNCATE","goal":"create, modify and remove database tables and columns using DDL statements","week":"8–9","minutes":20,"terms":[{"t":"DDL","m":"Data Definition Language (`CREATE`, `ALTER`, `DROP`, `TRUNCATE`)","tr":"Veri Tanımlama Dili"},{"t":"CREATE TABLE","m":"defines a new table, its columns and constraints","tr":"tablo oluşturma"},{"t":"ALTER TABLE","m":"modifies an existing table's columns or constraints","tr":"tablo değiştirme"},{"t":"DROP TABLE","m":"permanently removes a table and all its rows","tr":"tablo silme"}]},"db-09":{"html":"<p>Most of your day-to-day SQL work is <strong>DML (Data Manipulation Language)</strong> — especially <code>SELECT</code>, which reads data without changing it.</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-clause-order-in-select\"><h2 id=\"clause-order-in-select\">Clause order in <code>SELECT</code></h2>\n<p>You write a <code>SELECT</code> query in this fixed order:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><code>SELECT columns\nFROM table\nWHERE condition\nORDER BY column [ASC | DESC]\nLIMIT n;          -- (in SQL Server: SELECT TOP n ...)</code></pre>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"sqlrun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">SELECT DISTINCT city\nFROM Students\nORDER BY city ASC;</pre></div>\n<p><code>DISTINCT</code> removes duplicate rows from the output.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-filtering-with-where\"><h2 id=\"filtering-with-where\">Filtering with <code>WHERE</code></h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operator</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>=</code>, <code>&lt;&gt;</code> (or <code>!=</code>), <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;=</code>, <code>&gt;=</code></td><td>Standard comparisons</td><td><code>WHERE gpa &gt;= 3.5</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>AND</code>, <code>OR</code>, <code>NOT</code></td><td>Combine conditions (<code>AND</code> binds tighter than <code>OR</code>)</td><td><code>WHERE city = 'Istanbul' AND gpa &gt;= 3.0</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>BETWEEN a AND b</code></td><td>Inclusive range (<code>&gt;= a AND &lt;= b</code>)</td><td><code>WHERE enrollment_year BETWEEN 2025 AND 2026</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>IN (v1, v2, ...)</code></td><td>Matches any value in the list</td><td><code>WHERE city IN ('Ankara', 'Izmir')</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>LIKE 'pattern'</code></td><td>Pattern match: <code>%</code> = any chars, <code>_</code> = 1 char</td><td><code>WHERE last_name LIKE 'A%'</code> (starts with A)</td></tr><tr><td><code>IS NULL</code> / <code>IS NOT NULL</code></td><td>Tests for missing (<code>NULL</code>) values</td><td><code>WHERE email IS NULL</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-warn\"><div class=\"callout-h\">Never write <code>= NULL</code>!</div><p><code>NULL</code> means <em>\"unknown\"</em>. Comparing <code>email = NULL</code> is always <strong>unknown</strong> (false in <code>WHERE</code>), so it returns 0 rows! Always write <code>email IS NULL</code> or <code>email IS NOT NULL</code>.</p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-tsql\"><div class=\"callout-h\">TOP vs LIMIT</div><p>SQLite uses <code>LIMIT 5</code> at the end. <strong>SQL Server (T-SQL)</strong> puts <code>TOP 5</code> right after <code>SELECT</code>: <code>SELECT TOP 5 first_name, gpa FROM Students ORDER BY gpa DESC;</code></p></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Practice writing <code>SELECT</code> queries against the sample university database — every challenge checks your result automatically.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"challenges\" data-lesson=\"db-09\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Writing <code>WHERE email = NULL</code> instead of <code>WHERE email IS NULL</code>.</li><li>Combining <code>AND</code> and <code>OR</code> without parentheses: <code>WHERE city = 'Istanbul' OR city = 'Ankara' AND gpa &gt; 3.5</code> runs <code>AND</code> first! Use parentheses: <code>WHERE (city = 'Istanbul' OR city = 'Ankara') AND gpa &gt; 3.5</code>.</li><li>Using <code>=</code> instead of <code>LIKE</code> with <code>%</code> (<code>WHERE first_name = 'A%'</code> looks for the literal <code>%</code> sign).</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>DML</strong></td><td>Data Manipulation Language (<code>SELECT</code>, <code>INSERT</code>, <code>UPDATE</code>, <code>DELETE</code>)</td><td lang=\"tr\">Veri İşleme Dili</td></tr><tr><td><strong>DISTINCT</strong></td><td>removes duplicate rows from a <code>SELECT</code> result</td><td lang=\"tr\">benzersiz (DISTINCT)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>LIKE</strong></td><td>matches text patterns using <code>%</code> (zero or more chars) and <code>_</code> (one char)</td><td lang=\"tr\">desen eşleştirme</td></tr><tr><td><strong>NULL</strong></td><td>represents a missing or unknown value (test with <code>IS NULL</code>)</td><td lang=\"tr\">boş/bilinmeyen değer</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"Which <code>WHERE</code> clause finds students whose <code>gpa</code> is missing?","opts":[{"h":"<code>WHERE gpa = NULL</code>","why":"<code>= NULL</code> never matches anything in SQL."},{"h":"<code>WHERE gpa = ''</code>","why":"<code>''</code> is an empty text string, not <code>NULL</code>."},{"h":"<code>WHERE gpa IS NULL</code>","ok":1,"why":"Always use <code>IS NULL</code> or <code>IS NOT NULL</code> to test for <code>NULL</code>."},{"h":"<code>WHERE gpa == 0</code>","why":"<code>0</code> is a real number, not a missing value, and SQL uses <code>=</code>."}],"why":"<code>NULL</code> is unknown, so <code>= NULL</code> never returns true — always use <code>IS NULL</code>."},{"q":"What does <code>WHERE first_name LIKE '_a%'</code> match?","opts":[{"h":"Names starting with <code>a</code>","why":"<code>_</code> requires one character <em>before</em> <code>a</code>."},{"h":"Names whose <strong>second</strong> letter is <code>a</code> (e.g. <code>Kaan</code>, <code>Hanna</code>, <code>Tarik</code>)","ok":1,"why":"<code>_</code> matches 1 character, then <code>a</code>, then <code>%</code> matches the rest."},{"h":"Names ending with <code>a</code>","why":"<code>%a</code> matches names ending with <code>a</code>."},{"h":"Names containing <code>a</code> anywhere","why":"<code>%a%</code> matches <code>a</code> anywhere."}],"why":"<code>_</code> matches exactly one character; <code>%</code> matches zero or more characters."},{"q":"How do you return only the top 3 highest-paid instructors in <strong>SQL Server (T-SQL)</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>SELECT first_name, salary FROM Instructors ORDER BY salary DESC LIMIT 3;</code>","why":"<code>LIMIT</code> is SQLite/MySQL/PostgreSQL, not SQL Server."},{"h":"<code>SELECT TOP 3 first_name, salary FROM Instructors ORDER BY salary DESC;</code>","ok":1,"why":"SQL Server uses <code>TOP n</code> after <code>SELECT</code>."},{"h":"<code>SELECT FIRST 3 first_name, salary FROM Instructors;</code>","why":"<code>FIRST</code> is not SQL Server syntax."},{"h":"<code>SELECT first_name, salary FROM Instructors TOP 3;</code>","why":"<code>TOP 3</code> goes right after <code>SELECT</code>."}],"why":"In SQL Server, use <code>SELECT TOP 3 ... ORDER BY ...</code>."}]],"id":"db-09","title":"DML queries: SELECT, filtering and sorting","goal":"retrieve, filter and sort rows using SELECT, WHERE, DISTINCT, LIKE, IN, BETWEEN, IS NULL and ORDER BY","week":"10","minutes":25,"terms":[{"t":"DML","m":"Data Manipulation Language (`SELECT`, `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`)","tr":"Veri İşleme Dili"},{"t":"DISTINCT","m":"removes duplicate rows from a `SELECT` result","tr":"benzersiz (DISTINCT)"},{"t":"LIKE","m":"matches text patterns using `%` (zero or more chars) and `_` (one char)","tr":"desen eşleştirme"},{"t":"NULL","m":"represents a missing or unknown value (test with `IS NULL`)","tr":"boş/bilinmeyen değer"}]},"db-10":{"html":"<p>Instead of listing individual rows, you often want summaries: <em>How many students live in each city? What is the average grade per course?</em> That is what <strong>aggregate functions</strong> and <code>GROUP BY</code> do.</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-the-5-aggregate-functions\"><h2 id=\"the-5-aggregate-functions\">The 5 aggregate functions</h2>\n<ul><li><code>COUNT(*)</code>: counts all rows (including rows with <code>NULL</code>s).</li><li><code>COUNT(col)</code>: counts non-<code>NULL</code> values in <code>col</code>.</li><li><code>SUM(col)</code>, <code>AVG(col)</code>, <code>MIN(col)</code>, <code>MAX(col)</code>: ignore <code>NULL</code> values automatically.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"sqlrun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">SELECT\n    COUNT(*) AS total_students,\n    COUNT(gpa) AS students_with_gpa,\n    ROUND(AVG(gpa), 2) AS avg_gpa,\n    MAX(gpa) AS highest_gpa\nFROM Students;</pre></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-grouping-with-group-by-and-filtering-groups-with-having\"><h2 id=\"grouping-with-group-by-and-filtering-groups-with-having\">Grouping with <code>GROUP BY</code> and filtering groups with <code>HAVING</code></h2>\n<p><code>GROUP BY</code> splits rows into buckets (e.g. one bucket per <code>city</code>), then runs the aggregate function on each bucket.</p>\n<ul><li><strong><code>WHERE</code></strong> filters <strong>rows BEFORE</strong> grouping.</li><li><strong><code>HAVING</code></strong> filters <strong>groups AFTER</strong> grouping (using aggregate results like <code>COUNT(*) &gt;= 3</code>).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"sqlrun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">SELECT city, COUNT(*) AS student_count, ROUND(AVG(gpa), 2) AS avg_gpa\nFROM Students\nGROUP BY city\nHAVING COUNT(*) &gt;= 2\nORDER BY student_count DESC;</pre></div>\n<p>Remember the full execution order of a <code>SELECT</code> query: <code>FROM</code> → <code>WHERE</code> → <code>GROUP BY</code> → <code>HAVING</code> → <code>SELECT</code> → <code>ORDER BY</code> → <code>LIMIT</code>.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Solve the aggregation challenges below.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"challenges\" data-lesson=\"db-10\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Putting an aggregate like <code>WHERE COUNT(*) &gt; 2</code> in <code>WHERE</code> — <code>WHERE</code> runs before grouping! Use <code>HAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 2</code>.</li><li>Selecting a non-aggregated column that is not listed in <code>GROUP BY</code> (SQL Server rejects this with an error).</li><li>Confusing <code>COUNT(*)</code> (counts every row) with <code>COUNT(email)</code> (skips rows where <code>email IS NULL</code>).</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>aggregate function</strong></td><td>a function that combines many rows into one summary value (<code>COUNT</code>, <code>SUM</code>, <code>AVG</code>, <code>MIN</code>, <code>MAX</code>)</td><td lang=\"tr\">kümeleme fonksiyonu</td></tr><tr><td><strong>GROUP BY</strong></td><td>groups rows with matching values so aggregates run per group</td><td lang=\"tr\">gruplama</td></tr><tr><td><strong>HAVING</strong></td><td>filters groups <em>after</em> <code>GROUP BY</code> and aggregate calculation</td><td lang=\"tr\">grup koşulu (HAVING)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"What is the difference between <code>COUNT(*)</code> and <code>COUNT(email)</code> on <code>Students</code>?","opts":[{"h":"No difference","why":"They give different numbers whenever <code>email</code> has <code>NULL</code> values."},{"h":"<code>COUNT(*)</code> counts all rows; <code>COUNT(email)</code> counts only rows where <code>email</code> is NOT <code>NULL</code>","ok":1,"why":"Aggregate functions on a column skip <code>NULL</code>s."},{"h":"<code>COUNT(email)</code> counts unique emails","why":"For unique values, use <code>COUNT(DISTINCT email)</code>."},{"h":"<code>COUNT(*)</code> only counts numeric columns","why":"<code>COUNT(*)</code> counts whole rows."}],"why":"<code>COUNT(*)</code> counts every row; <code>COUNT(column)</code> ignores <code>NULL</code>s in that column."},{"q":"Which clause is valid for keeping only departments whose average instructor salary is above 60,000?","opts":[{"h":"<code>WHERE AVG(salary) &gt; 60000</code>","why":"Aggregate functions cannot be used in <code>WHERE</code>."},{"h":"<code>HAVING AVG(salary) &gt; 60000</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>HAVING</code> filters groups after <code>GROUP BY</code>."},{"h":"<code>GROUP BY AVG(salary) &gt; 60000</code>","why":"<code>GROUP BY</code> takes column names, not conditions."},{"h":"<code>ORDER BY AVG(salary) &gt; 60000</code>","why":"<code>ORDER BY</code> sorts, it does not filter."}],"why":"Use <code>WHERE</code> to filter individual rows before grouping, and <code>HAVING</code> to filter groups after <code>GROUP BY</code>."},{"q":"In <code>SELECT city, COUNT(*) FROM Students WHERE enrollment_year = 2026 GROUP BY city HAVING COUNT(*) &gt;= 3</code>, what runs first?","opts":[{"h":"<code>HAVING</code>","why":"<code>HAVING</code> runs after <code>GROUP BY</code>."},{"h":"<code>GROUP BY</code>","why":"<code>WHERE</code> filters rows before <code>GROUP BY</code> runs."},{"h":"<code>WHERE enrollment_year = 2026</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>FROM</code> and <code>WHERE</code> filter individual rows before grouping."},{"h":"<code>SELECT</code>","why":"<code>SELECT</code> projects the final columns after filtering and grouping."}],"why":"Execution order: <code>FROM</code> → <code>WHERE</code> → <code>GROUP BY</code> → <code>HAVING</code> → <code>SELECT</code> → <code>ORDER BY</code>."}]],"id":"db-10","title":"DML queries: Aggregates, GROUP BY and HAVING","goal":"summarize rows with COUNT, SUM, AVG, MIN, MAX, group them with GROUP BY and filter groups with HAVING","week":"10","minutes":20,"terms":[{"t":"aggregate function","m":"a function that combines many rows into one summary value (`COUNT`, `SUM`, `AVG`, `MIN`, `MAX`)","tr":"kümeleme fonksiyonu"},{"t":"GROUP BY","m":"groups rows with matching values so aggregates run per group","tr":"gruplama"},{"t":"HAVING","m":"filters groups *after* `GROUP BY` and aggregate calculation","tr":"grup koşulu (HAVING)"}]},"db-11":{"html":"<p>In a normalized database, facts live in separate tables: student names in <code>Students</code>, department names in <code>Departments</code>, enrollments in <code>Enrollments</code>. A <strong>JOIN</strong> stitches rows back together using matching key columns (usually <code>FK = PK</code>).</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-join-types-at-a-glance\"><h2 id=\"join-types-at-a-glance\">JOIN types at a glance</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"venn\" data-types=\"inner,left,right\"></div>\n<ul><li><strong><code>INNER JOIN</code></strong> (or just <code>JOIN</code>): returns <strong>only</strong> rows that have a match in <strong>both</strong> tables. Students with 0 enrollments disappear!</li><li><strong><code>LEFT JOIN</code></strong> (<code>LEFT OUTER JOIN</code>): returns <strong>all</strong> rows from the <strong>left</strong> table, plus matching rows from the right table (or <code>NULL</code> when there is no match).</li><li><strong><code>RIGHT JOIN</code></strong> (<code>RIGHT OUTER JOIN</code>): returns <strong>all</strong> rows from the <strong>right</strong> table, plus matching rows from the left (or <code>NULL</code>).</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-worked-examples\"><h2 id=\"worked-examples\">Worked examples</h2>\n<p>An <code>INNER JOIN</code> across <code>Students</code>, <code>Enrollments</code> and <code>Courses</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"sqlrun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">SELECT s.first_name, s.last_name, c.course_code, e.grade\nFROM Students s\nINNER JOIN Enrollments e ON s.student_id = e.student_id\nINNER JOIN Courses c     ON e.course_id  = c.course_id\nORDER BY s.last_name, c.course_code\nLIMIT 12;</pre></div>\n<p>A <code>LEFT JOIN</code> to find students who have <strong>not</strong> enrolled in any course yet (<code>e.enrollment_id IS NULL</code>):</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"sqlrun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">SELECT s.student_id, s.first_name, s.last_name\nFROM Students s\nLEFT JOIN Enrollments e ON s.student_id = e.student_id\nWHERE e.enrollment_id IS NULL;</pre></div>\n<p>And <code>RIGHT JOIN</code> in SQL Server (T-SQL) — note that swapping the tables in a <code>LEFT JOIN</code> gives the exact same result:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\"><code>-- SQL Server (T-SQL): all courses, even ones with 0 enrollments\nSELECT c.course_code, c.title, e.student_id\nFROM Enrollments e\nRIGHT JOIN Courses c ON e.course_id = c.course_id;\n\n-- Equivalent LEFT JOIN (works in every SQL version):\nSELECT c.course_code, c.title, e.student_id\nFROM Courses c\nLEFT JOIN Enrollments e ON c.course_id = e.student_id;</code></pre>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Solve the JOIN challenges below.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"challenges\" data-lesson=\"db-11\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting the <code>ON</code> condition (writing <code>FROM Students JOIN Departments</code> without <code>ON</code> produces a Cartesian product — every student paired with every department!).</li><li>Writing <code>WHERE e.grade &gt;= 80</code> after a <code>LEFT JOIN</code> — that turns the <code>LEFT JOIN</code> back into an <code>INNER JOIN</code> because <code>NULL &gt;= 80</code> is false! Put right-table filters in the <code>ON</code> clause if you want to keep unmatched left rows.</li><li>Ambiguous column names: if both <code>Students</code> and <code>Departments</code> have <code>dept_id</code>, always qualify it as <code>s.dept_id</code>.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>INNER JOIN</strong></td><td>returns only rows with matching keys in both tables</td><td lang=\"tr\">iç birleştirme</td></tr><tr><td><strong>LEFT JOIN</strong></td><td>returns all left-table rows plus matches (or <code>NULL</code>) from the right</td><td lang=\"tr\">sol dış birleştirme</td></tr><tr><td><strong>RIGHT JOIN</strong></td><td>returns all right-table rows plus matches (or <code>NULL</code>) from the left</td><td lang=\"tr\">sağ dış birleştirme</td></tr><tr><td><strong>table alias</strong></td><td>a short nickname like <code>Students s</code> to keep queries readable</td><td lang=\"tr\">tablo takma adı</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"You want a list of <strong>all courses</strong>, including courses that have <strong>zero</strong> enrolled students. Which join from <code>Courses</code> to <code>Enrollments</code> should you use?","opts":[{"h":"<code>INNER JOIN</code>","why":"<code>INNER JOIN</code> drops courses with zero enrollments."},{"h":"<code>LEFT JOIN</code> (with <code>Courses</code> on the left)","ok":1,"why":"<code>LEFT JOIN</code> keeps every row from <code>Courses</code> even if <code>Enrollments</code> has no match."},{"h":"<code>CROSS JOIN</code>","why":"<code>CROSS JOIN</code> pairs every course with every enrollment."},{"h":"<code>UNION</code>","why":"<code>UNION</code> stacks rows vertically rather than joining columns horizontally."}],"why":"<code>Courses LEFT JOIN Enrollments ON ...</code> keeps all courses, filling enrollment columns with <code>NULL</code> when nobody enrolled."},{"q":"What happens in <code>SELECT s.first_name, e.grade FROM Students s LEFT JOIN Enrollments e ON s.student_id = e.student_id</code> for a student with no enrollments?","opts":[{"h":"The student is omitted","why":"<code>INNER JOIN</code> omits them; <code>LEFT JOIN</code> keeps them."},{"h":"The student appears once with <code>grade</code> equal to <code>NULL</code>","ok":1,"why":"Unmatched right-table columns become <code>NULL</code>."},{"h":"The query throws an error","why":"Missing matches are normal in outer joins."},{"h":"<code>grade</code> becomes <code>0</code>","why":"SQL uses <code>NULL</code> (unknown/missing), never <code>0</code>."}],"why":"Unmatched rows in a <code>LEFT JOIN</code> appear with <code>NULL</code> in all columns from the right table."},{"q":"If <code>Students</code> has 24 rows and <code>Departments</code> has 5 rows, what happens if you forget the <code>ON</code> clause in <code>SELECT * FROM Students CROSS JOIN Departments</code>?","opts":[{"h":"24 rows","why":"That only happens if each student matches 1 department via <code>ON</code>."},{"h":"29 rows","why":"Joins multiply, they don't add."},{"h":"120 rows (<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"24 \\times 5\"></span>)","ok":1,"why":"Without a join condition, every row of A pairs with every row of B."},{"h":"5 rows","why":"Only if <code>Departments</code> were filtered."}],"why":"Always double-check your <code>ON a.key = b.key</code> condition to avoid an accidental Cartesian product."}]],"id":"db-11","title":"DML queries: JOINs (INNER, LEFT, RIGHT)","goal":"combine rows from two or more related tables using INNER JOIN, LEFT JOIN and RIGHT JOIN","week":"11","minutes":25,"terms":[{"t":"INNER JOIN","m":"returns only rows with matching keys in both tables","tr":"iç birleştirme"},{"t":"LEFT JOIN","m":"returns all left-table rows plus matches (or `NULL`) from the right","tr":"sol dış birleştirme"},{"t":"RIGHT JOIN","m":"returns all right-table rows plus matches (or `NULL`) from the left","tr":"sağ dış birleştirme"},{"t":"table alias","m":"a short nickname like `Students s` to keep queries readable","tr":"tablo takma adı"}]},"db-12":{"html":"<p>Besides reading data with <code>SELECT</code>, DML gives you three commands that <strong>change</strong> rows: <code>INSERT</code>, <code>UPDATE</code>, and <code>DELETE</code>.</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-insert-update-delete\"><h2 id=\"insert-update-delete\"><code>INSERT</code>, <code>UPDATE</code>, <code>DELETE</code></h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"sqlrun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">-- 1. INSERT a new student\nINSERT INTO Students (first_name, last_name, city, birth_date, dept_id, enrollment_year, gpa)\nVALUES ('Tuna', 'Kaya', 'Istanbul', '2006-05-10', 1, 2026, 3.10);\n\n-- 2. UPDATE that student's GPA\nUPDATE Students\nSET gpa = 3.35\nWHERE first_name = 'Tuna' AND last_name = 'Kaya';\n\n-- 3. DELETE that student again\nDELETE FROM Students\nWHERE first_name = 'Tuna' AND last_name = 'Kaya';\n\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS student_count FROM Students;</pre></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-warn\"><div class=\"callout-h\">The most famous SQL mistake: forgetting <code>WHERE</code>!</div><p>If you run:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\"><code>UPDATE Students SET gpa = 0.0;\n-- or:\nDELETE FROM Students;</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Every single row in the table is overwritten or deleted!</strong> Always write (and test!) your <code>WHERE</code> condition with a <code>SELECT</code> first, and wrap risky changes in a transaction (<code>BEGIN</code> / <code>ROLLBACK</code> / <code>COMMIT</code>).</p></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Solve the <code>INSERT</code>, <code>UPDATE</code> and <code>DELETE</code> challenges below.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"challenges\" data-lesson=\"db-12\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Running <code>UPDATE</code> or <code>DELETE</code> without a <code>WHERE</code> clause (modifying or wiping the whole table).</li><li>Trying to <code>DELETE</code> a parent row (e.g. a Department) while child rows (<code>Students</code> or <code>Courses</code>) still reference it via a <code>FOREIGN KEY</code>.</li><li>Writing <code>UPDATE Students SET gpa = 3.5 AND city = 'Ankara'</code> — <code>SET</code> uses <strong>commas</strong>, not <code>AND</code> (<code>SET gpa = 3.5, city = 'Ankara'</code>).</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>INSERT INTO</strong></td><td>adds one or more new rows to a table</td><td lang=\"tr\">veri ekleme</td></tr><tr><td><strong>UPDATE ... SET</strong></td><td>modifies existing row values matching <code>WHERE</code></td><td lang=\"tr\">veri güncelleme</td></tr><tr><td><strong>DELETE FROM</strong></td><td>removes existing rows matching <code>WHERE</code></td><td lang=\"tr\">veri silme</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"What happens if you run <code>UPDATE Instructors SET salary = salary * 1.10;</code> with no <code>WHERE</code> clause?","opts":[{"h":"SQL refuses to run it","why":"SQL executes it without asking!"},{"h":"Only the first row is updated","why":"All rows are updated."},{"h":"Every instructor's salary is increased by 10%","ok":1,"why":"Without <code>WHERE</code>, <code>UPDATE</code> applies to every row in the table."},{"h":"Nothing changes","why":"Without <code>WHERE</code>, all rows match."}],"why":"Never omit <code>WHERE</code> in <code>UPDATE</code> or <code>DELETE</code> unless you truly intend to change every row."},{"q":"Which syntax is valid for updating two columns at once?","opts":[{"h":"<code>UPDATE Students SET city = 'Izmir' AND gpa = 3.5 WHERE student_id = 5;</code>","why":"<code>AND</code> is for conditions in <code>WHERE</code>, not assignments in <code>SET</code>."},{"h":"<code>UPDATE Students SET city = 'Izmir', gpa = 3.5 WHERE student_id = 5;</code>","ok":1,"why":"Separate column assignments in <code>SET</code> with a comma."},{"h":"<code>UPDATE Students (city, gpa) VALUES ('Izmir', 3.5) WHERE student_id = 5;</code>","why":"<code>VALUES</code> is for <code>INSERT</code>, not <code>UPDATE</code>."},{"h":"<code>MODIFY Students SET city = 'Izmir', gpa = 3.5;</code>","why":"<code>MODIFY</code> is not the command."}],"why":"In <code>UPDATE ... SET</code>, separate <code>col = val</code> pairs with commas."},{"q":"What is a safe habit before running a <code>DELETE FROM Students WHERE ...</code> on a real database?","opts":[{"h":"Run <code>DROP TABLE</code> first","why":"That deletes the whole table!"},{"h":"Run <code>SELECT * FROM Students WHERE ...</code> first to verify which rows match","ok":1,"why":"Confirm the exact rows before deleting them."},{"h":"Always omit <code>WHERE</code>","why":"Omitting <code>WHERE</code> deletes every row."},{"h":"Use <code>TRUNCATE</code> instead","why":"<code>TRUNCATE</code> deletes all rows unconditionally."}],"why":"Test your <code>WHERE</code> clause with <code>SELECT</code> first, and use transactions (<code>BEGIN</code> / <code>ROLLBACK</code> / <code>COMMIT</code>) when editing live data."}]],"id":"db-12","title":"DML queries: INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE","goal":"add, modify and remove rows safely with INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE (and avoid the missing-WHERE disaster)","week":"11","minutes":15,"terms":[{"t":"INSERT INTO","m":"adds one or more new rows to a table","tr":"veri ekleme"},{"t":"UPDATE ... SET","m":"modifies existing row values matching `WHERE`","tr":"veri güncelleme"},{"t":"DELETE FROM","m":"removes existing rows matching `WHERE`","tr":"veri silme"}]},"db-13":{"html":"<p>Not everyone should be able to read instructor salaries or <code>DROP TABLE Students</code>. <strong>DCL (Data Control Language)</strong> controls <em>who</em> can connect to the database and <em>what</em> actions they are allowed to perform.</p>\n<p><em>(Note: SQLite has no user/permission system, so DCL commands are shown below as read-only SQL Server / T-SQL examples.)</em></p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-logins-users-and-roles-sql-server\"><h2 id=\"logins-users-and-roles-sql-server\">Logins, users and roles (SQL Server)</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Login:</strong> Server-level account that lets you connect to the SQL Server instance (<code>CREATE LOGIN ali WITH PASSWORD = '...';</code>).</li><li><strong>User:</strong> Database-level identity mapped to a login inside one specific database (<code>CREATE USER ali FOR LOGIN ali;</code>).</li><li><strong>Role:</strong> A named group of permissions (e.g. <code>db_datareader</code> can <code>SELECT</code> from all tables; <code>db_datawriter</code> can <code>INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE</code>; or custom roles like <code>RegistrarRole</code>). Assigning permissions to roles instead of individual users makes management much easier.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-grant-revoke-and-deny\"><h2 id=\"grant-revoke-and-deny\"><code>GRANT</code>, <code>REVOKE</code> and <code>DENY</code></h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>GRANT</code></td><td>Gives a permission (e.g. <code>SELECT</code>, <code>INSERT</code>, <code>UPDATE</code>, <code>EXECUTE</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>REVOKE</code></td><td>Removes a previously granted (or denied) permission</td></tr><tr><td><code>DENY</code></td><td>Explicitly blocks a permission — <strong>overrides</strong> any <code>GRANT</code> from a role!</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\"><code>-- T-SQL (SQL Server) examples:\n\n-- 1. Allow student_app to read Students and Courses\nGRANT SELECT ON Students TO student_app;\nGRANT SELECT ON Courses  TO student_app;\n\n-- 2. Allow registrar_role to read and update Students, but block DELETE\nGRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE ON Students TO registrar_role;\nDENY DELETE ON Students TO registrar_role;\n\n-- 3. Take back INSERT permission later\nREVOKE INSERT ON Students FROM registrar_role;</code></pre>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-principle-of-least-privilege\"><h2 id=\"principle-of-least-privilege\">Principle of least privilege</h2>\n<p><strong>Least privilege</strong> (<em>en az yetki ilkesi</em>) means every user or application gets <strong>only</strong> the permissions it actually needs to do its job, and nothing more. A public course-catalog webpage only needs <code>SELECT</code> on <code>Courses</code> — never <code>DELETE</code> or <code>DROP</code>, and never <code>SELECT</code> on <code>Instructors.salary</code>.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Practice DCL concepts with this short quiz from the question bank.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"mcq\" data-course=\"db\" data-tag=\"13\" data-n=\"5\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Connecting web apps as <code>sa</code> (system administrator) instead of a limited user with least privilege.</li><li>Confusing <code>REVOKE</code> (removes a permission setting, so a role could still grant it) with <code>DENY</code> (actively blocks the permission even if a role grants it).</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>DCL</strong></td><td>Data Control Language (<code>GRANT</code>, <code>REVOKE</code>, <code>DENY</code>)</td><td lang=\"tr\">Veri Kontrol Dili</td></tr><tr><td><strong>GRANT</strong></td><td>gives a user or role permission to perform an action</td><td lang=\"tr\">yetki verme</td></tr><tr><td><strong>REVOKE</strong></td><td>removes a previously given permission</td><td lang=\"tr\">yetkiyi geri alma</td></tr><tr><td><strong>DENY</strong></td><td>explicitly blocks a permission, overriding any <code>GRANT</code></td><td lang=\"tr\">yetkiyi reddetme</td></tr><tr><td><strong>least privilege</strong></td><td>giving each account only the minimum permissions it needs</td><td lang=\"tr\">en az yetki ilkesi</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"Which DCL command gives <code>user_ali</code> permission to run <code>SELECT</code> on <code>Courses</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>GRANT SELECT ON Courses TO user_ali;</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>GRANT</code> assigns permissions."},{"h":"<code>ALLOW SELECT ON Courses TO user_ali;</code>","why":"<code>ALLOW</code> is not a SQL DCL keyword."},{"h":"<code>REVOKE SELECT ON Courses FROM user_ali;</code>","why":"<code>REVOKE</code> removes a permission."},{"h":"<code>DENY SELECT ON Courses TO user_ali;</code>","why":"<code>DENY</code> blocks a permission."}],"why":"Use <code>GRANT &lt;permission&gt; ON &lt;object&gt; TO &lt;principal&gt;;</code>."},{"q":"A user belongs to <code>db_datareader</code> (which has <code>GRANT SELECT</code>), but you must ensure they can NEVER read <code>Instructors</code>. Which command should you run?","opts":[{"h":"<code>REVOKE SELECT ON Instructors FROM that_user;</code>","why":"<code>REVOKE</code> only removes a direct grant; they would still inherit <code>SELECT</code> from <code>db_datareader</code>."},{"h":"<code>DENY SELECT ON Instructors TO that_user;</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>DENY</code> overrides any <code>GRANT</code> inherited from a role."},{"h":"<code>DROP USER that_user;</code>","why":"That removes the user from the whole database."},{"h":"<code>DELETE FROM Instructors;</code>","why":"That deletes the data itself!"}],"why":"<code>DENY</code> takes precedence over <code>GRANT</code> (even from role membership)."},{"q":"What does the <strong>principle of least privilege</strong> recommend?","opts":[{"h":"Give every developer <code>sa</code> admin rights so nothing breaks","why":"That is the opposite of least privilege."},{"h":"Give each user or app only the exact permissions required for its task","ok":1,"why":"Limits damage if an account is compromised or has a bug."},{"h":"Store passwords in plain text","why":"Never store plain-text passwords."},{"h":"Disable all <code>FOREIGN KEY</code> constraints","why":"Constraints protect data integrity, not access control."}],"why":"Least privilege minimizes risk by granting only necessary permissions."}]],"id":"db-13","title":"DCL queries: Users, roles and permissions","goal":"understand logins, users, roles, GRANT, REVOKE, DENY and the principle of least privilege","week":"12–13","minutes":15,"terms":[{"t":"DCL","m":"Data Control Language (`GRANT`, `REVOKE`, `DENY`)","tr":"Veri Kontrol Dili"},{"t":"GRANT","m":"gives a user or role permission to perform an action","tr":"yetki verme"},{"t":"REVOKE","m":"removes a previously given permission","tr":"yetkiyi geri alma"},{"t":"DENY","m":"explicitly blocks a permission, overriding any `GRANT`","tr":"yetkiyi reddetme"},{"t":"least privilege","m":"giving each account only the minimum permissions it needs","tr":"en az yetki ilkesi"}]},"db-14":{"html":"<section class=\"sec sec-subqueries-nested-queries\"><h2 id=\"subqueries-nested-queries\">Subqueries (nested queries)</h2>\n<p>A <strong>subquery</strong> is a <code>SELECT</code> inside parentheses <code>( ... )</code> used inside another query.</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Scalar subquery in <code>WHERE</code>:</strong> returns a single value (e.g. an average).</li></ol>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"sqlrun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">-- Students whose GPA is above the overall average GPA\nSELECT first_name, last_name, gpa\nFROM Students\nWHERE gpa &gt; (SELECT AVG(gpa) FROM Students)\nORDER BY gpa DESC;</pre></div>\n<ol><li><strong><code>IN</code> and <code>EXISTS</code>:</strong> test whether a value is in a subquery's list, or whether any matching row exists.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"sqlrun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">-- Students who have at least one enrollment with grade &gt;= 90\nSELECT first_name, last_name\nFROM Students s\nWHERE EXISTS (\n    SELECT 1 FROM Enrollments e\n    WHERE e.student_id = s.student_id AND e.grade &gt;= 90\n);</pre></div>\n<ol><li><strong>Subquery in <code>FROM</code> (derived table):</strong> treat a query result like a temporary table.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"sqlrun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">SELECT ROUND(AVG(course_avg), 2) AS avg_of_course_avgs\nFROM (\n    SELECT course_id, AVG(grade) AS course_avg\n    FROM Enrollments\n    WHERE grade IS NOT NULL\n    GROUP BY course_id\n);</pre></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-transactions-begin-commit-rollback\"><h2 id=\"transactions-begin-commit-rollback\">Transactions (<code>BEGIN</code>, <code>COMMIT</code>, <code>ROLLBACK</code>)</h2>\n<p>A <strong>transaction</strong> (<em>işlem</em>) groups multiple SQL statements into <strong>one all-or-nothing unit</strong> — like transferring 500 TL from Account A to Account B (subtract from A AND add to B).</p>\n<ul><li><code>BEGIN TRANSACTION</code> (SQLite: <code>BEGIN</code>, SQL Server: <code>BEGIN TRAN</code>): starts a transaction.</li><li><code>COMMIT</code>: saves all changes permanently.</li><li><code>ROLLBACK</code>: undoes every change since <code>BEGIN</code>, leaving the database as if nothing happened.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"sqlrun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">BEGIN;\nUPDATE Instructors SET salary = salary + 5000 WHERE instructor_id = 1;\n-- Oops, changed our mind! Undo it:\nROLLBACK;\n\nSELECT instructor_id, first_name, salary FROM Instructors WHERE instructor_id = 1;</pre></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-acid-properties-in-plain-words\"><h2 id=\"acid-properties-in-plain-words\">ACID properties in plain words</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Letter</th><th>Property</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>A</strong></td><td><strong>Atomicity</strong> (<em>Bölünmezlik</em>)</td><td>All-or-nothing: either every step in the transaction succeeds, or all of them roll back.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>C</strong></td><td><strong>Consistency</strong> (<em>Tutarlılık</em>)</td><td>Every transaction leaves the database valid (all constraints, PKs, FKs and balances hold).</td></tr><tr><td><strong>I</strong></td><td><strong>Isolation</strong> (<em>Yalıtım</em>)</td><td>Concurrent transactions do not interfere with each other's half-finished work.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>D</strong></td><td><strong>Durability</strong> (<em>Kalıcılık</em>)</td><td>Once <code>COMMIT</code> succeeds, the data survives a power cut or crash.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Solve the subquery challenges below.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"challenges\" data-lesson=\"db-14\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Using <code>=</code> with a subquery that returns more than 1 row (<code>WHERE dept_id = (SELECT dept_id FROM ...)</code> crashes if the subquery returns 2+ rows — use <code>IN</code> instead).</li><li>Forgetting <code>ROLLBACK</code> or <code>COMMIT</code> in SSMS after <code>BEGIN TRAN</code> — an open transaction holds locks and blocks other users!</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>subquery</strong></td><td>a <code>SELECT</code> query nested inside another SQL statement</td><td lang=\"tr\">alt sorgu</td></tr><tr><td><strong>transaction</strong></td><td>a group of SQL steps executed as one all-or-nothing unit</td><td lang=\"tr\">işlem (transaction)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>COMMIT</strong></td><td>permanently saves all changes made in the current transaction</td><td lang=\"tr\">onaylama (COMMIT)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>ROLLBACK</strong></td><td>undoes all changes made since <code>BEGIN</code></td><td lang=\"tr\">geri alma (ROLLBACK)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>ACID</strong></td><td>Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability</td><td lang=\"tr\">ACID özellikleri</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"A subquery inside <code>WHERE dept_id = (SELECT ...)</code> returns 3 rows. What happens?","opts":[{"h":"SQL picks the first row","why":"SQL raises an error instead of guessing."},{"h":"The query fails because <code>=</code> expects a single scalar value","ok":1,"why":"Use <code>IN (...)</code> when a subquery can return multiple rows."},{"h":"SQL averages the 3 rows","why":"It does not aggregate automatically."},{"h":"It returns all rows of the table","why":"It raises a multi-row subquery error."}],"why":"Use <code>=</code> only for scalar (1-row, 1-column) subqueries; use <code>IN</code> or <code>EXISTS</code> for multi-row subqueries."},{"q":"Which ACID property guarantees <em>\"either all steps of the bank transfer happen, or none of them happen\"</em>?","opts":[{"h":"Atomicity","ok":1,"why":"Atomicity means indivisible (\"all or nothing\")."},{"h":"Consistency","why":"Consistency means constraints and rules remain valid."},{"h":"Isolation","why":"Isolation keeps concurrent transactions separated."},{"h":"Durability","why":"Durability means committed data survives crashes."}],"why":"Atomicity = all or nothing."},{"q":"You ran <code>BEGIN TRAN; DELETE FROM Enrollments;</code> and immediately realize you forgot <code>WHERE</code>. How do you restore the rows?","opts":[{"h":"<code>COMMIT;</code>","why":"<code>COMMIT</code> makes the deletion permanent!"},{"h":"<code>ROLLBACK;</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>ROLLBACK</code> undoes everything since <code>BEGIN TRAN</code>."},{"h":"<code>REVOKE;</code>","why":"<code>REVOKE</code> is a DCL permission command."},{"h":"<code>TRUNCATE TABLE Enrollments;</code>","why":"<code>TRUNCATE</code> also empties the table."}],"why":"<code>ROLLBACK</code> cancels all uncommitted changes back to <code>BEGIN</code>."}]],"id":"db-14","title":"Subqueries and transactions (ACID)","goal":"write subqueries in WHERE and FROM (with IN and EXISTS) and explain BEGIN, COMMIT, ROLLBACK and ACID","week":"14","minutes":25,"terms":[{"t":"subquery","m":"a `SELECT` query nested inside another SQL statement","tr":"alt sorgu"},{"t":"transaction","m":"a group of SQL steps executed as one all-or-nothing unit","tr":"işlem (transaction)"},{"t":"COMMIT","m":"permanently saves all changes made in the current transaction","tr":"onaylama (COMMIT)"},{"t":"ROLLBACK","m":"undoes all changes made since `BEGIN`","tr":"geri alma (ROLLBACK)"},{"t":"ACID","m":"Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability","tr":"ACID özellikleri"}]},"db-15":{"html":"<p>Hardware fails, disks die, and humans accidentally run <code>DELETE</code> without <code>WHERE</code>. A <strong>backup</strong> is your safety net.</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-full-vs-differential-backup\"><h2 id=\"full-vs-differential-backup\">Full vs differential backup</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>What it copies</th><th>Speed &amp; size</th><th>Restore steps</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Full backup</strong> (<em>Tam yedek</em>)</td><td>The <strong>entire</strong> database</td><td>Slower, largest file</td><td>Restore just this 1 file</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Differential backup</strong> (<em>Fark yedeği</em>)</td><td>Only data changed <strong>since the last full backup</strong></td><td>Faster, smaller</td><td>Restore the <strong>last full</strong> backup + the <strong>latest differential</strong> backup</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><em>(In SQL Server, there is also a <strong>Transaction Log backup</strong> for point-in-time recovery, e.g. restoring to 14:29 right before a bad <code>DELETE</code> at 14:30.)</em></p>\n<div class=\"callout callout-tsql\"><div class=\"callout-h\">Backup and restore in SQL Server</div><p>In SSMS: right-click the database › <strong>Tasks › Back Up…</strong> (or <strong>Restore › Database…</strong>). In T-SQL:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\"><code>BACKUP DATABASE UniversityDB\nTO DISK = 'C:\\Backups\\UniversityDB_full.bak';\n\n-- Differential backup later:\nBACKUP DATABASE UniversityDB\nTO DISK = 'C:\\Backups\\UniversityDB_diff.bak'\nWITH DIFFERENTIAL;</code></pre></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-exporting-data-and-generating-scripts\"><h2 id=\"exporting-data-and-generating-scripts\">Exporting data and generating scripts</h2>\n<p>Often you need to share data with someone who uses Excel, or move a database structure to another server:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Export to CSV / Excel:</strong> In SSMS, right-click the database › <strong>Tasks › Export Data…</strong> (or right-click a query result grid › <strong>Save Results As…</strong> <code>.csv</code>). A <strong>CSV</strong> (<em>Comma-Separated Values</em>) file opens directly in Excel.</li><li><strong>Generate SQL Scripts:</strong> Right-click the database › <strong>Tasks › Generate Scripts…</strong> to export <code>CREATE TABLE</code> statements (schema only, or schema + <code>INSERT</code> data) as a <code>.sql</code> text file.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Run this query, which produces a clean summary table ready to export to CSV for an academic advisor.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"sqlrun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">SELECT\n    s.student_id,\n    s.first_name || ' ' || s.last_name AS full_name,\n    d.dept_name,\n    COALESCE(CAST(s.gpa AS TEXT), 'N/A') AS gpa\nFROM Students s\nJOIN Departments d ON s.dept_id = d.dept_id\nORDER BY d.dept_name, s.last_name;</pre></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Keeping backups on the <strong>same</strong> physical disk or server as the live database — if that disk dies or is hit by ransomware, both are lost.</li><li>Taking differential backups without ever testing a <strong>restore</strong> — an untested backup is not a reliable backup.</li><li>Trying to restore <em>only</em> a differential backup without restoring the base full backup first.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>full backup</strong></td><td>a complete copy of the entire database</td><td lang=\"tr\">tam yedek</td></tr><tr><td><strong>differential backup</strong></td><td>a copy of all changes since the last full backup</td><td lang=\"tr\">fark yedeği</td></tr><tr><td><strong>restore</strong></td><td>loading a backup file to recover a database</td><td lang=\"tr\">geri yükleme</td></tr><tr><td><strong>CSV</strong></td><td>Comma-Separated Values — plain-text table format opened by Excel</td><td lang=\"tr\">CSV (virgülle ayrılmış değerler)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"You took a <strong>Full</strong> backup on Sunday, and <strong>Differential</strong> backups on Monday, Tuesday and Wednesday. The server crashes on Thursday morning. What do you need to restore?","opts":[{"h":"Sunday full + Monday diff + Tuesday diff + Wednesday diff","why":"Differential backups are cumulative since the last full backup, so Wednesday's already includes Monday and Tuesday."},{"h":"Sunday <strong>full</strong> backup + Wednesday <strong>differential</strong> backup","ok":1,"why":"Only the last full and the latest differential are needed."},{"h":"Only Wednesday's differential backup","why":"A differential backup cannot be restored without its base full backup."},{"h":"Only Monday's differential backup","why":"You would lose Tuesday and Wednesday's changes."}],"why":"To restore with differential backups, restore the latest <strong>full</strong> backup first, then the <strong>latest</strong> differential backup."},{"q":"Which format is best for exporting a <code>SELECT</code> result so a colleague can open it directly in Microsoft Excel?","opts":[{"h":"<code>.bak</code>","why":"<code>.bak</code> is a binary SQL Server backup file."},{"h":"<code>.csv</code>","ok":1,"why":"CSV is plain-text tabular data that Excel opens directly."},{"h":"<code>.exe</code>","why":"<code>.exe</code> is a Windows program, not data."},{"h":"<code>.pkt</code>","why":"<code>.pkt</code> is a Cisco Packet Tracer file."}],"why":"Export query results as <code>.csv</code> for Excel, or use <strong>Generate Scripts…</strong> (<code>.sql</code>) to recreate tables and data in SQL Server."},{"q":"In SSMS, which wizard creates a <code>.sql</code> file containing the <code>CREATE TABLE</code> statements (and optionally <code>INSERT</code>s) of your database?","opts":[{"h":"Shrink Database","why":"Shrink frees unused file space."},{"h":"Tasks › Generate Scripts…","ok":1,"why":"Generate Scripts exports schema and/or data as SQL statements."},{"h":"Activity Monitor","why":"Activity Monitor shows live server processes."},{"h":"SQL Server Profiler","why":"Profiler traces live SQL events."}],"why":"Use <strong>Tasks › Generate Scripts…</strong> to script out tables and data as <code>.sql</code>."}]],"id":"db-15","title":"Backup, restore and exporting data","goal":"compare full and differential backups, understand restore and export data to CSV or SQL scripts","week":"15","minutes":15,"terms":[{"t":"full backup","m":"a complete copy of the entire database","tr":"tam yedek"},{"t":"differential backup","m":"a copy of all changes since the last full backup","tr":"fark yedeği"},{"t":"restore","m":"loading a backup file to recover a database","tr":"geri yükleme"},{"t":"CSV","m":"Comma-Separated Values — plain-text table format opened by Excel","tr":"CSV (virgülle ayrılmış değerler)"}]}},"bank":[{"q":"Which best describes <strong>data</strong> before it is processed?","opts":[{"h":"Raw, unorganized facts such as <code>85</code> or <code>'Ankara'</code>","ok":1,"why":"Raw facts without context are data."},{"h":"A printed report comparing semester averages","why":"A report with context and summaries is information."},{"h":"A table of student pass rates","why":"Summarized, meaningful output is information."},{"h":"A database backup file","why":"That is a storage artifact."}],"why":"Data consists of raw facts; organizing and giving them context produces information.","tag":"01"},{"q":"Which problem occurs when the same customer address is stored in three files and only one file is updated?","opts":[{"h":"Data inconsistency","ok":1,"why":"Different copies of the same fact disagree."},{"h":"Atomicity failure","why":"Atomicity relates to database transactions."},{"h":"Normalization","why":"Normalization prevents this problem."},{"h":"Primary key collision","why":"That is a uniqueness violation."}],"why":"Redundancy (multiple copies) causes inconsistency when updates are not applied everywhere.","tag":"01"},{"q":"Why did databases replace flat file systems for multi-user applications?","opts":[{"h":"Flat files cannot store numbers","why":"Flat files can store numbers as text or binary."},{"h":"Databases centralize data, enforce integrity rules, and manage concurrent access and security","ok":1,"why":"One DBMS coordinates all users safely."},{"h":"Databases never require a schema","why":"Relational databases enforce a strict schema."},{"h":"Flat files cannot be copied","why":"Flat files can be copied, which actually worsens redundancy."}],"why":"A DBMS solves redundancy, inconsistency, concurrency and security problems of flat files.","tag":"01"},{"q":"In the university database, \"There are 14 Computer Programming students in Istanbul with an average GPA of 3.31\" is an example of:","opts":[{"h":"Raw data","why":"Raw data would be individual ungrouped numbers."},{"h":"Information","ok":1,"why":"It is processed, summarized and meaningful."},{"h":"Metadata","why":"Metadata describes table structure (column names/types), not student counts."},{"h":"A foreign key","why":"A foreign key is a relational link column."}],"why":"Meaningful, processed output derived from raw rows is information.","tag":"01"},{"q":"Which file-system issue happens when two clerks save edits to the same spreadsheet at the same time and one overwrites the other?","opts":[{"h":"Data redundancy","why":"Redundancy is duplicate storage."},{"h":"Lack of concurrency control","ok":1,"why":"Without a DBMS locking/isolating changes, concurrent writes overwrite each other."},{"h":"Data normalization","why":"Normalization is schema design."},{"h":"Referential integrity","why":"Referential integrity enforces valid foreign keys."}],"why":"A DBMS provides concurrency control so multiple users can work safely at the same time.","tag":"01"},{"q":"Which software is a <strong>DBMS</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"SQL","why":"SQL is a query language, not the DBMS software itself."},{"h":"Microsoft SQL Server","ok":1,"why":"SQL Server is the DBMS software that stores data and executes SQL."},{"h":"ER Diagram","why":"An ER diagram is a design drawing."},{"h":"CSV","why":"CSV is a plain-text file format."}],"why":"Examples of DBMS software: SQL Server, PostgreSQL, MySQL, Oracle, SQLite.","tag":"02"},{"q":"Which data model organizes data into two-dimensional tables linked by primary and foreign keys?","opts":[{"h":"Hierarchical","why":"Uses a strict single-parent tree."},{"h":"Network","why":"Uses a graph of records and pointers."},{"h":"Relational","ok":1,"why":"Uses tables (relations), rows and columns linked by keys."},{"h":"Key-value","why":"A NoSQL model storing opaque values by key."}],"why":"The relational model (Codd, 1970) stores data in tables linked by keys.","tag":"02"},{"q":"In the ANSI-SPARC three-level architecture, what does the <strong>external level</strong> define?","opts":[{"h":"How bytes and B-tree indexes are laid out on disk","why":"That is the internal (physical) level."},{"h":"All tables and relationships in the entire database","why":"That is the conceptual level."},{"h":"Customized views showing only the data a specific user or app needs","ok":1,"why":"Different users see different external views."},{"h":"The network cable speed","why":"Hardware networking is outside ANSI-SPARC."}],"why":"External = user views; Conceptual = whole logical schema; Internal = physical storage.","tag":"02"},{"q":"What is <strong>logical data independence</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"The ability to change the conceptual schema (e.g. add a column) without breaking existing user views/programs","ok":1,"why":"External views stay stable."},{"h":"Keeping data in Excel instead of a DBMS","why":"Not data independence."},{"h":"Running SQL without a computer","why":"Impossible."},{"h":"Deleting all foreign keys","why":"Removing keys breaks integrity, not independence."}],"why":"Three-level architecture isolates external views from conceptual changes, and conceptual design from physical storage changes.","tag":"02"},{"q":"In a client–server DBMS like SQL Server, where does the query execution engine run?","opts":[{"h":"Inside the user's web browser","why":"Only embedded engines like SQLite/sql.js run in the browser."},{"h":"On the database server","ok":1,"why":"Clients (apps, SSMS) send SQL over the network to the server."},{"h":"On the keyboard","why":"Hardware input device."},{"h":"Inside a CSV file","why":"CSV is just text."}],"why":"Clients send SQL requests to the database server, which processes them and returns results.","tag":"02"},{"q":"Which stage of database design chooses exact DBMS column types like <code>NVARCHAR(50)</code> and indexes?","opts":[{"h":"Conceptual design","why":"Conceptual design builds the ER model, independent of any DBMS."},{"h":"Logical design","why":"Logical design maps ER to normalized tables and keys."},{"h":"Physical design","ok":1,"why":"Physical design picks DBMS-specific types, indexes and storage."},{"h":"Requirements gathering","why":"Happens before conceptual design."}],"why":"Conceptual (ER) → Logical (tables/keys/3NF) → Physical (DBMS types/indexes).","tag":"03"},{"q":"In a <strong>1:N</strong> relationship between <code>Departments</code> (1) and <code>Students</code> (N), where do you place the Foreign Key?","opts":[{"h":"<code>dept_id</code> inside <code>Students</code>","ok":1,"why":"The FK always goes on the \"many\" (N) side."},{"h":"<code>student_id</code> inside <code>Departments</code>","why":"That would allow only one student per department."},{"h":"A third junction table is mandatory","why":"Junction tables are needed for M:N, not 1:N."},{"h":"Neither table needs a key","why":"Relational links require PK and FK."}],"why":"For 1:N, place the parent's PK as a Foreign Key in the child (\"many\") table.","tag":"03"},{"q":"Why do we introduce a junction table like <code>Enrollments</code> between <code>Students</code> and <code>Courses</code>?","opts":[{"h":"To avoid using Primary Keys","why":"<code>Enrollments</code> still has a Primary Key."},{"h":"To resolve a Many-to-Many (M:N) relationship into two 1:N relationships","ok":1,"why":"Relational tables cannot store M:N directly."},{"h":"Because SQL cannot join two tables","why":"SQL joins tables easily."},{"h":"To delete duplicate students","why":"Junction tables represent relationships, not deduplication."}],"why":"M:N relationships are always resolved with a junction table holding FKs to both sides.","tag":"03"},{"q":"Which property MUST every <strong>Primary Key</strong> have?","opts":[{"h":"It must be a text column","why":"Integer IDs are very common primary keys."},{"h":"Every value must be unique and NOT NULL","ok":1,"why":"A PK uniquely identifies each row."},{"h":"It must be hidden from <code>SELECT</code>","why":"PKs are normal columns you can select and join on."},{"h":"It can contain <code>NULL</code> for new rows","why":"Primary keys can never be <code>NULL</code>."}],"why":"Primary Key = Unique + Not Null.","tag":"03"},{"q":"In an ER diagram for a hospital, <code>Doctors</code> work in <code>Departments</code> (each doctor in 1 department, each department has many doctors). What is the cardinality?","opts":[{"h":"1:1","why":"A department has more than one doctor."},{"h":"1:N","ok":1,"why":"One department → many doctors."},{"h":"M:N","why":"Each doctor belongs to only one department here."},{"h":"0:0","why":"Not a valid cardinality."}],"why":"One department has many doctors → 1:N.","tag":"03"},{"q":"Which problem happens when deleting the last student in a course also erases the course's title and credits?","opts":[{"h":"Insert anomaly","why":"Insert anomaly is being unable to add a fact."},{"h":"Update anomaly","why":"Update anomaly is partial/inconsistent edits."},{"h":"Delete anomaly","ok":1,"why":"Removing one row unintentionally destroys unrelated data."},{"h":"Foreign key violation","why":"A FK violation blocks a delete; a delete anomaly loses data."}],"why":"A delete anomaly occurs when deleting a record wipes out another entity's facts stored in the same row.","tag":"04"},{"q":"A table stores <code>courses = \"CMP151, CMP153\"</code> in one cell. Which normal form does it violate?","opts":[{"h":"1NF","ok":1,"why":"1NF requires every cell to hold a single atomic value."},{"h":"Only 3NF","why":"It fails 1NF immediately, before 2NF or 3NF even apply."},{"h":"Only BCNF","why":"It fails 1NF first."},{"h":"None","why":"Multi-valued cells are never 1NF."}],"why":"1NF forbids lists or repeating groups in a cell.","tag":"04"},{"q":"What does <code>A → B</code> mean in normalization?","opts":[{"h":"<code>A</code> is smaller than <code>B</code>","why":"Not a comparison."},{"h":"<code>B</code> is functionally dependent on <code>A</code> (each <code>A</code> determines one <code>B</code>)","ok":1,"why":"Knowing <code>A</code> gives a unique <code>B</code>."},{"h":"<code>A</code> is deleted when <code>B</code> changes","why":"Not deletion."},{"h":"<code>A</code> and <code>B</code> are in different databases","why":"They are attributes in a relation."}],"why":"Functional dependency <code>A → B</code> means <code>A</code> uniquely determines <code>B</code>.","tag":"04"},{"q":"Why do columns like <code>phone1, phone2, phone3</code> in a <code>Students</code> table violate 1NF?","opts":[{"h":"They are text","why":"Text is fine in 1NF."},{"h":"They are a repeating group","ok":1,"why":"Repeating columns for the same attribute violate 1NF (use a child <code>StudentPhones</code> table instead)."},{"h":"They are not integers","why":"Types don't violate 1NF."},{"h":"They have no <code>DEFAULT</code>","why":"<code>DEFAULT</code> is optional."}],"why":"Both comma-separated lists and repeating columns (<code>phone1, phone2</code>) violate 1NF.","tag":"04"},{"q":"When you cannot add a new <code>Department</code> until a <code>Student</code> enrolls in it, what anomaly is that?","opts":[{"h":"Insert anomaly","ok":1,"why":"Cannot insert valid department data without unrelated student data."},{"h":"Update anomaly","why":"Happens on edits."},{"h":"Delete anomaly","why":"Happens on deletes."},{"h":"Transitive dependency","why":"A dependency pattern that <em>causes</em> anomalies, not the anomaly itself."}],"why":"Insert anomaly = blocked from inserting one entity because another entity is absent.","tag":"04"},{"q":"A table is in <strong>2NF</strong> when it is in 1NF and has no:","opts":[{"h":"Foreign keys","why":"Foreign keys are allowed and expected."},{"h":"Partial dependencies (non-key columns depending on only part of a composite PK)","ok":1,"why":"Every non-key column must depend on the whole PK."},{"h":"Composite keys","why":"Composite keys are fine in 2NF as long as non-key columns depend on the whole key."},{"h":"<code>NULL</code> values","why":"<code>NULL</code>s are unrelated to 2NF."}],"why":"2NF removes partial dependencies.","tag":"05"},{"q":"A table <code>Orders(&lt;u&gt;order_id&lt;/u&gt;, order_date, customer_id, customer_city)</code> has a single-column PK <code>order_id</code>. Which normal form does it violate?","opts":[{"h":"1NF","why":"All cells are atomic and it has a PK."},{"h":"2NF","why":"A single-column PK cannot have partial dependencies."},{"h":"3NF","ok":1,"why":"<code>order_id → customer_id → customer_city</code> is a transitive dependency."},{"h":"None","why":"<code>customer_city</code> depends on <code>customer_id</code>, not directly on <code>order_id</code>."}],"why":"Move <code>customer_id, customer_city</code> into a <code>Customers</code> table to reach 3NF.","tag":"05"},{"q":"Why is a table with a single-column Primary Key automatically in 2NF (if it is already in 1NF)?","opts":[{"h":"Because 2NF only applies to integers","why":"2NF applies to all types."},{"h":"Because there is no \"smaller part\" of a 1-column key to partially depend on","ok":1,"why":"Partial dependency requires a composite (2+ column) key."},{"h":"Because single-column keys are always 3NF","why":"A single-column key table can still fail 3NF via transitive dependencies."},{"h":"Because SQL Server skips 2NF","why":"Normalization is a mathematical design rule."}],"why":"Partial dependencies can only exist when the primary key has 2 or more columns.","tag":"05"},{"q":"What is a <strong>transitive dependency</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"A foreign key pointing to itself","why":"That is a self-referencing FK."},{"h":"A non-key column depending on another non-key column (<code>PK → X → Y</code>)","ok":1,"why":"Eliminated in 3NF."},{"h":"Two tables with the same column name","why":"Normal for PK/FK pairs."},{"h":"A table with no rows","why":"Empty tables still have a schema."}],"why":"3NF removes transitive dependencies (<code>PK → non-key → non-key</code>).","tag":"05"},{"q":"Which normal form is summarized as <em>\"the key, the whole key, and nothing but the key\"</em>?","opts":[{"h":"1NF only","why":"1NF is atomic values + key."},{"h":"2NF only","why":"2NF adds \"the whole key\"."},{"h":"3NF","ok":1,"why":"1NF (\"the key\"), 2NF (\"the whole key\"), 3NF (\"nothing but the key\")."},{"h":"0NF (UNF)","why":"UNF is unnormalized."}],"why":"3NF ensures every non-key attribute depends only on the entire primary key.","tag":"05"},{"q":"Which SQL Server data type should you choose for storing exact prices like <code>149.90</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>INT</code>","why":"<code>INT</code> drops <code>.90</code>."},{"h":"<code>BIT</code>","why":"<code>BIT</code> only stores <code>0</code> or <code>1</code>."},{"h":"<code>DECIMAL(10, 2)</code>","ok":1,"why":"Exact fixed-point decimal with 2 digits after the point."},{"h":"<code>NVARCHAR(10)</code>","why":"Text cannot be summed or averaged cleanly."}],"why":"Use <code>DECIMAL(p, s)</code> for money and exact decimals.","tag":"06"},{"q":"Why is <code>NVARCHAR</code> preferred over <code>VARCHAR</code> for names in Turkey?","opts":[{"h":"<code>NVARCHAR</code> takes half the disk space","why":"<code>NVARCHAR</code> actually uses 2 bytes per char (UTF-16), more than <code>VARCHAR</code>."},{"h":"<code>NVARCHAR</code> stores Unicode characters like <code>ş, ğ, ı, İ, ö, ü, ç</code> reliably","ok":1,"why":"Non-Unicode <code>VARCHAR</code> can turn them into <code>?</code>."},{"h":"<code>VARCHAR</code> only stores numbers","why":"<code>VARCHAR</code> stores text, just non-Unicode."},{"h":"<code>NVARCHAR</code> automatically encrypts text","why":"It does not encrypt."}],"why":"<code>NVARCHAR</code> supports Unicode across all collations.","tag":"06"},{"q":"In SQL Server, which statement switches your active session to the <code>UniversityDB</code> database?","opts":[{"h":"<code>SELECT UniversityDB;</code>","why":"<code>SELECT</code> queries rows."},{"h":"<code>USE UniversityDB;</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>USE</code> sets the current database context."},{"h":"<code>OPEN UniversityDB;</code>","why":"Not T-SQL syntax."},{"h":"<code>CONNECT UniversityDB;</code>","why":"Not T-SQL syntax."}],"why":"<code>USE &lt;db_name&gt;;</code> switches the active database in SQL Server.","tag":"06"},{"q":"Which SQL Server data type stores a true/false flag (<code>1</code> or <code>0</code>)?","opts":[{"h":"<code>CHAR(1)</code>","why":"Stores any single character, not a boolean bit."},{"h":"<code>BIT</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>BIT</code> stores <code>0</code>, <code>1</code>, or <code>NULL</code>."},{"h":"<code>DATE</code>","why":"Stores calendar dates."},{"h":"<code>REAL</code>","why":"Stores floating-point numbers."}],"why":"SQL Server uses <code>BIT</code> for boolean flags.","tag":"06"},{"q":"Which tool is the standard Microsoft desktop GUI for managing SQL Server and running T-SQL queries?","opts":[{"h":"Cisco Packet Tracer","why":"Packet Tracer is for networks."},{"h":"SSMS (SQL Server Management Studio)","ok":1,"why":"SSMS is Microsoft's IDE for SQL Server."},{"h":"Eclipse","why":"Eclipse is a Java/C IDE."},{"h":"Microsoft PowerPoint","why":"PowerPoint is for presentations."}],"why":"SSMS connects to SQL Server Express so you can run queries and manage databases.","tag":"06"},{"q":"Which constraint ensures a <code>grade</code> column only accepts numbers from <code>0</code> to <code>100</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>UNIQUE</code>","why":"Forbids duplicates, not out-of-range numbers."},{"h":"<code>NOT NULL</code>","why":"Forbids <code>NULL</code>, not <code>150</code>."},{"h":"<code>CHECK (grade &gt;= 0 AND grade &lt;= 100)</code>","ok":1,"why":"Rejects any value outside <code>0..100</code>."},{"h":"<code>DEFAULT 0</code>","why":"Only provides a fallback if omitted."}],"why":"<code>CHECK</code> enforces custom boolean rules on column values.","tag":"07"},{"q":"What is the SQLite equivalent of SQL Server's <code>IDENTITY(1,1)</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>SERIAL</code>","why":"PostgreSQL."},{"h":"<code>AUTO_INCREMENT</code>","why":"MySQL."},{"h":"<code>INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT</code>","ok":1,"why":"SQLite's auto-incrementing PK syntax."},{"h":"<code>SEQUENCE</code>","why":"Oracle/SQL Server object, not a column keyword."}],"why":"SQL Server uses <code>IDENTITY(1,1)</code>; SQLite uses <code>AUTOINCREMENT</code>.","tag":"07"},{"q":"What happens if you omit a column that has <code>DEFAULT '2026-Fall'</code> in an <code>INSERT</code> statement?","opts":[{"h":"The <code>INSERT</code> fails","why":"It only fails if there is no default AND <code>NOT NULL</code> is set."},{"h":"The database automatically inserts <code>'2026-Fall'</code>","ok":1,"why":"That is what <code>DEFAULT</code> is for."},{"h":"It inserts <code>0</code>","why":"It uses the defined default <code>'2026-Fall'</code>."},{"h":"It inserts <code>NULL</code>","why":"It uses <code>'2026-Fall'</code> unless you explicitly pass <code>NULL</code>."}],"why":"<code>DEFAULT</code> supplies a value whenever the <code>INSERT</code> omits that column.","tag":"07"},{"q":"Which constraint enforces referential integrity between <code>Students.dept_id</code> and <code>Departments.dept_id</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>UNIQUE</code>","why":"Checks uniqueness within one table."},{"h":"<code>FOREIGN KEY</code>","ok":1,"why":"Ensures every <code>Students.dept_id</code> matches an existing <code>Departments.dept_id</code>."},{"h":"<code>CHECK</code>","why":"Checks a row's own values, not another table."},{"h":"<code>DEFAULT</code>","why":"Supplies a fallback value."}],"why":"A <code>FOREIGN KEY</code> prevents orphan rows pointing to a non-existent parent.","tag":"07"},{"q":"Which <code>INSERT</code> syntax adds a row to <code>Departments (dept_name, building)</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>INSERT Departments VALUES ('AI', 'Block E');</code>","why":"Missing <code>INTO</code>."},{"h":"<code>INSERT INTO Departments (dept_name, building) VALUES ('AI', 'Block E');</code>","ok":1,"why":"Correct <code>INSERT INTO ... VALUES (...)</code> syntax."},{"h":"<code>ADD INTO Departments ('AI', 'Block E');</code>","why":"<code>ADD</code> is not a standalone SQL statement."},{"h":"<code>INSERT INTO Departments SET dept_name = 'AI';</code>","why":"<code>SET</code> is <code>UPDATE</code> syntax (or MySQL-only <code>INSERT</code>), not standard SQL/T-SQL."}],"why":"Standard SQL uses <code>INSERT INTO table (cols) VALUES (vals);</code>.","tag":"07"},{"q":"Which command deletes the entire <code>TempLogs</code> table — both its rows AND its column definitions?","opts":[{"h":"<code>DELETE FROM TempLogs;</code>","why":"Deletes rows, keeps the empty table."},{"h":"<code>TRUNCATE TABLE TempLogs;</code>","why":"Deletes all rows, keeps the empty table."},{"h":"<code>DROP TABLE TempLogs;</code>","ok":1,"why":"Removes the table completely from the database."},{"h":"<code>ALTER TABLE TempLogs DROP;</code>","why":"Incomplete/invalid syntax."}],"why":"<code>DROP TABLE</code> destroys both data and structure.","tag":"08"},{"q":"Which statement is part of <strong>DDL</strong> (Data Definition Language)?","opts":[{"h":"<code>SELECT</code>","why":"DML."},{"h":"<code>UPDATE</code>","why":"DML."},{"h":"<code>ALTER TABLE</code>","ok":1,"why":"DDL modifies database structure."},{"h":"<code>GRANT</code>","why":"DCL."}],"why":"DDL = <code>CREATE</code>, <code>ALTER</code>, <code>DROP</code>, <code>TRUNCATE</code>.","tag":"08"},{"q":"What does <code>TRUNCATE TABLE Logs;</code> do in SQL Server that <code>DELETE FROM Logs;</code> does NOT do?","opts":[{"h":"Deletes only rows matching <code>WHERE</code>","why":"<code>TRUNCATE</code> cannot take a <code>WHERE</code> clause."},{"h":"Resets the <code>IDENTITY</code> counter back to its seed value","ok":1,"why":"<code>DELETE</code> keeps the old <code>IDENTITY</code> counter."},{"h":"Deletes the table structure too","why":"<code>TRUNCATE</code> keeps the table structure."},{"h":"Prompts for confirmation","why":"Neither prompts."}],"why":"<code>TRUNCATE TABLE</code> empties all rows fast and resets <code>IDENTITY</code> in SQL Server.","tag":"08"},{"q":"How do you add a <code>phone</code> column to <code>Instructors</code> in SQL Server (T-SQL)?","opts":[{"h":"<code>ALTER TABLE Instructors ADD phone NVARCHAR(20);</code>","ok":1,"why":"In T-SQL, <code>ADD</code> takes column name and type directly."},{"h":"<code>MODIFY TABLE Instructors ADD phone NVARCHAR(20);</code>","why":"<code>MODIFY TABLE</code> is not valid SQL."},{"h":"<code>INSERT INTO Instructors ADD phone NVARCHAR(20);</code>","why":"<code>INSERT</code> adds rows."},{"h":"<code>CREATE COLUMN phone IN Instructors;</code>","why":"Invalid SQL."}],"why":"<code>ALTER TABLE &lt;tbl&gt; ADD &lt;col&gt; &lt;type&gt;;</code> adds a column in T-SQL.","tag":"08"},{"q":"Why might <code>DROP TABLE Departments;</code> fail with a Foreign Key error?","opts":[{"h":"Because <code>Departments</code> is empty","why":"Empty tables drop easily if nothing references them."},{"h":"Because another table (<code>Students</code> or <code>Courses</code>) still has a <code>FOREIGN KEY</code> referencing <code>Departments</code>","ok":1,"why":"Drop or alter child tables first."},{"h":"Because <code>DROP TABLE</code> requires <code>WHERE</code>","why":"<code>DROP TABLE</code> never takes <code>WHERE</code>."},{"h":"Because you must <code>TRUNCATE</code> first","why":"Even an empty parent table cannot be dropped while referenced by an FK."}],"why":"You cannot drop a parent table while active foreign keys point to it.","tag":"08"},{"q":"Which query finds students whose <code>first_name</code> ends with <code>'a'</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>WHERE first_name LIKE 'a%'</code>","why":"Matches names <em>starting</em> with <code>a</code>."},{"h":"<code>WHERE first_name LIKE '%a'</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>%</code> matches any prefix before a final <code>a</code>."},{"h":"<code>WHERE first_name = '%a'</code>","why":"<code>=</code> looks for the literal <code>%</code> character."},{"h":"<code>WHERE first_name LIKE '_a'</code>","why":"Matches only 2-letter names ending in <code>a</code>."}],"why":"In <code>LIKE</code>, <code>%</code> = zero or more characters; <code>_</code> = exactly one character.","tag":"09"},{"q":"What does <code>SELECT DISTINCT city FROM Students;</code> do?","opts":[{"h":"Sorts cities alphabetically","why":"Sorting requires <code>ORDER BY</code>."},{"h":"Lists each city name only once, removing duplicates","ok":1,"why":"<code>DISTINCT</code> deduplicates result rows."},{"h":"Counts how many cities exist","why":"Counting requires <code>COUNT(DISTINCT city)</code>."},{"h":"Deletes duplicate cities from the table","why":"<code>SELECT</code> never modifies stored rows."}],"why":"<code>DISTINCT</code> removes duplicate rows from the query output.","tag":"09"},{"q":"How do you sort <code>Students</code> by <code>gpa</code> from <strong>highest to lowest</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>ORDER BY gpa</code>","why":"Default <code>ORDER BY</code> is ascending (<code>ASC</code>, lowest first)."},{"h":"<code>ORDER BY gpa DESC</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>DESC</code> sorts descending (highest first)."},{"h":"<code>SORT BY gpa DESC</code>","why":"SQL uses <code>ORDER BY</code>, not <code>SORT BY</code>."},{"h":"<code>GROUP BY gpa DESC</code>","why":"<code>GROUP BY</code> groups rows, it doesn't sort them."}],"why":"Use <code>ORDER BY col DESC</code> for descending order.","tag":"09"},{"q":"Why does <code>SELECT * FROM Students WHERE email = NULL;</code> return 0 rows even when some emails are missing?","opts":[{"h":"<code>email</code> is a text column","why":"Type does not matter."},{"h":"In SQL, <code>NULL = NULL</code> evaluates to UNKNOWN (not true); you must use <code>IS NULL</code>","ok":1,"why":"Always use <code>IS NULL</code> / <code>IS NOT NULL</code>."},{"h":"<code>SELECT *</code> cannot read <code>NULL</code>s","why":"<code>SELECT *</code> displays <code>NULL</code>s fine."},{"h":"You need double equals <code>== NULL</code>","why":"SQL uses <code>=</code>, and <code>IS NULL</code> for <code>NULL</code>."}],"why":"Always test for missing values with <code>IS NULL</code> or <code>IS NOT NULL</code>.","tag":"09"},{"q":"Which <code>WHERE</code> condition is equivalent to <code>WHERE credits &gt;= 3 AND credits &lt;= 5</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>WHERE credits IN (3, 5)</code>","why":"Matches only 3 or 5, missing 4."},{"h":"<code>WHERE credits BETWEEN 3 AND 5</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>BETWEEN</code> is inclusive of both endpoints."},{"h":"<code>WHERE credits LIKE '3-5'</code>","why":"<code>LIKE</code> is for text patterns."},{"h":"<code>WHERE credits &gt; 3 AND credits &lt; 5</code>","why":"Excludes 3 and 5."}],"why":"<code>BETWEEN a AND b</code> includes both <code>a</code> and <code>b</code>.","tag":"09"},{"q":"Which aggregate function ignores <code>NULL</code> values when computing a result?","opts":[{"h":"Only <code>COUNT(*)</code>","why":"<code>COUNT(*)</code> counts every row, <em>including</em> <code>NULL</code>s."},{"h":"<code>AVG(grade)</code> (and <code>SUM</code>, <code>MIN</code>, <code>MAX</code>, <code>COUNT(grade)</code>)","ok":1,"why":"All column aggregates ignore <code>NULL</code>s."},{"h":"None of them","why":"Column aggregates ignore <code>NULL</code>s."},{"h":"Only <code>MIN</code>","why":"All five (<code>SUM, AVG, MIN, MAX, COUNT(col)</code>) ignore <code>NULL</code>s."}],"why":"<code>COUNT(*)</code> counts rows; <code>COUNT(col)</code>, <code>SUM</code>, <code>AVG</code>, <code>MIN</code>, <code>MAX</code> skip <code>NULL</code>s.","tag":"10"},{"q":"What is the difference between <code>WHERE</code> and <code>HAVING</code>?","opts":[{"h":"No difference; they are synonyms","why":"They run at different stages."},{"h":"<code>WHERE</code> filters individual rows <em>before</em> <code>GROUP BY</code>; <code>HAVING</code> filters groups <em>after</em> <code>GROUP BY</code>","ok":1,"why":"Aggregates like <code>HAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 2</code> belong in <code>HAVING</code>."},{"h":"<code>HAVING</code> is only for <code>DELETE</code>","why":"<code>HAVING</code> is used in <code>SELECT</code>."},{"h":"<code>WHERE</code> runs after <code>ORDER BY</code>","why":"<code>WHERE</code> runs near the very start, before grouping."}],"why":"Filter rows with <code>WHERE</code>; filter grouped summaries with <code>HAVING</code>.","tag":"10"},{"q":"In what order does SQL logically process a <code>SELECT</code> query?","opts":[{"h":"<code>SELECT</code> → <code>FROM</code> → <code>WHERE</code> → <code>GROUP BY</code>","why":"<code>FROM</code> and <code>WHERE</code> must run before <code>SELECT</code>."},{"h":"<code>FROM</code> → <code>WHERE</code> → <code>GROUP BY</code> → <code>HAVING</code> → <code>SELECT</code> → <code>ORDER BY</code>","ok":1,"why":"Standard logical SQL query processing order."},{"h":"<code>ORDER BY</code> → <code>SELECT</code> → <code>FROM</code> → <code>WHERE</code>","why":"<code>ORDER BY</code> runs last."},{"h":"<code>GROUP BY</code> → <code>WHERE</code> → <code>FROM</code> → <code>SELECT</code>","why":"<code>FROM</code> always comes first."}],"why":"Remember: <code>FROM</code> → <code>WHERE</code> → <code>GROUP BY</code> → <code>HAVING</code> → <code>SELECT</code> → <code>ORDER BY</code>.","tag":"10"},{"q":"If <code>Enrollments</code> has 4 rows with grades <code>[80, 90, NULL, 70]</code>, what does <code>AVG(grade)</code> return?","opts":[{"h":"<code>60</code> (<code>240 / 4</code>)","why":"<code>NULL</code> is NOT treated as <code>0</code>."},{"h":"<code>80</code> (<code>240 / 3</code>)","ok":1,"why":"<code>AVG</code> ignores <code>NULL</code> and divides by the 3 non-NULL grades."},{"h":"<code>NULL</code>","why":"Single-row <code>+</code> with <code>NULL</code> gives <code>NULL</code>, but <code>AVG()</code> skips <code>NULL</code>s."},{"h":"An error","why":"<code>AVG</code> handles <code>NULL</code>s without error."}],"why":"Aggregate functions ignore <code>NULL</code> values (so <code>240 / 3 = 80</code>).","tag":"10"},{"q":"Which query counts how many students live in each <code>city</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>SELECT city, COUNT(*) FROM Students;</code>","why":"Missing <code>GROUP BY city</code>."},{"h":"<code>SELECT city, COUNT(*) FROM Students GROUP BY city;</code>","ok":1,"why":"Groups by <code>city</code> so <code>COUNT(*)</code> counts per city."},{"h":"<code>SELECT city, SUM(*) FROM Students GROUP BY city;</code>","why":"<code>SUM(*)</code> is invalid; use <code>COUNT(*)</code>."},{"h":"<code>SELECT DISTINCT city, COUNT(*) FROM Students;</code>","why":"Missing <code>GROUP BY city</code>."}],"why":"Whenever you mix a regular column (<code>city</code>) with an aggregate (<code>COUNT(*)</code>), put the regular column in <code>GROUP BY</code>.","tag":"10"},{"q":"Which join returns ONLY rows where the join key matches in <strong>both</strong> tables?","opts":[{"h":"<code>INNER JOIN</code>","ok":1,"why":"Keeps only matching pairs from both tables."},{"h":"<code>LEFT JOIN</code>","why":"Keeps all left rows even without a match."},{"h":"<code>RIGHT JOIN</code>","why":"Keeps all right rows even without a match."},{"h":"<code>FULL OUTER JOIN</code>","why":"Keeps all rows from both tables."}],"why":"<code>INNER JOIN</code> returns the intersection where both tables match.","tag":"11"},{"q":"How do you find all <code>Courses</code> that have <strong>zero</strong> rows in <code>Enrollments</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>FROM Courses c INNER JOIN Enrollments e ON c.course_id = e.course_id WHERE e.enrollment_id IS NULL</code>","why":"<code>INNER JOIN</code> already dropped unmatched courses!"},{"h":"<code>FROM Courses c LEFT JOIN Enrollments e ON c.course_id = e.course_id WHERE e.enrollment_id IS NULL</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>LEFT JOIN</code> keeps unmatched courses with <code>e.enrollment_id</code> = <code>NULL</code>."},{"h":"<code>FROM Courses c RIGHT JOIN Enrollments e ON c.course_id = e.course_id</code>","why":"Keeps all enrollments, not unmatched courses."},{"h":"<code>FROM Courses c CROSS JOIN Enrollments e</code>","why":"Creates all pairs."}],"why":"<code>LEFT JOIN ... WHERE right_pk IS NULL</code> is the standard pattern to find unmatched rows.","tag":"11"},{"q":"You run <code>SELECT s.first_name, e.grade FROM Students s LEFT JOIN Enrollments e ON s.student_id = e.student_id WHERE e.grade &gt;= 80;</code>. What happens to students with NO enrollments?","opts":[{"h":"They appear with <code>grade = NULL</code>","why":"The <code>WHERE e.grade &gt;= 80</code> filter removes them!"},{"h":"They are excluded because <code>NULL &gt;= 80</code> is not true","ok":1,"why":"Filtering the right table in <code>WHERE</code> turns a <code>LEFT JOIN</code> into an <code>INNER JOIN</code>."},{"h":"The query fails with an error","why":"It runs, just drops unmatched rows."},{"h":"Their grade becomes <code>80</code>","why":"<code>NULL</code> is never changed to <code>80</code>."}],"why":"Put filters on the optional (right) table inside <code>ON ... AND e.grade &gt;= 80</code> if you want to keep unmatched left rows.","tag":"11"},{"q":"When joining <code>Students</code> and <code>Departments</code> (both have <code>dept_id</code>), why should you write <code>s.dept_id</code>?","opts":[{"h":"It makes the query case-insensitive","why":"SQL keywords are already case-insensitive."},{"h":"Without a table alias/prefix, <code>dept_id</code> is ambiguous because both tables have a column with that name","ok":1,"why":"The DBMS doesn't know which table's <code>dept_id</code> you mean."},{"h":"It deletes duplicate columns","why":"Aliases only disambiguate names."},{"h":"It is only required in SQLite","why":"All SQL engines require disambiguating shared column names."}],"why":"Prefix shared column names with table aliases (<code>s.dept_id</code>, <code>d.dept_id</code>).","tag":"11"},{"q":"What does <code>A RIGHT JOIN B ON ...</code> return?","opts":[{"h":"All rows from <code>A</code>, plus matches from <code>B</code>","why":"That is <code>LEFT JOIN</code>."},{"h":"All rows from <code>B</code>, plus matches (or <code>NULL</code>s) from <code>A</code>","ok":1,"why":"Equivalent to <code>B LEFT JOIN A ON ...</code>."},{"h":"Only rows in <code>A</code> that are not in <code>B</code>","why":"Not a <code>RIGHT JOIN</code>."},{"h":"Twice as many rows as <code>INNER JOIN</code>","why":"Row count depends on matches."}],"why":"<code>A RIGHT JOIN B</code> is identical to <code>B LEFT JOIN A</code>.","tag":"11"},{"q":"What happens if you run <code>DELETE FROM Enrollments;</code> without a <code>WHERE</code> clause?","opts":[{"h":"Nothing is deleted","why":"SQL does not protect you from missing <code>WHERE</code>."},{"h":"Only the last row is deleted","why":"Every row is deleted."},{"h":"Every row in <code>Enrollments</code> is deleted","ok":1,"why":"Without <code>WHERE</code>, <code>DELETE</code> affects all rows."},{"h":"The table structure is also deleted","why":"<code>DROP TABLE</code> deletes the structure; <code>DELETE</code> keeps the empty table."}],"why":"Always double-check <code>WHERE</code> before running <code>DELETE</code> or <code>UPDATE</code>.","tag":"12"},{"q":"Which <code>UPDATE</code> syntax is valid?","opts":[{"h":"<code>UPDATE Students SET gpa = 3.5 AND city = 'Ankara' WHERE student_id = 1;</code>","why":"Use a comma <code>,</code> between assignments, not <code>AND</code>."},{"h":"<code>UPDATE Students SET gpa = 3.5, city = 'Ankara' WHERE student_id = 1;</code>","ok":1,"why":"Comma-separated <code>column = value</code> pairs in <code>SET</code>."},{"h":"<code>UPDATE Students VALUES (3.5, 'Ankara') WHERE student_id = 1;</code>","why":"<code>VALUES</code> is for <code>INSERT</code>."},{"h":"<code>SET Students gpa = 3.5 WHERE student_id = 1;</code>","why":"Must start with <code>UPDATE &lt;table&gt; SET ...</code>."}],"why":"In <code>UPDATE</code>, separate multiple column updates with commas.","tag":"12"},{"q":"Why might <code>DELETE FROM Departments WHERE dept_id = 1;</code> fail?","opts":[{"h":"Because <code>dept_id = 1</code> is a number","why":"Numbers are fine."},{"h":"Because <code>Students</code> or <code>Courses</code> still has rows with <code>dept_id = 1</code> (Foreign Key constraint)","ok":1,"why":"You cannot delete a parent row while children reference it."},{"h":"Because <code>DELETE</code> cannot use <code>=</code>","why":"<code>=</code> is standard in <code>WHERE</code>."},{"h":"Because <code>Departments</code> has a <code>UNIQUE</code> constraint","why":"<code>UNIQUE</code> prevents duplicate inserts, not deletes."}],"why":"Foreign keys protect referential integrity by blocking deletion of referenced parent rows.","tag":"12"},{"q":"Which command adds 2 points to every student's grade in <code>course_id = 1</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>INSERT INTO Enrollments (grade) VALUES (grade + 2) WHERE course_id = 1;</code>","why":"<code>INSERT</code> creates new rows."},{"h":"<code>UPDATE Enrollments SET grade = grade + 2 WHERE course_id = 1;</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>UPDATE</code> modifies existing rows."},{"h":"<code>ALTER TABLE Enrollments SET grade = grade + 2;</code>","why":"<code>ALTER TABLE</code> changes structure."},{"h":"<code>SELECT grade + 2 FROM Enrollments WHERE course_id = 1;</code>","why":"<code>SELECT</code> only reads; it does not save changes."}],"why":"Use <code>UPDATE ... SET col = col + 2 WHERE ...</code> to modify existing rows.","tag":"12"},{"q":"What is the safest way to test a <code>DELETE</code> query before committing it?","opts":[{"h":"Run <code>DROP TABLE</code> first","why":"Never!"},{"h":"Preview the rows with <code>SELECT * ... WHERE ...</code> first, and wrap the <code>DELETE</code> in <code>BEGIN</code> / <code>ROLLBACK</code> or <code>COMMIT</code>","ok":1,"why":"Verify the exact rows before making it permanent."},{"h":"Remove the <code>WHERE</code> clause","why":"That deletes everything."},{"h":"Turn off foreign keys","why":"Disabling constraints risks corrupting data."}],"why":"Always verify your <code>WHERE</code> clause with <code>SELECT</code> first.","tag":"12"},{"q":"Which DCL command gives <code>report_user</code> permission to run <code>SELECT</code> on <code>Students</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>GRANT SELECT ON Students TO report_user;</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>GRANT</code> assigns permissions."},{"h":"<code>REVOKE SELECT ON Students FROM report_user;</code>","why":"<code>REVOKE</code> removes permissions."},{"h":"<code>DENY SELECT ON Students TO report_user;</code>","why":"<code>DENY</code> blocks permissions."},{"h":"<code>GIVE SELECT ON Students TO report_user;</code>","why":"<code>GIVE</code> is not a SQL keyword."}],"why":"DCL permissions use <code>GRANT</code>, <code>REVOKE</code>, and <code>DENY</code>.","tag":"13"},{"q":"A user is a member of <code>db_datareader</code>, which has <code>SELECT</code> on all tables, but you want to block them from reading <code>Instructors</code>. Which command works?","opts":[{"h":"<code>REVOKE SELECT ON Instructors FROM user1;</code>","why":"They still inherit <code>SELECT</code> via <code>db_datareader</code>."},{"h":"<code>DENY SELECT ON Instructors TO user1;</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>DENY</code> explicitly blocks the permission and overrides role <code>GRANT</code>s."},{"h":"<code>REMOVE SELECT ON Instructors FROM user1;</code>","why":"Not valid SQL."},{"h":"<code>ALTER TABLE Instructors HIDE;</code>","why":"Not valid SQL."}],"why":"<code>DENY</code> takes precedence over any <code>GRANT</code> (direct or from a role).","tag":"13"},{"q":"In SQL Server, what is the difference between a <strong>Login</strong> and a <strong>User</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"No difference","why":"One is server-level, one is database-level."},{"h":"A <strong>Login</strong> authenticates to the SQL Server instance; a <strong>User</strong> is mapped to that login inside a specific database","ok":1,"why":"Logins enter the server; Users hold permissions inside a database."},{"h":"A User is only for admins","why":"Any account needs a database User to access objects in that DB."},{"h":"Logins are only for SQLite","why":"SQLite has neither logins nor users."}],"why":"Login = server-level authentication; User = database-level identity.","tag":"13"},{"q":"What does the <strong>principle of least privilege</strong> mean?","opts":[{"h":"Every user gets <code>db_owner</code> so they don't see errors","why":"Violates least privilege completely."},{"h":"Every user or app gets only the minimum permissions needed for its job","ok":1,"why":"Reduces security risk."},{"h":"Passwords must be at least 3 characters","why":"Unrelated (and too short!)."},{"h":"Only 1 user can connect per hour","why":"Least privilege is about <em>what</em> you can do, not connection count."}],"why":"Grant only the permissions genuinely required — never <code>sa</code> or <code>DROP</code> for a read-only app.","tag":"13"},{"q":"Why do DBAs assign permissions to <strong>roles</strong> instead of individual users?","opts":[{"h":"SQL Server forbids granting to users","why":"You <em>can</em> grant to users directly, it just doesn't scale."},{"h":"Roles group permissions once so adding/removing staff only means changing role membership","ok":1,"why":"Easier and less error-prone to maintain."},{"h":"Roles bypass <code>DENY</code>","why":"<code>DENY</code> still overrides role <code>GRANT</code>s."},{"h":"Roles automatically back up the database","why":"Unrelated to backups."}],"why":"Role-based access control keeps permissions clean as people join or leave.","tag":"13"},{"q":"Which operator should you use when a subquery in <code>WHERE</code> can return <strong>multiple</strong> rows?","opts":[{"h":"<code>=</code>","why":"<code>=</code> fails if the subquery returns more than 1 row."},{"h":"<code>IN</code> (or <code>EXISTS</code>)","ok":1,"why":"<code>IN</code> checks membership in a multi-row list."},{"h":"<code>LIKE</code>","why":"<code>LIKE</code> compares a string to a single pattern."},{"h":"<code>BETWEEN</code>","why":"<code>BETWEEN</code> expects two scalar bounds."}],"why":"Use <code>IN</code> or <code>EXISTS</code> for multi-row subqueries; <code>=</code> only works for 1-row, 1-column scalar subqueries.","tag":"14"},{"q":"Which command permanently saves all changes made since <code>BEGIN TRANSACTION</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>ROLLBACK</code>","why":"<code>ROLLBACK</code> undoes the changes."},{"h":"<code>COMMIT</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>COMMIT</code> makes the transaction's changes permanent."},{"h":"<code>SAVE</code>","why":"Not the standard commit command."},{"h":"<code>END</code>","why":"<code>END</code> closes a <code>BEGIN...END</code> code block, not a transaction."}],"why":"<code>BEGIN</code> starts a transaction, <code>COMMIT</code> saves it, <code>ROLLBACK</code> undoes it.","tag":"14"},{"q":"In <strong>ACID</strong>, what does <strong>Durability</strong> mean?","opts":[{"h":"All steps succeed or none do","why":"That is Atomicity."},{"h":"Constraints remain valid","why":"That is Consistency."},{"h":"Concurrent users don't see half-done work","why":"That is Isolation."},{"h":"Once a transaction is committed, its changes survive crashes and power loss","ok":1,"why":"Stored safely on disk/log."}],"why":"A = Atomicity, C = Consistency, I = Isolation, D = Durability.","tag":"14"},{"q":"What is a <strong>correlated subquery</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"A subquery that runs without <code>FROM</code>","why":"Can exist, not what \"correlated\" means."},{"h":"A subquery that references a column from the outer query (e.g. <code>WHERE e.student_id = s.student_id</code>)","ok":1,"why":"Re-evaluated for each outer row."},{"h":"A subquery inside <code>ORDER BY</code>","why":"Correlation is about referencing the outer query."},{"h":"Two <code>SELECT</code>s joined by <code>UNION</code>","why":"<code>UNION</code> is a set operation, not a subquery."}],"why":"In <code>WHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM Enrollments e WHERE e.student_id = s.student_id)</code>, the inner query uses <code>s.student_id</code> from the outer query.","tag":"14"},{"q":"Which ACID property prevents User B from reading User A's uncommitted, half-finished transfer?","opts":[{"h":"Atomicity","why":"Atomicity is all-or-nothing."},{"h":"Consistency","why":"Consistency is rule/constraint validity."},{"h":"Isolation","ok":1,"why":"Isolation keeps concurrent transactions from seeing each other's intermediate states."},{"h":"Durability","why":"Durability is crash survival after <code>COMMIT</code>."}],"why":"Isolation protects concurrent transactions from interfering with each other.","tag":"14"},{"q":"Which backup type copies <strong>only</strong> the data that changed since the last <strong>full</strong> backup?","opts":[{"h":"Full backup","why":"Copies the entire database."},{"h":"Differential backup","ok":1,"why":"Copies all changes since the last full backup."},{"h":"Mirror backup","why":"Not a standard SQL Server backup term."},{"h":"Schema script","why":"Exports <code>CREATE TABLE</code> DDL, not a binary backup."}],"why":"A differential backup captures everything changed since the most recent full backup.","tag":"15"},{"q":"You have a Sunday Full backup and daily Differential backups (Mon, Tue, Wed). To recover on Thursday, what do you restore?","opts":[{"h":"Sunday Full + Mon + Tue + Wed differentials","why":"You don't need Mon or Tue because Wed differential already includes all changes since Sunday."},{"h":"Sunday Full + Wednesday Differential","ok":1,"why":"Restore the last full backup, then the latest differential."},{"h":"Only Wednesday Differential","why":"A differential cannot be restored without its base full backup."},{"h":"Only Sunday Full","why":"You would lose Mon–Wed's data."}],"why":"Differential backups are cumulative since the last full backup: restore Full + latest Differential.","tag":"15"},{"q":"Which file format is best for exporting a query result to open in Excel?","opts":[{"h":"<code>.csv</code> (Comma-Separated Values)","ok":1,"why":"Plain-text table format supported natively by Excel."},{"h":"<code>.bak</code>","why":"SQL Server binary backup format."},{"h":"<code>.ldf</code>","why":"SQL Server transaction log file."},{"h":"<code>.c</code>","why":"C source code file."}],"why":"Export tabular data as <code>.csv</code> for spreadsheets.","tag":"15"},{"q":"Why should backups NOT be stored only on the same hard drive as the live database?","opts":[{"h":"SQL Server forbids writing <code>.bak</code> to the same drive","why":"SQL Server allows it, though it is risky."},{"h":"If that drive fails or is destroyed, you lose both the live database and its backups","ok":1,"why":"Always keep a copy off-drive / off-site."},{"h":"Backups on the same drive cannot be restored","why":"They can, until the drive dies."},{"h":"It doubles the row count in tables","why":"Backups don't alter live tables."}],"why":"Store backups on separate storage / off-site so a single hardware failure cannot wipe both.","tag":"15"},{"q":"What does <strong>Tasks › Generate Scripts…</strong> in SSMS produce?","opts":[{"h":"A compressed binary <code>.bak</code> file","why":"That is <strong>Tasks › Back Up…</strong>."},{"h":"A <code>.sql</code> text file with <code>CREATE TABLE</code> (and optionally <code>INSERT</code>) statements","ok":1,"why":"Useful for recreating the schema on another server."},{"h":"An Excel <code>.xlsx</code> chart","why":"SSMS does not generate Excel charts."},{"h":"A Packet Tracer <code>.pkt</code> file","why":"Packet Tracer is for networking."}],"why":"Generate Scripts creates readable <code>.sql</code> scripts of your database objects (and optionally rows).","tag":"15"}],"exercises":[],"challenges":[{"id":"db-q01","diff":"easy","topic":"INSERT","lesson":"db-07","title":"Add a new department","prompt":"<p>Insert a new department named <code>'Robotics'</code> in <code>'Block E'</code> into <code>Departments</code>.</p>","sql":"INSERT INTO Departments (dept_name, building) VALUES ('Robotics', 'Block E');","check":"SELECT dept_name, building FROM Departments WHERE dept_name = 'Robotics';","hint":"Use <code>INSERT INTO Departments (dept_name, building) VALUES (...)</code>.","ordered":false},{"id":"db-q02","diff":"easy","topic":"INSERT","lesson":"db-07","title":"Add a student relying on defaults","prompt":"<p>Insert a student named <code>'Selim'</code> <code>'Kaya'</code> from <code>'Izmir'</code>, born <code>'2006-03-15'</code>, in <code>dept_id = 1</code>, <code>enrollment_year = 2026</code>, with <code>gpa = 3.20</code> (leave <code>email</code> NULL).</p>","sql":"INSERT INTO Students (first_name, last_name, city, birth_date, dept_id, enrollment_year, gpa)\nVALUES ('Selim', 'Kaya', 'Izmir', '2006-03-15', 1, 2026, 3.20);","check":"SELECT first_name, last_name, city, gpa FROM Students WHERE first_name = 'Selim' AND last_name = 'Kaya';","hint":"List the columns you are providing in <code>INSERT INTO Students (...)</code>, omitting <code>student_id</code> and <code>email</code>.","ordered":false},{"id":"db-q03","diff":"medium","topic":"INSERT","lesson":"db-07","title":"Enroll a student with DEFAULT semester","prompt":"<p>Enroll <code>student_id = 23</code> in <code>course_id = 1</code> with <code>grade = 88</code>, letting <code>semester</code> use its <code>DEFAULT</code> value.</p>","sql":"INSERT INTO Enrollments (student_id, course_id, grade) VALUES (23, 1, 88);","check":"SELECT student_id, course_id, semester, grade FROM Enrollments WHERE student_id = 23 AND course_id = 1;","hint":"Omit <code>semester</code> from the column list so the <code>DEFAULT '2026-Fall'</code> constraint fills it in.","ordered":false},{"id":"db-q04","diff":"easy","topic":"DDL","lesson":"db-08","title":"Create a Projects table","prompt":"<p>Create a table named <code>Projects</code> with columns: <code>project_id</code> (INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT), <code>title</code> (TEXT NOT NULL), and <code>budget</code> (REAL DEFAULT 1000).</p>","sql":"CREATE TABLE Projects (\n    project_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,\n    title TEXT NOT NULL,\n    budget REAL DEFAULT 1000\n);","check":"SELECT name FROM sqlite_master WHERE type = 'table' AND name = 'Projects';","hint":"Use <code>CREATE TABLE Projects (...)</code>.","ordered":false},{"id":"db-q05","diff":"medium","topic":"DDL","lesson":"db-08","title":"Alter and populate a new table","prompt":"<p>Create a table <code>Rooms (room_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, code TEXT NOT NULL)</code>, then add a <code>capacity</code> (<code>INTEGER</code>) column using <code>ALTER TABLE</code>, and insert <code>(1, 'A-101', 40)</code>.</p>","sql":"CREATE TABLE Rooms (room_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, code TEXT NOT NULL);\nALTER TABLE Rooms ADD COLUMN capacity INTEGER;\nINSERT INTO Rooms (room_id, code, capacity) VALUES (1, 'A-101', 40);","check":"SELECT room_id, code, capacity FROM Rooms;","hint":"Remember <code>ALTER TABLE Rooms ADD COLUMN capacity INTEGER;</code>.","ordered":false},{"id":"db-q06","diff":"medium","topic":"DDL","lesson":"db-08","title":"Create a table with a CHECK constraint","prompt":"<p>Create a table <code>Exams (exam_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, max_score INTEGER CHECK (max_score &gt; 0))</code> and insert <code>(1, 100)</code>.</p>","sql":"CREATE TABLE Exams (\n    exam_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,\n    max_score INTEGER CHECK (max_score > 0)\n);\nINSERT INTO Exams (exam_id, max_score) VALUES (1, 100);","check":"SELECT exam_id, max_score FROM Exams;","hint":"Put <code>CHECK (max_score &gt; 0)</code> after the column type.","ordered":false},{"id":"db-q07","diff":"easy","topic":"SELECT","lesson":"db-09","title":"All departments","prompt":"<p>List <code>dept_id</code>, <code>dept_name</code> and <code>building</code> for all departments, ordered by <code>dept_id</code>.</p>","sql":"SELECT dept_id, dept_name, building FROM Departments ORDER BY dept_id;","hint":"Select the three columns and add <code>ORDER BY dept_id</code>.","ordered":true},{"id":"db-q08","diff":"easy","topic":"SELECT","lesson":"db-09","title":"Distinct student cities","prompt":"<p>List each distinct <code>city</code> from <code>Students</code>, sorted alphabetically (<code>city</code>).</p>","sql":"SELECT DISTINCT city FROM Students ORDER BY city;","hint":"Use <code>SELECT DISTINCT city</code>.","ordered":true},{"id":"db-q09","diff":"easy","topic":"SELECT","lesson":"db-09","title":"Students in Istanbul","prompt":"<p>List <code>first_name</code> and <code>last_name</code> of all students whose <code>city</code> is <code>'Istanbul'</code>, ordered by <code>last_name</code>, <code>first_name</code>.</p>","sql":"SELECT first_name, last_name FROM Students WHERE city = 'Istanbul' ORDER BY last_name, first_name;","hint":"Use <code>WHERE city = 'Istanbul'</code>.","ordered":true},{"id":"db-q10","diff":"easy","topic":"SELECT","lesson":"db-09","title":"High-GPA students","prompt":"<p>List <code>first_name</code>, <code>last_name</code> and <code>gpa</code> of students with <code>gpa &gt;= 3.5</code>, ordered from highest GPA to lowest.</p>","sql":"SELECT first_name, last_name, gpa FROM Students WHERE gpa >= 3.5 ORDER BY gpa DESC;","hint":"Use <code>ORDER BY gpa DESC</code>.","ordered":true},{"id":"db-q11","diff":"easy","topic":"SELECT","lesson":"db-09","title":"Top 5 highest-paid instructors","prompt":"<p>List <code>first_name</code>, <code>last_name</code> and <code>salary</code> of the 5 highest-paid instructors, ordered from highest salary to lowest.</p>","sql":"SELECT first_name, last_name, salary FROM Instructors ORDER BY salary DESC LIMIT 5;","hint":"Use <code>ORDER BY salary DESC LIMIT 5</code> (in SQL Server: <code>SELECT TOP 5 ...</code>).","ordered":true},{"id":"db-q12","diff":"easy","topic":"SELECT","lesson":"db-09","title":"Missing student emails","prompt":"<p>List <code>student_id</code>, <code>first_name</code> and <code>last_name</code> of students whose <code>email</code> is missing (<code>NULL</code>), ordered by <code>student_id</code>.</p>","sql":"SELECT student_id, first_name, last_name FROM Students WHERE email IS NULL ORDER BY student_id;","hint":"Never write <code>= NULL</code>; use <code>IS NULL</code>.","ordered":true},{"id":"db-q13","diff":"easy","topic":"SELECT","lesson":"db-09","title":"Students from Ankara or Izmir","prompt":"<p>List <code>first_name</code>, <code>last_name</code> and <code>city</code> of students from <code>'Ankara'</code> or <code>'Izmir'</code>, ordered by <code>city</code>, <code>last_name</code>.</p>","sql":"SELECT first_name, last_name, city FROM Students WHERE city IN ('Ankara', 'Izmir') ORDER BY city, last_name;","hint":"Use <code>city IN ('Ankara', 'Izmir')</code>.","ordered":true},{"id":"db-q14","diff":"easy","topic":"SELECT","lesson":"db-09","title":"Courses with 4 to 6 credits","prompt":"<p>List <code>course_code</code>, <code>title</code> and <code>credits</code> for courses with <code>credits</code> between 4 and 6 (inclusive), ordered by <code>course_code</code>.</p>","sql":"SELECT course_code, title, credits FROM Courses WHERE credits BETWEEN 4 AND 6 ORDER BY course_code;","hint":"Use <code>BETWEEN 4 AND 6</code>.","ordered":true},{"id":"db-q15","diff":"medium","topic":"SELECT","lesson":"db-09","title":"Last names starting with A","prompt":"<p>List <code>first_name</code> and <code>last_name</code> of students whose <code>last_name</code> starts with <code>'A'</code>, ordered by <code>last_name</code>, <code>first_name</code>.</p>","sql":"SELECT first_name, last_name FROM Students WHERE last_name LIKE 'A%' ORDER BY last_name, first_name;","hint":"Use <code>LIKE 'A%'</code>.","ordered":true},{"id":"db-q16","diff":"medium","topic":"SELECT","lesson":"db-09","title":"CMP courses","prompt":"<p>List <code>course_code</code> and <code>title</code> of all courses whose <code>course_code</code> starts with <code>'CMP'</code>, ordered by <code>course_code</code>.</p>","sql":"SELECT course_code, title FROM Courses WHERE course_code LIKE 'CMP%' ORDER BY course_code;","hint":"Use <code>course_code LIKE 'CMP%'</code>.","ordered":true},{"id":"db-q17","diff":"medium","topic":"SELECT","lesson":"db-09","title":"Istanbul 2026 students with GPA >= 3.2","prompt":"<p>List <code>first_name</code>, <code>last_name</code> and <code>gpa</code> of students from <code>'Istanbul'</code> who enrolled in <code>2026</code> and have <code>gpa &gt;= 3.2</code>, ordered by <code>gpa</code> descending.</p>","sql":"SELECT first_name, last_name, gpa\nFROM Students\nWHERE city = 'Istanbul' AND enrollment_year = 2026 AND gpa >= 3.2\nORDER BY gpa DESC;","hint":"Combine all three conditions with <code>AND</code>.","ordered":true},{"id":"db-q18","diff":"medium","topic":"SELECT","lesson":"db-09","title":"Students NOT in Istanbul with known GPA","prompt":"<p>List <code>first_name</code>, <code>last_name</code>, <code>city</code>, <code>gpa</code> for students whose <code>city</code> is not <code>'Istanbul'</code> and whose <code>gpa</code> is not <code>NULL</code>, ordered by <code>student_id</code>.</p>","sql":"SELECT first_name, last_name, city, gpa\nFROM Students\nWHERE city <> 'Istanbul' AND gpa IS NOT NULL\nORDER BY student_id;","hint":"Use <code>city &lt;&gt; 'Istanbul'</code> (or <code>!=</code>) and <code>gpa IS NOT NULL</code>.","ordered":true},{"id":"db-q19","diff":"easy","topic":"Aggregates","lesson":"db-10","title":"Count all students","prompt":"<p>Return one column <code>total_students</code> showing the total number of rows in <code>Students</code>.</p>","sql":"SELECT COUNT(*) AS total_students FROM Students;","hint":"Use <code>COUNT(*) AS total_students</code>.","ordered":false},{"id":"db-q20","diff":"easy","topic":"Aggregates","lesson":"db-10","title":"Average instructor salary","prompt":"<p>Return one column <code>avg_salary</code> showing the average salary of all instructors (rounded to 2 decimals with <code>ROUND(..., 2)</code>).</p>","sql":"SELECT ROUND(AVG(salary), 2) AS avg_salary FROM Instructors;","hint":"Use <code>ROUND(AVG(salary), 2) AS avg_salary</code>.","ordered":false},{"id":"db-q21","diff":"easy","topic":"Aggregates","lesson":"db-10","title":"Min and max grade","prompt":"<p>Return <code>min_grade</code> and <code>max_grade</code> across all graded rows in <code>Enrollments</code>.</p>","sql":"SELECT MIN(grade) AS min_grade, MAX(grade) AS max_grade FROM Enrollments;","hint":"<code>MIN</code> and <code>MAX</code> ignore <code>NULL</code> grades automatically.","ordered":false},{"id":"db-q22","diff":"medium","topic":"GROUP BY","lesson":"db-10","title":"Students per city","prompt":"<p>List <code>city</code> and <code>student_count</code> for every city in <code>Students</code>, ordered by <code>student_count</code> descending, then <code>city</code> ascending.</p>","sql":"SELECT city, COUNT(*) AS student_count\nFROM Students\nGROUP BY city\nORDER BY student_count DESC, city ASC;","hint":"Group by <code>city</code> and count rows.","ordered":true},{"id":"db-q23","diff":"medium","topic":"GROUP BY","lesson":"db-10","title":"Cities with at least 3 students","prompt":"<p>List <code>city</code> and <code>student_count</code> for cities that have 3 or more students, ordered by <code>student_count</code> descending.</p>","sql":"SELECT city, COUNT(*) AS student_count\nFROM Students\nGROUP BY city\nHAVING COUNT(*) >= 3\nORDER BY student_count DESC;","hint":"Use <code>HAVING COUNT(*) &gt;= 3</code> after <code>GROUP BY city</code>.","ordered":true},{"id":"db-q24","diff":"medium","topic":"GROUP BY","lesson":"db-10","title":"Average GPA by enrollment year","prompt":"<p>List <code>enrollment_year</code> and <code>avg_gpa</code> (<code>ROUND(AVG(gpa), 2)</code>) for each enrollment year, ordered by <code>enrollment_year</code>.</p>","sql":"SELECT enrollment_year, ROUND(AVG(gpa), 2) AS avg_gpa\nFROM Students\nGROUP BY enrollment_year\nORDER BY enrollment_year;","hint":"Group by <code>enrollment_year</code>.","ordered":true},{"id":"db-q25","diff":"medium","topic":"GROUP BY","lesson":"db-10","title":"Total credits per department","prompt":"<p>List <code>dept_id</code> and <code>total_credits</code> (<code>SUM(credits)</code>) for each department in <code>Courses</code>, ordered by <code>dept_id</code>.</p>","sql":"SELECT dept_id, SUM(credits) AS total_credits\nFROM Courses\nGROUP BY dept_id\nORDER BY dept_id;","hint":"Sum <code>credits</code> grouped by <code>dept_id</code>.","ordered":true},{"id":"db-q26","diff":"exam","topic":"GROUP BY","lesson":"db-10","title":"Courses with average grade >= 85","prompt":"<p>From <code>Enrollments</code>, list <code>course_id</code> and <code>avg_grade</code> (<code>ROUND(AVG(grade), 2)</code>) for courses whose average grade is at least 85, ordered by <code>avg_grade</code> descending.</p>","sql":"SELECT course_id, ROUND(AVG(grade), 2) AS avg_grade\nFROM Enrollments\nWHERE grade IS NOT NULL\nGROUP BY course_id\nHAVING AVG(grade) >= 85\nORDER BY avg_grade DESC;","hint":"Filter groups with <code>HAVING AVG(grade) &gt;= 85</code>.","ordered":true},{"id":"db-q27","diff":"easy","topic":"JOIN","lesson":"db-11","title":"Students with their department name","prompt":"<p>List <code>s.first_name</code>, <code>s.last_name</code> and <code>d.dept_name</code> for every student, ordered by <code>s.student_id</code>.</p>","sql":"SELECT s.first_name, s.last_name, d.dept_name\nFROM Students s\nJOIN Departments d ON s.dept_id = d.dept_id\nORDER BY s.student_id;","hint":"Join <code>Students</code> and <code>Departments</code> on <code>dept_id</code>.","ordered":true},{"id":"db-q28","diff":"easy","topic":"JOIN","lesson":"db-11","title":"Courses with instructor names","prompt":"<p>List <code>c.course_code</code>, <code>c.title</code>, <code>i.first_name</code> and <code>i.last_name</code> for every course, ordered by <code>c.course_code</code>.</p>","sql":"SELECT c.course_code, c.title, i.first_name, i.last_name\nFROM Courses c\nJOIN Instructors i ON c.instructor_id = i.instructor_id\nORDER BY c.course_code;","hint":"Join <code>Courses</code> and <code>Instructors</code> on <code>instructor_id</code>.","ordered":true},{"id":"db-q29","diff":"medium","topic":"JOIN","lesson":"db-11","title":"All enrollments with student and course","prompt":"<p>List <code>s.first_name</code>, <code>s.last_name</code>, <code>c.course_code</code> and <code>e.grade</code> for all rows in <code>Enrollments</code>, ordered by <code>s.last_name</code>, <code>c.course_code</code>.</p>","sql":"SELECT s.first_name, s.last_name, c.course_code, e.grade\nFROM Enrollments e\nJOIN Students s ON e.student_id = s.student_id\nJOIN Courses c ON e.course_id = c.course_id\nORDER BY s.last_name, c.course_code;","hint":"Chain two <code>JOIN</code>s starting from <code>Enrollments</code>.","ordered":true},{"id":"db-q30","diff":"medium","topic":"JOIN","lesson":"db-11","title":"Students with NO enrollments","prompt":"<p>List <code>s.student_id</code>, <code>s.first_name</code> and <code>s.last_name</code> of students who have zero rows in <code>Enrollments</code>, ordered by <code>s.student_id</code>.</p>","sql":"SELECT s.student_id, s.first_name, s.last_name\nFROM Students s\nLEFT JOIN Enrollments e ON s.student_id = e.student_id\nWHERE e.enrollment_id IS NULL\nORDER BY s.student_id;","hint":"Use <code>LEFT JOIN Enrollments</code> and filter <code>WHERE e.enrollment_id IS NULL</code>.","ordered":true},{"id":"db-q31","diff":"medium","topic":"JOIN","lesson":"db-11","title":"Enrollment count per course (including 0)","prompt":"<p>List <code>c.course_code</code>, <code>c.title</code> and <code>enrolled_count</code> (<code>COUNT(e.enrollment_id)</code>) for <strong>every</strong> course — including courses with 0 enrollments — ordered by <code>enrolled_count</code> descending, <code>c.course_code</code> ascending.</p>","sql":"SELECT c.course_code, c.title, COUNT(e.enrollment_id) AS enrolled_count\nFROM Courses c\nLEFT JOIN Enrollments e ON c.course_id = e.course_id\nGROUP BY c.course_id, c.course_code, c.title\nORDER BY enrolled_count DESC, c.course_code ASC;","hint":"Use <code>LEFT JOIN</code> and <code>COUNT(e.enrollment_id)</code> (not <code>COUNT(*)</code>, which would count 1 for an unmatched course!).","ordered":true},{"id":"db-q32","diff":"exam","topic":"JOIN","lesson":"db-11","title":"Department student counts","prompt":"<p>List <code>d.dept_name</code> and <code>student_count</code> (<code>COUNT(s.student_id)</code>) for all departments, ordered by <code>student_count</code> descending, <code>d.dept_name</code> ascending.</p>","sql":"SELECT d.dept_name, COUNT(s.student_id) AS student_count\nFROM Departments d\nLEFT JOIN Students s ON d.dept_id = s.dept_id\nGROUP BY d.dept_id, d.dept_name\nORDER BY student_count DESC, d.dept_name ASC;","hint":"Use <code>LEFT JOIN Students</code> so departments with 0 students still appear.","ordered":true},{"id":"db-q33","diff":"exam","topic":"JOIN","lesson":"db-11","title":"Highest grade per student","prompt":"<p>List <code>s.first_name</code>, <code>s.last_name</code> and <code>best_grade</code> (<code>MAX(e.grade)</code>) for students who have at least one non-NULL grade, ordered by <code>best_grade</code> descending, <code>s.last_name</code> ascending.</p>","sql":"SELECT s.first_name, s.last_name, MAX(e.grade) AS best_grade\nFROM Students s\nJOIN Enrollments e ON s.student_id = e.student_id\nWHERE e.grade IS NOT NULL\nGROUP BY s.student_id, s.first_name, s.last_name\nORDER BY best_grade DESC, s.last_name ASC;","hint":"Filter <code>e.grade IS NOT NULL</code>, group by student, and take <code>MAX(e.grade)</code>.","ordered":true},{"id":"db-q34","diff":"easy","topic":"UPDATE","lesson":"db-12","title":"Update one student's city","prompt":"<p>Update <code>student_id = 5</code> in <code>Students</code> so their <code>city</code> becomes <code>'Istanbul'</code>.</p>","sql":"UPDATE Students SET city = 'Istanbul' WHERE student_id = 5;","check":"SELECT student_id, first_name, city FROM Students WHERE student_id = 5;","hint":"Always include <code>WHERE student_id = 5</code>!","ordered":false},{"id":"db-q35","diff":"medium","topic":"UPDATE","lesson":"db-12","title":"Raise salaries in Department 1","prompt":"<p>Give a 10% raise (<code>salary = salary * 1.10</code>) to all instructors in <code>dept_id = 1</code>.</p>","sql":"UPDATE Instructors SET salary = salary * 1.10 WHERE dept_id = 1;","check":"SELECT instructor_id, first_name, salary FROM Instructors WHERE dept_id = 1 ORDER BY instructor_id;","hint":"Use <code>SET salary = salary * 1.10 WHERE dept_id = 1</code>.","ordered":true},{"id":"db-q36","diff":"medium","topic":"DELETE","lesson":"db-12","title":"Delete NULL-grade enrollments","prompt":"<p>Delete all rows from <code>Enrollments</code> where <code>grade IS NULL</code>.</p>","sql":"DELETE FROM Enrollments WHERE grade IS NULL;","check":"SELECT COUNT(*) AS remaining_enrollments FROM Enrollments;","hint":"Use <code>DELETE FROM Enrollments WHERE grade IS NULL;</code>.","ordered":false},{"id":"db-q37","diff":"medium","topic":"UPDATE","lesson":"db-12","title":"Update missing GPA","prompt":"<p>Set <code>gpa = 2.00</code> for all students whose <code>gpa IS NULL</code>.</p>","sql":"UPDATE Students SET gpa = 2.00 WHERE gpa IS NULL;","check":"SELECT student_id, first_name, gpa FROM Students WHERE student_id IN (8, 18, 24) ORDER BY student_id;","hint":"Use <code>WHERE gpa IS NULL</code>.","ordered":true},{"id":"db-q38","diff":"medium","topic":"Subquery","lesson":"db-14","title":"Students above average GPA","prompt":"<p>List <code>first_name</code>, <code>last_name</code> and <code>gpa</code> of students whose <code>gpa</code> is strictly greater than the average GPA of all students, ordered by <code>gpa</code> descending.</p>","sql":"SELECT first_name, last_name, gpa\nFROM Students\nWHERE gpa > (SELECT AVG(gpa) FROM Students)\nORDER BY gpa DESC;","hint":"Compare <code>gpa &gt; (SELECT AVG(gpa) FROM Students)</code>.","ordered":true},{"id":"db-q39","diff":"medium","topic":"Subquery","lesson":"db-14","title":"Instructors earning above average","prompt":"<p>List <code>first_name</code>, <code>last_name</code> and <code>salary</code> of instructors who earn more than the average instructor salary, ordered by <code>salary</code> descending.</p>","sql":"SELECT first_name, last_name, salary\nFROM Instructors\nWHERE salary > (SELECT AVG(salary) FROM Instructors)\nORDER BY salary DESC;","hint":"Use a scalar subquery <code>(SELECT AVG(salary) FROM Instructors)</code>.","ordered":true},{"id":"db-q40","diff":"medium","topic":"Subquery","lesson":"db-14","title":"Courses in Block B","prompt":"<p>Using a subquery with <code>IN</code>, list <code>course_code</code> and <code>title</code> of courses belonging to departments in <code>'Block B'</code>, ordered by <code>course_code</code>.</p>","sql":"SELECT course_code, title\nFROM Courses\nWHERE dept_id IN (SELECT dept_id FROM Departments WHERE building = 'Block B')\nORDER BY course_code;","hint":"<code>WHERE dept_id IN (SELECT dept_id FROM Departments WHERE building = 'Block B')</code>.","ordered":true},{"id":"db-q41","diff":"medium","topic":"Subquery","lesson":"db-14","title":"Students enrolled in CMP153 (IN)","prompt":"<p>Using a subquery, list <code>first_name</code> and <code>last_name</code> of students enrolled in <code>'CMP153'</code>, ordered by <code>last_name</code>, <code>first_name</code>.</p>","sql":"SELECT first_name, last_name\nFROM Students\nWHERE student_id IN (\n    SELECT e.student_id\n    FROM Enrollments e\n    JOIN Courses c ON e.course_id = c.course_id\n    WHERE c.course_code = 'CMP153'\n)\nORDER BY last_name, first_name;","hint":"Find <code>student_id</code>s in <code>Enrollments</code> joined with <code>Courses</code> where <code>course_code = 'CMP153'</code>.","ordered":true},{"id":"db-q42","diff":"exam","topic":"Subquery","lesson":"db-14","title":"Courses with no enrollments (NOT EXISTS)","prompt":"<p>Using <code>NOT EXISTS</code>, list <code>course_code</code> and <code>title</code> of courses that have zero enrollments, ordered by <code>course_code</code>.</p>","sql":"SELECT c.course_code, c.title\nFROM Courses c\nWHERE NOT EXISTS (\n    SELECT 1 FROM Enrollments e WHERE e.course_id = c.course_id\n)\nORDER BY c.course_code;","hint":"Correlate <code>e.course_id = c.course_id</code> inside <code>NOT EXISTS (...)</code>.","ordered":true},{"id":"db-q43","diff":"exam","topic":"Subquery","lesson":"db-14","title":"Students with a grade >= 90 (EXISTS)","prompt":"<p>Using <code>EXISTS</code>, list <code>first_name</code> and <code>last_name</code> of students who have at least one grade of 90 or higher, ordered by <code>last_name</code>, <code>first_name</code>.</p>","sql":"SELECT s.first_name, s.last_name\nFROM Students s\nWHERE EXISTS (\n    SELECT 1 FROM Enrollments e\n    WHERE e.student_id = s.student_id AND e.grade >= 90\n)\nORDER BY s.last_name, s.first_name;","hint":"Use <code>WHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM Enrollments e WHERE e.student_id = s.student_id AND e.grade &gt;= 90)</code>.","ordered":true},{"id":"db-q44","diff":"exam","topic":"Subquery","lesson":"db-14","title":"Highest-paid instructor(s)","prompt":"<p>List <code>first_name</code>, <code>last_name</code> and <code>salary</code> of the instructor(s) earning the maximum salary.</p>","sql":"SELECT first_name, last_name, salary\nFROM Instructors\nWHERE salary = (SELECT MAX(salary) FROM Instructors);","hint":"Compare <code>salary = (SELECT MAX(salary) FROM Instructors)</code>.","ordered":false},{"id":"db-q45","diff":"exam","topic":"Subquery","lesson":"db-14","title":"Average of department averages","prompt":"<p>Using a subquery in <code>FROM</code>, return one column <code>avg_dept_salary</code> (<code>ROUND(AVG(dept_avg), 2)</code>) showing the average of each department's average instructor salary.</p>","sql":"SELECT ROUND(AVG(dept_avg), 2) AS avg_dept_salary\nFROM (\n    SELECT dept_id, AVG(salary) AS dept_avg\n    FROM Instructors\n    GROUP BY dept_id\n);","hint":"Group by <code>dept_id</code> in the inner query, then take <code>ROUND(AVG(dept_avg), 2)</code> in the outer query.","ordered":false},{"id":"db-q46","diff":"exam","topic":"JOIN","lesson":"db-11","title":"Students with 2 or more enrollments","prompt":"<p>List <code>s.first_name</code>, <code>s.last_name</code> and <code>course_count</code> (<code>COUNT(*)</code>) for students enrolled in at least 2 courses, ordered by <code>course_count</code> descending, <code>s.last_name</code> ascending.</p>","sql":"SELECT s.first_name, s.last_name, COUNT(*) AS course_count\nFROM Students s\nJOIN Enrollments e ON s.student_id = e.student_id\nGROUP BY s.student_id, s.first_name, s.last_name\nHAVING COUNT(*) >= 2\nORDER BY course_count DESC, s.last_name ASC;","hint":"Join <code>Students</code> and <code>Enrollments</code>, group by student, and use <code>HAVING COUNT(*) &gt;= 2</code>.","ordered":true}],"cheat":{"html":"<section class=\"sec sec-1-sql-command-groups\"><h2 id=\"1-sql-command-groups\">1. SQL command groups</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Commands</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>DDL</strong></td><td>Structure (tables, columns)</td><td><code>CREATE</code>, <code>ALTER</code>, <code>DROP</code>, <code>TRUNCATE</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>DML</strong></td><td>Data (rows)</td><td><code>SELECT</code>, <code>INSERT</code>, <code>UPDATE</code>, <code>DELETE</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>DCL</strong></td><td>Permissions</td><td><code>GRANT</code>, <code>REVOKE</code>, <code>DENY</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>TCL</strong></td><td>Transactions</td><td><code>BEGIN</code> (<code>BEGIN TRAN</code>), <code>COMMIT</code>, <code>ROLLBACK</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-2-select-clause-order-memorize-this\"><h2 id=\"2-select-clause-order-memorize-this\">2. <code>SELECT</code> clause order (memorize this!)</h2>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\"><code>SELECT DISTINCT cols        -- 5. pick columns / expressions\nFROM table1 t1              -- 1. starting table\nJOIN table2 t2 ON ...       -- 2. combine related tables\nWHERE row_condition         -- 3. filter individual rows (no aggregates!)\nGROUP BY group_cols         -- 4. form buckets\nHAVING group_condition      -- 4b. filter buckets (aggregates allowed)\nORDER BY col ASC | DESC     -- 6. sort output\nLIMIT n;                    -- 7. (SQL Server: SELECT TOP n ...)</code></pre>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-3-join-types\"><h2 id=\"3-join-types\">3. JOIN types</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"venn\" data-types=\"inner,left,right\"></div>\n<ul><li><code>INNER JOIN</code>: only matching rows in both tables.</li><li><code>LEFT JOIN</code>: all left rows + matching right rows (<code>NULL</code> if no match).</li><li><code>RIGHT JOIN</code>: all right rows + matching left rows (<code>NULL</code> if no match).</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-4-table-constraints\"><h2 id=\"4-table-constraints\">4. Table constraints</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Constraint</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>PRIMARY KEY</code></td><td>Unique + <code>NOT NULL</code>; identifies each row</td></tr><tr><td><code>FOREIGN KEY</code></td><td>Must match an existing PK in the parent table</td></tr><tr><td><code>NOT NULL</code></td><td>Column cannot be empty (<code>NULL</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>UNIQUE</code></td><td>No duplicate non-NULL values</td></tr><tr><td><code>DEFAULT val</code></td><td>Value used if omitted on <code>INSERT</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>CHECK (cond)</code></td><td>Row must satisfy <code>cond</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-5-normalization-in-one-line-each\"><h2 id=\"5-normalization-in-one-line-each\">5. Normalization in one line each</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>1NF:</strong> Every cell has <strong>one atomic value</strong> (no lists, no repeating columns) and the table has a Primary Key.</li><li><strong>2NF:</strong> In 1NF + <strong>no partial dependencies</strong> (every non-key column depends on the <em>whole</em> PK).</li><li><strong>3NF:</strong> In 2NF + <strong>no transitive dependencies</strong> (no non-key column depends on another non-key column).</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-6-delete-vs-truncate-vs-drop\"><h2 id=\"6-delete-vs-truncate-vs-drop\">6. <code>DELETE</code> vs <code>TRUNCATE</code> vs <code>DROP</code></h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th><code>DELETE FROM T</code></th><th><code>TRUNCATE TABLE T</code></th><th><code>DROP TABLE T</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Group</td><td>DML</td><td>DDL</td><td>DDL</td></tr><tr><td><code>WHERE</code> allowed?</td><td>Yes</td><td>No (all rows)</td><td>No (whole table)</td></tr><tr><td>Keeps structure?</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td><strong>No</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Resets <code>IDENTITY</code>?</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>N/A</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-7-sqlite-this-site-vs-sql-server-t-sql\"><h2 id=\"7-sqlite-this-site-vs-sql-server-t-sql\">7. SQLite (this site) vs SQL Server (T-SQL)</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>SQLite</th><th>SQL Server (T-SQL)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Limit rows</td><td><code>SELECT ... LIMIT 5</code></td><td><code>SELECT TOP 5 ...</code></td></tr><tr><td>Auto-number PK</td><td><code>INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT</code></td><td><code>INT PRIMARY KEY IDENTITY(1,1)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Current date</td><td><code>date('now')</code></td><td><code>GETDATE()</code></td></tr><tr><td>Unicode text</td><td><code>TEXT</code></td><td><code>NVARCHAR(n)</code></td></tr><tr><td>String length</td><td><code>LENGTH(s)</code></td><td><code>LEN(s)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Start transaction</td><td><code>BEGIN;</code></td><td><code>BEGIN TRAN;</code></td></tr><tr><td>Empty all rows</td><td><code>DELETE FROM T;</code></td><td><code>TRUNCATE TABLE T;</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>","title":"Database Management Systems I (SQL) — Cheat Sheet"},"mocks":{"midterm":{"html":"<section class=\"sec sec-question-1-15-pts-data-dbms-and-architectures\"><h2 id=\"question-1-15-pts-data-dbms-and-architectures\">Question 1 (15 pts) — Data, DBMS and architectures</h2>\n<ol><li>Explain the difference between <strong>data</strong> and <strong>information</strong> using your own short example.</li><li>Name <strong>three</strong> problems of flat file systems that a DBMS solves.</li><li>What are the three levels of the <strong>ANSI-SPARC</strong> architecture, and what does each level describe?</li></ol>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show model answer</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><ol><li><strong>Data</strong> is raw, unprocessed facts (e.g. <code>82, 90, 74</code>). <strong>Information</strong> is data given context and meaning (e.g. <em>\"The class average in CMP151 is 82.0\"</em>).</li><li>Any three of: <strong>data redundancy</strong> (duplicate copies), <strong>data inconsistency</strong> (copies disagree), <strong>data isolation</strong>, <strong>lack of concurrency control</strong>, <strong>weak security</strong>.</li><li><strong>External level</strong> (customized user views), <strong>Conceptual level</strong> (the complete logical schema of all tables, types and relationships), <strong>Internal level</strong> (how data is physically stored on disk in files and indexes).</li></ol></div></details>\n<div class=\"widget rubric\" data-widget=\"rubric\"><div class=\"checklist-h\">Question 1 marking checklist</div><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Contrasts raw facts vs processed/meaningful output with an example</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Lists 3 genuine flat-file problems</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Names External, Conceptual and Internal levels accurately</span></label></li></ul><div class=\"rubric-score\"></div></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-question-2-25-pts-er-diagram-design\"><h2 id=\"question-2-25-pts-er-diagram-design\">Question 2 (25 pts) — ER diagram design</h2>\n<p>A university clinic needs a database:</p>\n<ul><li>Doctors have an ID, full name, specialty and phone. Each doctor belongs to one clinic branch (branch ID, name, city).</li><li>Patients have an ID, full name and birth date.</li><li>Patients book appointments with doctors; each appointment records the date/time and a diagnosis note.</li></ul>\n<p>List the <strong>entities</strong>, <strong>attributes</strong>, <strong>Primary Keys (PK)</strong>, <strong>Foreign Keys (FK)</strong> and <strong>cardinalities</strong> (1:1, 1:N, M:N).</p>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show model answer</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><ul><li><strong>Branches</strong> (<code>*branch_id</code> PK, <code>branch_name</code>, <code>city</code>)</li><li><strong>Doctors</strong> (<code>*doctor_id</code> PK, <code>full_name</code>, <code>specialty</code>, <code>phone</code>, <code>branch_id</code> FK → Branches)</li><li><strong>Patients</strong> (<code>*patient_id</code> PK, <code>full_name</code>, <code>birth_date</code>)</li><li><strong>Appointments</strong> (<code>*appt_id</code> PK, <code>patient_id</code> FK → Patients, <code>doctor_id</code> FK → Doctors, <code>appt_datetime</code>, <code>diagnosis</code>)</li><li><strong>Cardinalities:</strong> <code>Branches</code> <strong>1:N</strong> <code>Doctors</code>; <code>Patients</code> <strong>M:N</strong> <code>Doctors</code>, resolved via <code>Appointments</code> (<code>Patients</code> 1:N <code>Appointments</code>, <code>Doctors</code> 1:N <code>Appointments</code>).</li></ul></div></details>\n<div class=\"widget rubric\" data-widget=\"rubric\"><div class=\"checklist-h\">Question 2 marking checklist</div><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Identifies all 4 entities (Branches, Doctors, Patients, Appointments)</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Marks PKs and puts <code>branch_id</code> FK in Doctors (on the N side)</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Resolves the M:N between Patients and Doctors with Appointments</span></label></li></ul><div class=\"rubric-score\"></div></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-question-3-35-pts-normalization-unf-3nf\"><h2 id=\"question-3-35-pts-normalization-unf-3nf\">Question 3 (35 pts) — Normalization (UNF → 3NF)</h2>\n<p>Normalize this table to <strong>1NF</strong>, <strong>2NF</strong>, and <strong>3NF</strong>: <code>ProjectLog(emp_id, emp_name, dept_id, dept_name, projects)</code> where <code>projects</code> holds <code>\"P1: Web (10h), P2: Mobile (5h)\"</code>.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show model answer</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><ul><li><strong>1NF</strong> (atomic cells, composite PK <code>(emp_id, proj_id)</code>): <code>ProjectLog_1NF(&lt;u&gt;emp_id&lt;/u&gt;, &lt;u&gt;proj_id&lt;/u&gt;, proj_name, hours, emp_name, dept_id, dept_name)</code></li><li><strong>2NF</strong> (remove partial dependencies on <code>emp_id</code> and <code>proj_id</code>): - <code>Employees(&lt;u&gt;emp_id&lt;/u&gt;, emp_name, dept_id, dept_name)</code> - <code>Projects(&lt;u&gt;proj_id&lt;/u&gt;, proj_name)</code> - <code>Assignments(&lt;u&gt;emp_id&lt;/u&gt;, &lt;u&gt;proj_id&lt;/u&gt;, hours)</code></li><li><strong>3NF</strong> (remove transitive dependency <code>emp_id → dept_id → dept_name</code> in <code>Employees</code>): - <code>Departments(&lt;u&gt;dept_id&lt;/u&gt;, dept_name)</code> - <code>Employees(&lt;u&gt;emp_id&lt;/u&gt;, emp_name, dept_id)</code> (FK → Departments) - <code>Projects(&lt;u&gt;proj_id&lt;/u&gt;, proj_name)</code> - <code>Assignments(&lt;u&gt;emp_id&lt;/u&gt;, &lt;u&gt;proj_id&lt;/u&gt;, hours)</code></li></ul></div></details>\n<div class=\"widget rubric\" data-widget=\"rubric\"><div class=\"checklist-h\">Question 3 marking checklist</div><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>1NF splits multi-valued <code>projects</code> into rows with composite PK <code>(emp_id, proj_id)</code></span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>2NF separates <code>Employees</code>, <code>Projects</code>, and <code>Assignments</code></span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>3NF moves <code>dept_name</code> to <code>Departments</code> to remove the transitive dependency</span></label></li></ul><div class=\"rubric-score\"></div></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-question-4-25-pts-create-table-with-constraints-t-sql-or-sqlite\"><h2 id=\"question-4-25-pts-create-table-with-constraints-t-sql-or-sqlite\">Question 4 (25 pts) — <code>CREATE TABLE</code> with constraints (T-SQL or SQLite)</h2>\n<p>Write a <code>CREATE TABLE Books (...)</code> statement with:</p>\n<ul><li><code>book_id</code> as an auto-incrementing Primary Key,</li><li><code>isbn</code> as required (<code>NOT NULL</code>) and <code>UNIQUE</code> text,</li><li><code>title</code> as required Unicode text (up to 100 chars in T-SQL),</li><li><code>pages</code> as an integer that must be <code>&gt; 0</code>,</li><li><code>status</code> as text defaulting to <code>'Available'</code>.</li></ul>\n<p>Then write one valid <code>INSERT INTO Books</code> statement.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show model answer</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\"><code>-- SQL Server (T-SQL):\nCREATE TABLE Books (\n    book_id INT PRIMARY KEY IDENTITY(1,1),\n    isbn    VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL UNIQUE,\n    title   NVARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,\n    pages   INT CHECK (pages &gt; 0),\n    status  NVARCHAR(20) DEFAULT 'Available'\n);\n\nINSERT INTO Books (isbn, title, pages)\nVALUES ('978-0-13-110362-7', 'C Programming', 272);</code></pre>\n<p><em>(In SQLite, replace <code>INT PRIMARY KEY IDENTITY(1,1)</code> with <code>INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT</code> and <code>NVARCHAR</code>/<code>VARCHAR</code> with <code>TEXT</code>.)</em></p></div></details>\n<div class=\"widget rubric\" data-widget=\"rubric\"><div class=\"checklist-h\">Question 4 marking checklist</div><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Auto-incrementing <code>PRIMARY KEY</code> on <code>book_id</code></span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span><code>NOT NULL</code> and <code>UNIQUE</code> on <code>isbn</code>, <code>NOT NULL</code> on <code>title</code></span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span><code>CHECK (pages &gt; 0)</code> and <code>DEFAULT 'Available'</code></span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Valid <code>INSERT INTO</code> omitting <code>book_id</code> (and optionally <code>status</code>)</span></label></li></ul><div class=\"rubric-score\"></div></div>\n</section>","title":"DBMS I — Midterm Mock Exam (Weeks 1–6)","minutes":90},"final":{"html":"<section class=\"sec sec-question-1-15-pts-delete-vs-truncate-vs-drop\"><h2 id=\"question-1-15-pts-delete-vs-truncate-vs-drop\">Question 1 (15 pts) — <code>DELETE</code> vs <code>TRUNCATE</code> vs <code>DROP</code></h2>\n<p>Explain the differences between <code>DELETE FROM Students</code>, <code>TRUNCATE TABLE Students</code>, and <code>DROP TABLE Students</code> in terms of: (a) SQL group (DML/DDL), (b) <code>WHERE</code> support, (c) table structure, and (d) <code>IDENTITY</code> counter in SQL Server.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show model answer</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><ul><li><strong><code>DELETE FROM Students</code>:</strong> DML; supports <code>WHERE</code> to remove specific rows; keeps the table structure; does <strong>not</strong> reset <code>IDENTITY</code>.</li><li><strong><code>TRUNCATE TABLE Students</code>:</strong> DDL; cannot use <code>WHERE</code> (removes all rows fast); keeps the empty table structure; <strong>resets</strong> <code>IDENTITY</code> back to seed.</li><li><strong><code>DROP TABLE Students</code>:</strong> DDL; no <code>WHERE</code>; deletes <strong>both</strong> all rows and the table structure itself.</li></ul></div></details>\n<div class=\"widget rubric\" data-widget=\"rubric\"><div class=\"checklist-h\">Question 1 marking checklist</div><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Correctly classifies DML (<code>DELETE</code>) vs DDL (<code>TRUNCATE</code>, <code>DROP</code>)</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Mentions <code>WHERE</code> support, table structure retention, and <code>IDENTITY</code> reset</span></label></li></ul><div class=\"rubric-score\"></div></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-question-2-20-pts-normalization-to-3nf\"><h2 id=\"question-2-20-pts-normalization-to-3nf\">Question 2 (20 pts) — Normalization to 3NF</h2>\n<p>Take <code>Invoice(&lt;u&gt;inv_no&lt;/u&gt;, &lt;u&gt;prod_id&lt;/u&gt;, inv_date, cust_id, cust_city, prod_name, unit_price, qty)</code> (already in 1NF) to <strong>3NF</strong>.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show model answer</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><ul><li><strong>2NF:</strong> - <code>Invoices(&lt;u&gt;inv_no&lt;/u&gt;, inv_date, cust_id, cust_city)</code> - <code>Products(&lt;u&gt;prod_id&lt;/u&gt;, prod_name, unit_price)</code> - <code>InvoiceLines(&lt;u&gt;inv_no&lt;/u&gt;, &lt;u&gt;prod_id&lt;/u&gt;, qty)</code></li><li><strong>3NF:</strong> - <code>Customers(&lt;u&gt;cust_id&lt;/u&gt;, cust_city)</code> - <code>Invoices(&lt;u&gt;inv_no&lt;/u&gt;, inv_date, cust_id)</code> - <code>Products(&lt;u&gt;prod_id&lt;/u&gt;, prod_name, unit_price)</code> - <code>InvoiceLines(&lt;u&gt;inv_no&lt;/u&gt;, &lt;u&gt;prod_id&lt;/u&gt;, qty)</code></li></ul></div></details>\n<div class=\"widget rubric\" data-widget=\"rubric\"><div class=\"checklist-h\">Question 2 marking checklist</div><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Removes partial dependencies on <code>inv_no</code> and <code>prod_id</code> (2NF)</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Removes transitive dependency <code>inv_no → cust_id → cust_city</code> (3NF)</span></label></li></ul><div class=\"rubric-score\"></div></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-question-3-35-pts-writing-sql-queries\"><h2 id=\"question-3-35-pts-writing-sql-queries\">Question 3 (35 pts) — Writing SQL queries</h2>\n<p>Using the university schema (<code>Departments</code>, <code>Instructors</code>, <code>Students</code>, <code>Courses</code>, <code>Enrollments</code>):</p>\n<ol><li>List <code>first_name</code>, <code>last_name</code> and <code>gpa</code> of students in <code>'Istanbul'</code> with <code>gpa &gt;= 3.5</code> or <code>gpa IS NULL</code>, ordered by <code>last_name</code>.</li><li>List each <code>dept_name</code> and the number of students in it (including departments with 0 students).</li><li>List <code>first_name</code>, <code>last_name</code> of students who have <strong>never</strong> enrolled in any course (using <code>LEFT JOIN</code> or <code>NOT EXISTS</code>).</li><li>Using a subquery, list <code>first_name</code>, <code>last_name</code> and <code>salary</code> of instructors who earn more than the average instructor salary.</li></ol>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show model answer</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\"><code>-- 1.\nSELECT first_name, last_name, gpa\nFROM Students\nWHERE city = 'Istanbul' AND (gpa &gt;= 3.5 OR gpa IS NULL)\nORDER BY last_name;\n\n-- 2.\nSELECT d.dept_name, COUNT(s.student_id) AS student_count\nFROM Departments d\nLEFT JOIN Students s ON d.dept_id = s.dept_id\nGROUP BY d.dept_id, d.dept_name\nORDER BY student_count DESC;\n\n-- 3.\nSELECT s.first_name, s.last_name\nFROM Students s\nLEFT JOIN Enrollments e ON s.student_id = e.student_id\nWHERE e.enrollment_id IS NULL;\n\n-- 4.\nSELECT first_name, last_name, salary\nFROM Instructors\nWHERE salary &gt; (SELECT AVG(salary) FROM Instructors)\nORDER BY salary DESC;</code></pre></div></details>\n<div class=\"widget rubric\" data-widget=\"rubric\"><div class=\"checklist-h\">Question 3 marking checklist</div><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Query 1 uses parentheses around <code>OR</code> and uses <code>IS NULL</code> (not <code>= NULL</code>)</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Query 2 uses <code>LEFT JOIN</code> and <code>COUNT(s.student_id)</code> (not <code>COUNT(*)</code>)</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Query 3 correctly finds unmatched students via <code>LEFT JOIN ... IS NULL</code> or <code>NOT EXISTS</code></span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Query 4 compares <code>salary &gt; (SELECT AVG(salary) FROM Instructors)</code></span></label></li></ul><div class=\"rubric-score\"></div></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-question-4-15-pts-dcl-least-privilege\"><h2 id=\"question-4-15-pts-dcl-least-privilege\">Question 4 (15 pts) — DCL &amp; Least Privilege</h2>\n<p>Write T-SQL statements to:</p>\n<ol><li>Give <code>clerk_role</code> permission to <code>SELECT</code> and <code>UPDATE</code> on <code>Students</code>.</li><li>Ensure user <code>intern_ali</code> (who is in <code>clerk_role</code>) can <strong>never</strong> <code>UPDATE</code> <code>Students</code>.</li></ol>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show model answer</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\"><code>GRANT SELECT, UPDATE ON Students TO clerk_role;\nDENY UPDATE ON Students TO intern_ali;</code></pre>\n<p>(<code>DENY</code> overrides the <code>GRANT</code> inherited from <code>clerk_role</code>.)</p></div></details>\n<div class=\"widget rubric\" data-widget=\"rubric\"><div class=\"checklist-h\">Question 4 marking checklist</div><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Uses <code>GRANT SELECT, UPDATE ON Students TO clerk_role</code></span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Uses <code>DENY UPDATE ON Students TO intern_ali</code> (not <code>REVOKE</code>)</span></label></li></ul><div class=\"rubric-score\"></div></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-question-5-15-pts-transactions-acid-backup\"><h2 id=\"question-5-15-pts-transactions-acid-backup\">Question 5 (15 pts) — Transactions (ACID) &amp; Backup</h2>\n<ol><li>Explain what <strong>Atomicity</strong> and <strong>Durability</strong> mean in ACID.</li><li>You have a Sunday Full backup and daily Differential backups (Mon–Thu). The server fails on Friday. Which backups do you restore, and in what order?</li></ol>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show model answer</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><ol><li><strong>Atomicity:</strong> all steps in a transaction succeed together, or all are rolled back on failure. <strong>Durability:</strong> once <code>COMMIT</code> succeeds, changes are permanently saved and survive crashes.</li><li>Restore <strong>Sunday's Full</strong> backup first, then <strong>Thursday's Differential</strong> backup (since differential backups are cumulative since the last full backup).</li></ol></div></details>\n<div class=\"widget rubric\" data-widget=\"rubric\"><div class=\"checklist-h\">Question 5 marking checklist</div><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Defines Atomicity (all-or-nothing) and Durability (survives crashes after COMMIT)</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Restores Sunday Full + Thursday Differential only (in that order)</span></label></li></ul><div class=\"rubric-score\"></div></div>\n</section>","title":"DBMS I — Final Mock Exam (All Topics)","minutes":120}},"extra":{"seed":"PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON;\n\nCREATE TABLE Departments (\n    dept_id     INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,\n    dept_name   TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE,\n    building    TEXT NOT NULL\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE Instructors (\n    instructor_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,\n    first_name    TEXT NOT NULL,\n    last_name     TEXT NOT NULL,\n    email         TEXT UNIQUE,\n    dept_id       INTEGER NOT NULL,\n    hire_date     TEXT NOT NULL,\n    salary        REAL NOT NULL CHECK (salary > 0),\n    FOREIGN KEY (dept_id) REFERENCES Departments(dept_id)\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE Students (\n    student_id      INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,\n    first_name      TEXT NOT NULL,\n    last_name       TEXT NOT NULL,\n    city            TEXT NOT NULL,\n    birth_date      TEXT NOT NULL,\n    email           TEXT UNIQUE,\n    dept_id         INTEGER NOT NULL,\n    enrollment_year INTEGER NOT NULL,\n    gpa             REAL CHECK (gpa IS NULL OR (gpa >= 0.0 AND gpa <= 4.0)),\n    FOREIGN KEY (dept_id) REFERENCES Departments(dept_id)\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE Courses (\n    course_id     INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,\n    course_code   TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE,\n    title         TEXT NOT NULL,\n    credits       INTEGER NOT NULL CHECK (credits > 0),\n    dept_id       INTEGER NOT NULL,\n    instructor_id INTEGER NOT NULL,\n    FOREIGN KEY (dept_id) REFERENCES Departments(dept_id),\n    FOREIGN KEY (instructor_id) REFERENCES Instructors(instructor_id)\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE Enrollments (\n    enrollment_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,\n    student_id    INTEGER NOT NULL,\n    course_id     INTEGER NOT NULL,\n    semester      TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT '2026-Fall',\n    grade         REAL CHECK (grade IS NULL OR (grade >= 0 AND grade <= 100)),\n    UNIQUE (student_id, course_id, semester),\n    FOREIGN KEY (student_id) REFERENCES Students(student_id),\n    FOREIGN KEY (course_id) REFERENCES Courses(course_id)\n);\n\nINSERT INTO Departments (dept_name, building) VALUES\n('Computer Programming', 'Block A'),\n('Software Engineering', 'Block B'),\n('Information Security', 'Block B'),\n('Graphic Design', 'Block C'),\n('Business Administration', 'Block D');\n\nINSERT INTO Instructors (first_name, last_name, email, dept_id, hire_date, salary) VALUES\n('Mehmet', 'Yilmaz', 'm.yilmaz@igu.edu.tr', 1, '2018-09-01', 62000),\n('Ayse', 'Demir', 'a.demir@igu.edu.tr', 1, '2020-02-15', 58000),\n('Kemal', 'Kaya', 'k.kaya@igu.edu.tr', 2, '2016-09-01', 71000),\n('Zeynep', 'Celik', 'z.celik@igu.edu.tr', 3, '2021-09-01', 55000),\n('Can', 'Arslan', 'c.arslan@igu.edu.tr', 1, '2023-02-01', 51000),\n('Elif', 'Sahin', NULL, 4, '2019-09-01', 56000),\n('Murat', 'Ozturk', 'm.ozturk@igu.edu.tr', 5, '2017-03-10', 64000),\n('Selin', 'Aydin', 's.aydin@igu.edu.tr', 2, '2022-09-01', 53000);\n\nINSERT INTO Students (first_name, last_name, city, birth_date, email, dept_id, enrollment_year, gpa) VALUES\n('Deniz', 'Aksoy', 'Istanbul', '2006-04-12', 'deniz.aksoy@stu.igu.edu.tr', 1, 2026, 3.45),\n('Ece', 'Yildiz', 'Istanbul', '2005-11-03', 'ece.yildiz@stu.igu.edu.tr', 1, 2026, 3.80),\n('Kerem', 'Koc', 'Ankara', '2006-01-22', 'kerem.koc@stu.igu.edu.tr', 1, 2026, 2.90),\n('Merve', 'Polat', 'Izmir', '2005-07-19', 'merve.polat@stu.igu.edu.tr', 1, 2025, 3.15),\n('Emre', 'Gunes', 'Bursa', '2006-09-08', NULL, 1, 2026, 2.50),\n('Nisa', 'Kurt', 'Istanbul', '2006-03-30', 'nisa.kurt@stu.igu.edu.tr', 2, 2026, 3.92),\n('Bora', 'Tas', 'Ankara', '2005-12-14', 'bora.tas@stu.igu.edu.tr', 2, 2025, 2.75),\n('Sude', 'Aslan', 'Antalya', '2006-06-25', 'sude.aslan@stu.igu.edu.tr', 1, 2026, NULL),\n('Yusuf', 'Karahan', 'Istanbul', '2005-05-05', 'yusuf.karahan@stu.igu.edu.tr', 3, 2025, 3.10),\n('Defne', 'Ozkan', 'Izmir', '2006-08-17', 'defne.ozkan@stu.igu.edu.tr', 3, 2026, 3.60),\n('Arda', 'Bulut', 'Istanbul', '2006-02-11', NULL, 1, 2026, 2.20),\n('Irem', 'Dogan', 'Trabzon', '2005-10-29', 'irem.dogan@stu.igu.edu.tr', 4, 2025, 3.30),\n('Kaan', 'Erdogan', 'Istanbul', '2006-07-04', 'kaan.erdogan@stu.igu.edu.tr', 1, 2026, 3.05),\n('Lina', 'Mansour', 'Istanbul', '2005-09-18', 'lina.mansour@stu.igu.edu.tr', 1, 2026, 3.70),\n('Omar', 'Farouk', 'Ankara', '2006-11-27', 'omar.farouk@stu.igu.edu.tr', 2, 2026, 3.25),\n('Tarik', 'Sari', 'Bursa', '2005-03-09', 'tarik.sari@stu.igu.edu.tr', 5, 2025, 2.65),\n('Melis', 'Cetin', 'Istanbul', '2006-12-01', 'melis.cetin@stu.igu.edu.tr', 1, 2026, 3.55),\n('Cem', 'Yavuz', 'Izmir', '2005-08-23', NULL, 3, 2025, NULL),\n('Hanna', 'Alver', 'Istanbul', '2006-05-14', 'hanna.alver@stu.igu.edu.tr', 1, 2026, 3.88),\n('Baran', 'Eren', 'Adana', '2006-01-06', 'baran.eren@stu.igu.edu.tr', 4, 2026, 2.80),\n('Zehra', 'Kilic', 'Istanbul', '2005-04-21', 'zehra.kilic@stu.igu.edu.tr', 1, 2025, 3.40),\n('Ozan', 'Tekin', 'Ankara', '2006-10-15', 'ozan.tekin@stu.igu.edu.tr', 2, 2026, 2.95),\n('Asli', 'Duman', 'Istanbul', '2006-06-02', 'asli.duman@stu.igu.edu.tr', 1, 2026, 3.12),\n('Rami', 'Nasser', 'Istanbul', '2005-02-28', 'rami.nasser@stu.igu.edu.tr', 1, 2026, NULL);\n\nINSERT INTO Courses (course_code, title, credits, dept_id, instructor_id) VALUES\n('CMP153', 'Programming Basics I', 6, 1, 1),\n('CMP151', 'Database Management Systems I', 5, 1, 2),\n('CMP155', 'Computer Networks', 5, 1, 5),\n('MTH153', 'Mathematics I', 4, 1, 2),\n('CMP156', 'Integrated Office Applications', 3, 1, 5),\n('SE201', 'Software Design', 6, 2, 3),\n('SEC101', 'Network Security Basics', 5, 3, 4),\n('GD105', 'Digital Typography', 4, 4, 6),\n('BUS101', 'Intro to Business', 3, 5, 7),\n('CMP299', 'Special Topics in C', 3, 1, 1);\n\nINSERT INTO Enrollments (student_id, course_id, semester, grade) VALUES\n(1, 1, '2026-Fall', 85),\n(1, 2, '2026-Fall', 90),\n(1, 3, '2026-Fall', 78),\n(2, 1, '2026-Fall', 95),\n(2, 2, '2026-Fall', 92),\n(2, 4, '2026-Fall', 88),\n(3, 1, '2026-Fall', 70),\n(3, 2, '2026-Fall', 65),\n(4, 1, '2026-Fall', 82),\n(4, 2, '2026-Fall', 79),\n(4, 3, '2026-Fall', 84),\n(5, 1, '2026-Fall', 58),\n(5, 2, '2026-Fall', NULL),\n(6, 6, '2026-Fall', 96),\n(6, 2, '2026-Fall', 94),\n(7, 6, '2026-Fall', 68),\n(8, 1, '2026-Fall', NULL),\n(9, 7, '2026-Fall', 76),\n(9, 3, '2026-Fall', 80),\n(10, 7, '2026-Fall', 89),\n(11, 1, '2026-Fall', 45),\n(11, 2, '2026-Fall', 52),\n(12, 8, '2026-Fall', 83),\n(13, 1, '2026-Fall', 74),\n(13, 2, '2026-Fall', 81),\n(14, 1, '2026-Fall', 91),\n(14, 2, '2026-Fall', 87),\n(15, 6, '2026-Fall', 84),\n(16, 9, '2026-Fall', 72),\n(17, 1, '2026-Fall', 88),\n(17, 2, '2026-Fall', 86),\n(19, 1, '2026-Fall', 93),\n(19, 2, '2026-Fall', 91),\n(20, 8, '2026-Fall', 75),\n(21, 1, '2026-Fall', 85),\n(21, 5, '2026-Fall', 90),\n(22, 6, '2026-Fall', 71);\n-- Note: students 18, 23, 24 have no enrollments; course 10 (CMP299) has no enrollments.\n"},"essentials":{"html":"<p>A database is just <strong>organised tables</strong>, and SQL is the language you use to ask them questions. Seven ideas cover most of this course. The examples run on a small university database right in your browser — press <strong>Run</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout-idea\"><div class=\"callout-h\">A database is tables that are linked</div><p>Data = raw facts (<code>\"Ayşe\", 85</code>). <strong>Information</strong> = data that means something (\"Ayşe passed\"). Keeping data in separate files causes <strong>duplicates</strong> and <strong>contradictions</strong>. A <strong>database</strong> stores it once, in <strong>tables</strong>, and a <strong>DBMS</strong> (like SQL Server or SQLite) manages it safely.</p>\n<p>A table has <strong>columns</strong> (fields like <code>name</code>, <code>city</code>) and <strong>rows</strong> (one record each).</p>\n<p><a href=\"#/lesson/db-01\">Go deeper: data and databases</a></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-idea\"><div class=\"callout-h\">Keys connect the tables</div><ul><li>A <strong>primary key (PK)</strong> uniquely identifies each row: <code>student_id</code>. No two rows share it, and it can't be empty.</li><li>A <strong>foreign key (FK)</strong> is a column that points to another table's PK. <code>Enrollments.student_id</code> → <code>Students.student_id</code>.</li></ul>\n<p>That's how the database \"knows\" which student took which course, without copying the student's name everywhere.</p>\n<p><a href=\"#/lesson/db-07\">Go deeper: keys and constraints</a></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-idea\"><div class=\"callout-h\">ER diagrams are the plan</div><p>Before building tables, you draw an <strong>Entity–Relationship diagram</strong>: <strong>entities</strong> (things: Student, Course) with <strong>attributes</strong> (their columns), joined by <strong>relationships</strong> with a <strong>cardinality</strong>:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>1:1</strong> — one person, one passport</li><li><strong>1:N</strong> — one department, many students</li><li><strong>M:N</strong> — many students, many courses → needs a <strong>junction table</strong> in the middle (<code>Enrollments</code>)</li></ul>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"erd\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">Students: *student_id, first_name, dept_id\nEnrollments: *enrollment_id, student_id, course_id, grade\nCourses: *course_id, title\nStudents 1--N Enrollments\nCourses 1--N Enrollments</pre></div>\n<p><a href=\"#/lesson/db-03\">Go deeper: ER diagrams</a></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-idea\"><div class=\"callout-h\">Normalization = no repeated facts</div><p>If the same fact is written in many rows, sooner or later one copy gets changed and the others don't. <strong>Normalization</strong> splits tables to stop that:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>1NF:</strong> one value per cell (no \"Math, Physics\" in one cell).</li><li><strong>2NF:</strong> every column depends on the <strong>whole</strong> key.</li><li><strong>3NF:</strong> no column depends on another non-key column (e.g. <code>dept_name</code> belongs in <code>Departments</code>, not in <code>Students</code>).</li></ul>\n<p>Short version: <em>\"the key, the whole key, and nothing but the key.\"</em></p>\n<p><a href=\"#/lesson/db-04\">Go deeper: normalization</a></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-idea\"><div class=\"callout-h\">SELECT asks a question</div><div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"sqlrun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">SELECT first_name, last_name, city\nFROM Students\nWHERE city = 'Istanbul'\nORDER BY last_name;</pre></div>\n<p>Read it like English: <em>show these columns, from this table, only rows where…, sorted by…</em>. Useful extras: <code>LIKE 'A%'</code> (starts with A), <code>IN (…)</code>, <code>BETWEEN</code>, <code>IS NULL</code>, <code>DISTINCT</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Counting and grouping:</strong> <code>COUNT</code>, <code>SUM</code>, <code>AVG</code>, <code>MIN</code>, <code>MAX</code>, with <code>GROUP BY</code> to get one result per group and <code>HAVING</code> to filter the groups.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"sqlrun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">SELECT city, COUNT(*) AS students\nFROM Students\nGROUP BY city\nORDER BY students DESC;</pre></div>\n<p><a href=\"#/lesson/db-09\">Go deeper: SELECT</a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/db-10\">GROUP BY</a></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-idea\"><div class=\"callout-h\">JOIN puts tables back together</div><p>Because data is split across tables, you <strong>JOIN</strong> them on the key that links them:</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"sqlrun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">SELECT s.first_name, c.title, e.grade\nFROM Enrollments e\nJOIN Students s ON e.student_id = s.student_id\nJOIN Courses  c ON e.course_id  = c.course_id\nORDER BY e.grade DESC\nLIMIT 5;</pre></div>\n<ul><li><strong>INNER JOIN</strong> (just <code>JOIN</code>): only rows that match in both tables.</li><li><strong>LEFT JOIN</strong>: all rows from the left table, even with no match (missing parts become <code>NULL</code>).</li></ul>\n<p><a href=\"#/lesson/db-11\">Go deeper: JOINs</a></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-idea\"><div class=\"callout-h\">Four families of SQL commands</div><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Family</th><th>Does</th><th>Commands</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>DDL</strong> (definition)</td><td>builds the structure</td><td><code>CREATE</code>, <code>ALTER</code>, <code>DROP</code>, <code>TRUNCATE</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>DML</strong> (manipulation)</td><td>works with the rows</td><td><code>SELECT</code>, <code>INSERT</code>, <code>UPDATE</code>, <code>DELETE</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>DCL</strong> (control)</td><td>permissions</td><td><code>GRANT</code>, <code>REVOKE</code>, <code>DENY</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>TCL</strong> (transactions)</td><td>all-or-nothing changes</td><td><code>BEGIN</code>, <code>COMMIT</code>, <code>ROLLBACK</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The most dangerous line in SQL: <code>DELETE FROM Students;</code> — <strong>without <code>WHERE</code> it deletes every row.</strong> Always write the <code>WHERE</code> first.</p>\n<p><code>DELETE</code> removes rows (can use WHERE) · <code>TRUNCATE</code> empties the whole table fast · <code>DROP</code> deletes the table itself.</p>\n<p><a href=\"#/lesson/db-08\">Go deeper: DDL</a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/db-12\">INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE</a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/db-14\">transactions</a></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-remember\"><div class=\"callout-h\">If you remember only this</div><ul><li>Tables have rows and columns; <strong>PK</strong> identifies a row, <strong>FK</strong> links to another table.</li><li>ER diagram first; M:N needs a junction table.</li><li>Normalize so each fact is stored once (1NF → 2NF → 3NF).</li><li>Clause order: <code>SELECT → FROM → JOIN → WHERE → GROUP BY → HAVING → ORDER BY</code>.</li><li><code>UPDATE</code> and <code>DELETE</code> without <code>WHERE</code> change <strong>every</strong> row.</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-tsql\"><div class=\"callout-h\">In SQL Server (T-SQL)</div><p>The university uses Microsoft SQL Server. It is almost the same; the main difference you'll notice is <code>SELECT TOP 5 …</code> instead of <code>… LIMIT 5</code>.</p></div>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"Which column is the best primary key for a Students table?","opts":[{"h":"first_name","why":"two students can have the same name"},{"h":"city","why":"many students share a city"},{"h":"student_id","ok":1,"why":"unique for every student"},{"h":"birth_date","why":"not unique"}],"why":"A PK must be unique and never empty."},{"q":"Students and Courses have a many-to-many relationship. What do you need?","opts":[{"h":"one bigger table","why":"that creates repeated data"},{"h":"a junction table such as Enrollments","ok":1,"why":"it holds pairs of student_id and course_id"},{"h":"nothing special","why":"M:N can't be stored directly"},{"h":"a second database","why":"no"}],"why":"M:N is resolved with a junction table."},{"q":"Which clause filters rows <strong>before</strong> grouping?","opts":[{"h":"WHERE","ok":1,"why":"WHERE filters rows; HAVING filters groups"},{"h":"HAVING","why":"that filters groups after GROUP BY"},{"h":"ORDER BY","why":"that sorts"},{"h":"SELECT","why":"that chooses columns"}],"why":"WHERE → rows, HAVING → groups."},{"q":"What does <code>DELETE FROM Courses;</code> do?","opts":[{"h":"deletes the Courses table","why":"that's DROP"},{"h":"deletes every row in Courses","ok":1,"why":"there is no WHERE"},{"h":"deletes one row","why":"no condition was given"},{"h":"nothing","why":"it runs"}],"why":"Always check the WHERE before UPDATE/DELETE."},{"q":"An INNER JOIN between Students and Enrollments returns:","opts":[{"h":"all students, even those with no enrollments","why":"that's LEFT JOIN"},{"h":"only students that have at least one matching enrollment","ok":1,"why":"only matching rows"},{"h":"only enrollments with no student","why":"no"},{"h":"every possible pair of rows","why":"that's a CROSS JOIN"}],"why":"INNER = matches in both tables."}]],"title":"Databases and SQL — the essentials","minutes":25,"intro":"tables and keys, ER diagrams, normalization, SELECT, JOIN, changing data"}});
